/* Índice del buscador en inglés (generado por _src-publicaciones/ingles/indice_en.py, no editar a mano). */
window.CATTO_INDICE = (window.CATTO_INDICE || []).concat([{"t":"Markets, products and processes","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#mercado","x":"02 Markets, products and processes What and how much is produced is decided by the market: the analysis of demand, competition and prices defines the volume and the variety. Each product goes through a life cycle —introduction, growth, maturity and decline— that changes what matters at each stage: flexibility at the beginning, cost and efficiency at maturity. Depending on volume and variety, the process is organized in different ways: Process type Volume and variety Example in electronics Project A single, unique unit A radar station Job shop (by functions) Low volume, high variety Prototypes and made-to-order equipment Batch Medium volume Laboratory instruments Line High volume, low variety Assembly of phones or televisions Continuous Homogeneous product without interruption Manufacture of wafers or cables"},{"t":"Balancing a line","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#balanceo","x":"03 Balancing a line On a line, the product passes through successive stations and all of them have the same time to work: the cycle time, which is set by demand. Balancing the line means distributing the tasks among the stations while respecting the precedence order, without any station exceeding the cycle and using the smallest possible number of stations: \\[ c = \\frac{\\text{available time}}{\\text{demand}} \\] \\[ N_{min} = \\left\\lceil \\frac{\\sum t_i}{c} \\right\\rceil, \\quad \\eta = \\frac{\\sum t_i}{N\\,c} \\] The exact problem is combinatorial; in practice heuristics are used. The largest task time rule assigns to each station, among the available tasks that fit in the remaining time, the longest one. It does not always reach the theoretical minimum. Lab · balancing an assembly line Ten tasks to assemble a unit, in seconds: A inspect the board (15), B insert large components (45, after A), C solder connectors (30, after A), D touch up solder joints (25, after B), E mount the heat sink (20, after B), F electrical test (35, after C and D), G mount in the cabinet (30, after E and F), H wire the front panel (25, after G), I program (20, after F) and J final test and packing (30, after H and I). A 450-minute shift. Each bar is a station; the red line, the cycle time. Demand per shift ="},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Flow process chart Make the flow process chart for the assembly of a power supply in the laboratory and propose a better method by eliminating transports and delays. Activity 2 · Standard time Time a soldering task ten times, rate the pace and calculate the standard time with allowances. Activity 3 · Manual balancing Balance the laboratory line by hand for 540 units and explain why it cannot be brought down to six stations, even though the theoretical minimum suggests it."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Measuring without having improved the method. An inefficient way of working gets standardized. Forgetting the allowances. A standard time without rest is impossible to sustain. Setting a cycle shorter than the longest task. The task has to be split or the station duplicated. Believing the heuristic gives the optimum. It is a good starting point, not a guarantee."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What cycle time corresponds to 300 units in a 450-minute shift? 90 s. How many stations are needed at minimum, if the tasks add up to 275 s? \\( \\lceil 275/90 \\rceil = 4 \\). What efficiency does the line have with those four stations? \\( 275/(4 \\cdot 90) \\approx 76 \\) %. What process is suitable for producing varied prototypes? A job shop organized by functions."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading International Labour Organization (George Kanawaty, ed.). Introduction to Work Study. 4th ed., ILO, 1992. (Spanish edition: “Introducción al estudio del trabajo”, 4th ed., ILO, 1996.) Methods study, flow process charts and work measurement. Benjamin W. Niebel and Andris Freivalds. Methods, Standards, and Work Design. McGraw-Hill. (Spanish edition: “Ingeniería industrial: métodos, estándares y diseño del trabajo”.) Methods and times, with many worked cases. Jay Heizer and Barry Render. Principles of Operations Management. Pearson. (Spanish edition: “Principios de administración de operaciones”.) Process types and line balancing."},{"t":"The superheterodyne spectrum analyzer","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#super","x":"01 The superheterodyne spectrum analyzer The oscilloscope shows the signal in time; the spectrum analyzer, its power as a function of frequency. The classic analyzer is a self-tuning superheterodyne receiver: a local oscillator sweeps a range of frequencies, a mixer translates each input component to a fixed intermediate frequency, and there a narrow filter, the resolution bandwidth (RBW) filter, lets through only what falls within it. A detector measures the power, and the screen plots it as a function of the local oscillator frequency. Three controls govern the measurement: the span of frequencies swept, the RBW and the video bandwidth (VBW), a filter after the detector that smooths the trace. The presentation of spectra as an analysis tool was covered in spectra, in Signals and Systems."},{"t":"RBW: resolution, noise and time","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#rbw","x":"02 RBW: resolution, noise and time The RBW decides three things at once. Two signals are seen as separate only if their spacing is of the order of the RBW or greater. The noise entering the detector is proportional to the bandwidth, so the displayed noise floor drops 10 dB for each decade of RBW. And the filter needs time to respond, so the sweep cannot be faster than: \\[ t_{sweep} \\approx k\\, \\frac{\\mathrm{span}}{\\mathrm{RBW}^2} \\] \\[ P_{floor} = DANL_{1\\,Hz} + 10 \\log \\mathrm{RBW} \\] \\( k \\) is between 2 and 3 for analog Gaussian filters. Lowering the RBW by a factor of ten lowers the floor by 10 dB but makes the sweep a hundred times slower. \\( DANL \\) is the displayed average noise level, which is specified normalized to 1 Hz. When the two signals have very different levels, the resolution also depends on the filter’s selectivity: the skirt of the strong signal’s response can hide the weak one even if it is farther away than the RBW. Lab · two tones and the resolution filter Two close tones, the first at 0 dBm, seen with a Gaussian resolution filter, over a span of 200 kHz. The analyzer has a noise level of −150 dBm/Hz. The tones are considered resolved if between them the trace drops at least 3 dB relative to the weaker one. RBW = 1 kHz RBW = 3 kHz RBW = 10 kHz RBW = 30 kHz Spacing between tones = Level of the second tone ="},{"t":"FFT and real-time analyzers","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#otros","x":"03 FFT and real-time analyzers At low frequencies, and in modern analyzers also at the intermediate frequency, the spectrum is calculated by digitizing the signal and applying an FFT. With narrow RBWs it is much faster than sweeping, because it processes all the frequencies at once. Real-time analyzers compute overlapping FFTs with no dead time and can display signals that appear for microseconds, such as a Bluetooth frequency hop, which a sweep would miss. The same instruments measure phase noise, channel power, adjacent-channel leakage ratio and digital modulation quality."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Resolving two tones Combine two generators with a 5 kHz difference and lower the RBW until they are separated. Repeat with one of them 40 dB lower. Activity 2 · Noise floor With the input terminated in 50 Ω, measure the noise floor with several RBWs and verify the slope of 10 dB per decade. Activity 3 · Distortion of the analyzer itself Measure the harmonics of a generator with different input attenuations: if the level of the harmonic changes when the attenuation is changed, the analyzer is generating it."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Using a large RBW to see a weak signal. The noise floor hides it. Sweeping faster than the filter allows. Amplitudes come out low and frequencies shifted; instruments warn with an uncalibrated alarm. Overloading the input mixer. Distortion products appear that are not in the signal. Confusing channel power with peak level. For modulated signals, it is measured by integrating over the bandwidth."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment By how much does the noise floor drop when going from 100 kHz to 1 kHz of RBW? 20 dB. How long does a 1 MHz sweep take with a 1 kHz RBW and \\( k = 2.5 \\)? \\( 2.5 \\cdot 10^6/10^6 = 2.5 \\) s. What noise level does a −150 dBm/Hz analyzer show with a 10 kHz RBW? −110 dBm. Why does a real-time analyzer see signals that a sweep misses? Because it processes all the frequencies continuously, with no dead time; the sweep looks at each frequency only for an instant."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Keysight Technologies. Spectrum Analysis Basics. Application Note 150, Keysight. The classic reference on the superheterodyne analyzer, the RBW and the sweep time. Christoph Rauscher, Volker Janssen and Roland Minihold. Fundamentals of Spectrum Analysis. Rohde & Schwarz, 2001. A complete book on swept and FFT analyzers, with their sources of error. Clyde F. Coombs Jr. (ed.). Electronic Instrument Handbook. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1999. Spectrum and signal analyzers within the whole of instrumentation."},{"t":"What defines an antenna","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#parametros","x":"01 What defines an antenna An antenna is the transducer between a transmission line and space. The radiation unit covered radiation resistance, the pattern and directivity; to design a link you also need: Parameter What it tells you Gain \\( G = e \\cdot D \\) The directivity after subtracting the losses of the antenna; given in dBi Beamwidth The angle between the directions where the power falls to half (−3 dB) Impedance and bandwidth The range of frequencies where the SWR is acceptable, typically less than 1.5 or 2 Polarization The orientation of the electric field; the two antennas of the link have to match Front-to-back ratio How much less it radiates backward than forward An antenna works the same for transmitting as for receiving: by reciprocity, the pattern and the gain are the same. In reception, the gain translates into an effective area: \\[ A_e = \\frac{\\lambda^2}{4\\pi}\\, G \\] The power delivered by the antenna is the incoming power density multiplied by its effective area. A half-wave dipole at 146 MHz has 0.55 m²; the same dipole at 2.4 GHz, 270 times less."},{"t":"Antennas in common use","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#tipos","x":"02 Antennas in common use Antenna Typical gain Use \\( \\lambda /4 \\) monopole over a ground plane 5.2 dBi, 36 Ω AM broadcasting (the tower is the monopole), mobile units, VHF radios Half-wave dipole 2.15 dBi, 73 Ω Reference; element of larger antennas Yagi-Uda 8 to 15 dBi Television, VHF and UHF links Patch on a board 6 to 9 dBi GPS, Wi-Fi, cellular telephony arrays Horn 10 to 25 dBi Feed for parabolic dishes, measurement standard Parabolic dish of diameter \\( D \\) \\( e\\,(\\pi D/\\lambda)^2 \\), 30 to 45 dBi Microwave and satellite links The monopole takes advantage of the image formed by the ground plane: it behaves as half a dipole, with half the resistance and twice the directivity, because it radiates only over the upper half-space. The parabolic dish concentrates the energy in a beam of approximate width \\( 70°\\, \\lambda / D \\): a 0.6 m dish at 11 GHz has 34 dBi and a 3° beam, and it has to be aimed with that precision."},{"t":"The Friis equation","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#friis","x":"03 The Friis equation Between two antennas in free space, separated by a distance \\( d \\) much greater than their size, the received power is: \\[ P_r = P_t\\, G_t\\, G_r \\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{4\\pi d}\\right)^2 \\] \\[ L_{fs} = 32.45 + 20\\log f_{MHz} + 20\\log d_{km} \\] The factor in parentheses is the free-space loss, which the second expression gives in decibels. It is not an absorption: it is the power spreading over an ever larger sphere, seen through the effective area of an isotropic antenna. In decibels, the link is built by adding and subtracting, just like the budget of a line or that of a fiber: transmitter power, minus cables, plus gains, minus path loss. What arrives is compared with the receiver threshold, which starts from thermal noise, −174 dBm per hertz at room temperature, and adds the bandwidth, the noise figure and the signal-to-noise ratio that the modulation requires. The difference is the fade margin, the reserve for rain and for changes in propagation."},{"t":"Line of sight and Fresnel zones","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#fresnel","x":"04 Line of sight and Fresnel zones A microwave link needs more than just seeing the other antenna: the energy travels through a volume around the direct ray. The first Fresnel zone is the ellipsoid where the indirect path is up to half a wavelength longer than the direct one. Its radius at a point \\( d_1 \\) and \\( d_2 \\) from the ends is: \\[ r_1 = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\lambda\\, d_1 d_2}{d_1 + d_2}} \\] The design criterion is to keep at least 60% of the first zone free of obstacles. Over 30 km at 11 GHz, the radius at the center is 14 m. The most common obstacle on a long link is the Earth itself. The refraction of the atmosphere bends the rays downward, and the effect is modeled with an effective Earth radius of 4/3 the real one. Even so, at the center of a 30 km link the ground rises 13 m above the straight line joining the bases of the towers: the antennas have to be about 22 m high to clear 60% of the zone over flat terrain. Lab · a point-to-point microwave link Two identical dishes, at the same height over flat terrain. The graph shows the profile (the vertical scale is greatly exaggerated): the bulge of the Earth with an effective radius of 4/3, the line of sight and the first Fresnel zone, with the 60% marked. A 23 dBm radio, 2 dB of cable loss and a receiver with 28 MHz of bandwidth and a 6 dB noise figure that needs a 17 dB signal-to-noise ratio. 2.4 GHz 5.8 GHz 11 GHz 18 GHz 0.3 m antennas 0.6 m antennas 1.2 m antennas Distance = Antenna height ="},{"t":"Other propagation mechanisms","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#propagacion","x":"05 Other propagation mechanisms Mechanism Frequencies What takes advantage of it Ground wave Long and medium wave AM radio by day, hundreds of kilometers over moist ground or sea Ionospheric reflection 3 to 30 MHz Shortwave with worldwide reach, varying with the time of day and the solar cycle Line of sight VHF and above Television, cellular telephony, microwave links Multipath UHF and microwaves in cities Reflections off buildings; OFDM and MIMO turn them into an advantage Rain attenuation Above about 10 GHz Sets the margin needed on links from 18 to 80 GHz"},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#electronica","x":"06 Where it appears in electronics System What decides the link Radio link between base stations 0.3 to 1.2 m dishes at 6 to 38 GHz, with margin for rain Home Wi-Fi Antennas of a few dBi, walls and multipath more than distance Satellite television 60 cm dish at 36,000 km, with a low-noise amplifier at the focus Field telemetry A Yagi at each end to gain range with low-power transmitters Antenna installation and alignment are covered in antennas and in microwave links, in the technician program."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring the gain With two identical antennas facing each other at a known distance in the far field, measure the attenuation between them with a generator and an analyzer. Solve for the gain of each from the Friis equation. Activity 2 · The pattern of a Yagi Rotate a UHF Yagi on an axis in 10° steps facing a fixed transmitter and plot the pattern. Measure the beamwidth and the front-to-back ratio. Activity 3 · Designing a real link With a terrain profile obtained from an elevation map, design a 5.8 GHz link between two points in the city: tower height, antennas and margin."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Believing that free-space loss depends on frequency by itself. It grows with frequency because the effective area of an antenna of fixed gain shrinks; with antennas of fixed area, the link improves as the frequency goes up. Designing with line of sight but without clearing the Fresnel zone. The link works in the test and drops out with changes in refraction. Forgetting polarization. Two crossed antennas lose 20 dB or more. Leaving no margin. An 18 GHz link with no margin drops out in the first storm."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the free-space loss over 30 km at 11 GHz? \\( 32.45 + 20\\log 11\\,000 + 20\\log 30 \\approx 142.8 \\) dB. What gain does a 0.6 m parabolic dish have at 11 GHz with 55% efficiency? \\( 0.55\\,(\\pi \\cdot 0.6 / 0.0273)^2 \\approx 2630 \\), that is, 34.2 dBi. How much does the received power change if the distance is doubled? It drops 6 dB, to a quarter. Why does a \\( \\lambda /4 \\) monopole have 36 Ω and not 73 Ω? Because with its image in the ground plane it forms a half-wave dipole, but only the upper half radiates: the same current delivers half the power."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Constantine A. Balanis. Antenna Theory: Analysis and Design. 4th ed., Wiley, 2016. The types of antenna one by one, arrays, and the Friis equation with all its assumptions. Warren L. Stutzman and Gary A. Thiele. Antenna Theory and Design. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2012. More design-oriented, with good chapters on aperture antennas and parabolic dishes. Roger L. Freeman. Radio System Design for Telecommunications. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2007. The design of complete radio links: profiles, Fresnel zones, rain and availability."},{"t":"What a preliminary design contains","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#contenido","x":"01 What a preliminary design contains The preliminary design is the document that shows, before building, that the proposed solution will meet the specification. It is not the detailed design —the complete schematics and the board come later—, but the architecture decisions justified with calculations. A typical preliminary design includes: Part What it says Descriptive report The problem, the user, the scope and what is left out Specification The quantitative and verifiable requirements Block diagram The functions, the interfaces between them and the signals that connect them Selection of main components The microcontroller, the sensor, the radio, the power supply, with the discarded alternatives and why Sizing budgets Energy, measurement error, radio link, dissipation, response times Test plan How each point of the specification will be verified Preliminary costs and schedule The priced bill of materials and the stages"},{"t":"The sizing budgets","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#presupuestos","x":"02 The sizing budgets A budget distributes a limited resource among the blocks and verifies that it is enough with a margin. The error budget adds up the uncertainties of the sensor, signal conditioning and converter, as seen in Measurements; the link budget adds up powers, gains and losses down to the receiver sensitivity; the thermal budget verifies junction temperatures with the dissipation of each component, and feeds the reliability estimate. In a battery-powered device, the most important is the energy budget: with active and sleep duty cycles, the average current is \\[ \\bar I = \\frac{I_a\\,t_a + I_s\\,(T - t_a)}{T} \\] \\[ \\text{battery life} = \\frac{\\eta\\, C}{\\bar I} \\] \\( I_a \\) is the active current during \\( t_a \\), \\( I_s \\) the sleep current, \\( T \\) the period between wake-ups, \\( C \\) the battery capacity and \\( \\eta \\) the usable fraction, taken here as 0.8 to cover the cutoff voltage, temperature and aging. Lab · energy budget of a sensor node A node that wakes up every T seconds, measures and transmits for t a at current I a, and sleeps the rest of the time at I s. The curve is the battery life in years as a function of the period, on a logarithmic scale; the yellow dot is the chosen design. Sleep current I s = Active current I a = Active time t a = Period T = Battery Two alkaline AA cells (2500 mAh) CR2032 coin cell (225 mAh) 18650 lithium cell (3000 mAh)"},{"t":"The test plan","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#ensayos","x":"03 The test plan Each point of the specification needs a test that says how it will be measured, with what instrument, under what conditions and what the acceptance criterion is. Writing it into the preliminary design forces the specification to be verifiable and anticipates the instruments that will be needed. The degree’s laboratory tests —from Electronic Measurements I and II— are the basis; those for regulatory compliance, if the product requires them, are usually done in an external laboratory and should be budgeted. The manufacturing technology was defined in construction technology."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Block diagram Draw the block diagram of the final project with the interfaces, the supply voltages and the signals between blocks. Activity 2 · Measuring consumption With a sense resistor and the oscilloscope, measure the current profile of a microcontroller that sleeps and wakes up, and compare it with the budget. Activity 3 · Verification matrix Build a table with each specification, the test that verifies it and the instrument required."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Taking the sleep current from the microcontroller datasheet alone. Regulators, dividers, sensors and pull-ups also consume. Using the full nominal capacity of the battery. At low temperature or with current peaks considerably less is usable. Promising battery lives of decades. Battery self-discharge sets a limit of a few years to a decade. Leaving the test plan for the end. If a specification cannot be tested, it has to be rewritten now."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What average current does the lab node have with its initial values? About 38.3 μA. What battery life does that give with two AA cells? Almost 6 years: \\( 0.8 \\cdot 2500 / 0.0383 \\approx 52\\,000 \\) hours. And with a CR2032 coin cell? A little over half a year. What is the difference between a preliminary design and a detailed design? The preliminary design fixes and justifies the architecture with calculations; the detailed design produces the complete schematics, board and software."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Karl T. Ulrich and Steven D. Eppinger. Product Design and Development. McGraw-Hill. (Spanish edition: “Diseño y desarrollo de productos”.) Product architecture and system-level design. Elecia White. Making Embedded Systems. 2nd ed., O'Reilly, 2024. Embedded systems architecture and power reduction. Paul Horowitz and Winfield Hill. The Art of Electronics. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2015. Low-power circuit design and component selection."},{"t":"Increase, decrease and extrema","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#crecimiento","x":"01 Increase, decrease and extrema The sign of the derivative tells you where the function is heading, and the points where it vanishes are the candidates for maximum or minimum: If on an interval… then f… \\( f'(x) > 0 \\) increases \\( f'(x) < 0 \\) decreases \\( f'(c) = 0 \\) or does not exist has a critical point at c: it has to be analyzed The first-derivative test settles each critical point by looking at how the sign of \\( f' \\) changes as it passes through it: from + to − is a maximum; from − to + is a minimum; if it does not change, it is not an extremum (like \\( x^3 \\) at the origin). A zero derivative does not guarantee an extremum \\( f(x) = x^3 \\) has \\( f'(0) = 0 \\) and at 0 there is neither a maximum nor a minimum: there is an inflection point with a horizontal tangent. That is why a critical point is a candidate, not a conclusion. To find the absolute extrema on a closed interval [a, b] —which exist, by Weierstrass — you compare the values at the critical points and at the two endpoints of the interval. The largest and the smallest of that list are the answer."},{"t":"Concavity and inflection","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#concavidad","x":"02 Concavity and inflection The second derivative measures how the slope changes: if \\( f'' > 0 \\) the curve is concave up and the slope increases; if \\( f'' < 0 \\), concave down. Where \\( f'' \\) changes sign there is an inflection point. From this comes the second-derivative test, quicker when it can be applied: if \\( f'(c) = 0 \\) and \\( f''(c) > 0 \\), there is a minimum at c; if \\( f''(c) < 0 \\), a maximum. If \\( f''(c) = 0 \\), the test is inconclusive and you have to go back to the first-derivative test. Lab · the function, its derivative and its second derivative The marked points are the critical and inflection points, computed live by checking where each derivative changes sign. f(x) = x³ − 3x f(x) = x⁴/4 − x² f(x) = 3·e −x² Damped oscillation: 3·e −0.4x ·sin(2x) Power delivered to a load R (with r = 2) f′ f″"},{"t":"Rolle, the mean value theorem and L’Hôpital","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#teoremas","x":"03 Rolle, the mean value theorem and L’Hôpital Lagrange’s theorem, or the mean value theorem If f is continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b), there is at least one point c where the slope of the tangent equals the slope of the secant joining the endpoints: \\[ f' (c) = \\frac{f (b) -f (a)}{b -a} \\] Translated: if a car covers 120 km in one hour, at some instant its speedometer read exactly \\( 120\\text{ km/h} \\). Rolle’\\( s \\) theorem is the special case with \\( f(a) = f(b) \\), where that slope is zero. Consequences of Lagrange’s theorem are used without being named: a function with zero derivative on an interval is constant, and two functions with the same derivative differ by a constant —the basis of the indefinite integral — and the increase and decrease criteria of section 1. L’Hôpital’s rule For a \\( 0/0 \\) or \\( \\infty/\\infty \\) indeterminate form, the limit of the quotient equals the limit of the quotient of the derivatives, if the latter exists: \\[ \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f (x)}{g (x)} = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f' (x)}{g' (x)} \\] It can be repeated if the indeterminate form persists. It is not the derivative of the quotient: it is the two derivatives taken separately. And you have to check that the indeterminate form is present before applying it."},{"t":"Optimization: the design problem","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#optimizacion","x":"04 Optimization: the design problem Optimizing is the bread and butter of engineering: maximizing power, efficiency or bandwidth; minimizing consumption, cost or dissipation. The method is always the same: Write the quantity to optimize as a function of a single variable, using the relations of the problem to eliminate the others. Establish the domain that makes physical sense. Differentiate, set equal to zero and solve. Check that it is a maximum or a minimum, and also check the boundaries of the domain. Worked example · maximum power transfer A voltage source V with internal resistance \\( r \\) drives a load \\( R \\). The current is \\( i = V/(r + R) \\) and the power in the load is: \\[ P (R) = i^2 R = \\frac{V^2 R}{{\\left(r + R \\right)}^2} \\] Differentiating with the quotient rule: \\[ P' (R) = V^2 \\frac{\\left(r + R)^2 -R \\cdot 2 (r + R \\right)}{{\\left(r + R \\right)}^4} = V^2 \\frac{r -R}{{\\left(r + R \\right)}^3} \\] \\( P' \\) vanishes at \\( R = r \\), and since \\( P' > 0 \\) before and \\( P' < 0 \\) after, it is a maximum. The maximum power is \\( P = V^2/(4r) \\), and the efficiency at that point is barely 50%: half of the energy is dissipated inside the source. That is why the criterion is used with small signals —an antenna, a radio-frequency amplifier— and not in power distribution, where what matters is efficiency."},{"t":"Taylor polynomials","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#taylor","x":"05 Taylor polynomials The tangent line approximates a function with a polynomial of degree 1. By adding terms with the successive derivatives, the approximation improves: \\[ f (x) \\approx f (a) + f' (a) (x -a) + \\frac{f^{''} (a)}{2 !} {\\left(x -a \\right)}^2 + \\cdots + \\frac{f^{\\left(n \\right)} (a)}{n !} {\\left(x -a \\right)}^n \\] With \\( a = 0 \\) it is called a Maclaurin polynomial. The error it makes has a formula of its own, the Lagrange remainder. Lab · approximating with polynomials f(x) = sin x f(x) = eˣ f(x) = ln(1 + x) f(x) = √(1 + x) degree n 1 The two approximations most used in electronics come from here: \\( \\sin x \\approx x \\) for small angles and \\( e^x \\approx 1 + x \\), which linearizes the diode equation around an operating point."},{"t":"Where all this is used","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#electronica","x":"06 Where all this is used Operating point and maximum swing. Placing a transistor’s Q point in the middle of the load line is an optimization problem: maximizing the symmetric swing of the signal before clipping. Impedance matching. The result \\( R = r \\) from section 4 is the DC version of the maximum transfer criterion, which at radio frequency is solved with the Smith chart. Harmonic distortion. When you expand an amplifier’\\( s \\) nonlinear characteristic in a Taylor series, \\( v_o = a_1v + a_2v^2 + a_3v^3 \\) + …, the quadratic term generates the second harmonic and the cubic one the third. Distortion is not an accident: it is the higher-order terms of the expansion. Newton–Raphson. The numerical method that solves nonlinear equations —the operating point of a diode, for example— is “moving along the tangent”: \\( x_{n+1} = x_n - f(x_n)/f'(x_n) \\). It converges much faster than bisection. Sensitivity and tolerances. The derivative of the output with respect to each component tells you how much its tolerance affects the result, and where it is worth spending on precision."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Exercise 1 · Verifying maximum transfer With a 10 V source and 100 Ω in series —the simulated internal resistance—, measure the power in loads of 10, 50, 100, 200 and 500 Ω. Plot \\( P \\) as a function of R and check that the maximum is at 100 Ω and equals \\( V^2/4r = 250\\text{ mW} \\). Exercise 2 · Distortion and harmonics Drive an amplifier close to clipping and observe the spectrum with an analyzer. Relate the appearance of harmonics to the nonlinear terms of the Taylor expansion. You can practice this in the instrument simulator. Exercise 3 · Optimizing a heat sink Set up the junction temperature of a transistor as a function of the thermal resistance of the heat sink and of cost, and find the minimum cost that meets the thermal specification. It is the same four-step method."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Taking every critical point to be an extremum without analyzing the sign change. Forgetting the endpoints of the interval when looking for absolute extrema. Applying L’Hôpital without an indeterminate form, or differentiating the quotient instead of each part. Optimizing without a physical domain: a negative resistance can be a mathematical solution and not exist. Confusing maximum power with maximum efficiency. At \\( R = r \\) the efficiency is 50%. Using \\( \\sin x \\approx x \\) outside small angles: at 30° the error is already almost 5%."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment Find and classify the extrema of \\( f(x) = x^3 - 3x \\). \\( f' = 3x^2 - 3 = 0 \\) at \\( x = \\pm 1 \\). \\( f'' = 6x \\): at \\( x = 1 \\) it is positive (minimum, \\( f = -2 \\)) and at \\( x = -1 \\) it is negative (maximum, \\( f = 2 \\)). Where is the inflection point of \\( f(x) = x^3 - 3x \\)? \\( f'' = 6x \\) vanishes and changes sign at \\( x = 0 \\): that is where the inflection is. Compute \\( \\lim_{x\\to 0} (e^x - 1)/x \\) with L’Hôpital. It is \\( 0/0 \\); differentiating top and bottom leaves \\( e^x/1 \\to \\) \\( 1 \\). A 12 V source with \\( r = 50\\ \\Omega \\). What load draws the maximum power and how much is it? \\( R = 50\\ \\Omega \\) and \\( P = V^2/(4r) = 144/200 = \\) \\( 0.72\\text{ W} \\). Write the degree-3 Maclaurin polynomial of \\( \\sin x \\). \\( x - x^3/6 \\). The degree-2 term is zero because the sine is odd. What does the mean value theorem say about a signal that goes from 0 to 5 V in 1 µs? That at some instant its slope was exactly \\( 5\\text{ V/}\\mu\\text{s} \\). If the amplifier has a lower slew rate, it cannot have produced that edge."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading James Stewart. Single Variable Calculus: Early Transcendentals. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo de una variable. Trascendentes tempranas”), Cengage Learning, 2012. Chapter 4 is exactly this topic: extrema, the mean value theorem, L’Hôpital’s rule and optimization. Louis Leithold. The Calculus 7. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “El cálculo”), Oxford University Press. Many solved optimization problems, with the setup detailed step by step. Tom M. Apostol. Calculus, Volume 1. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Calculus, volumen 1”), Reverté. Taylor’s formula with the remainder proved, so you know how good the approximation is."},{"t":"Areas between curves","d":"Applications of the definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-integral#areas","x":"01 Areas between curves The area enclosed between two curves is the integral of the difference, the upper one minus the lower one, between the points where they intersect: \\[ A = \\int_a^b \\left[ f (x) -g (x) \\right] d x \\] The limits a and b come from solving \\( f(x) = g(x) \\). If the curves cross inside the interval, you have to split it and swap the order of the subtraction on each piece. Worked example Between \\( y = x \\) and \\( y = x^2 \\): they intersect at \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\), and over that stretch the line is on top. \\( A = \\int_0^1 (x - x^2)\\,dx = [x^2/2 - x^3/3]_0^1 = 1/2 - 1/3 = \\) \\( 1/6 \\)."},{"t":"Volumes, lengths and surfaces","d":"Applications of the definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-integral#volumenes","x":"02 Volumes, lengths and surfaces Quantity Formula The idea Volume by disks \\( V = \\pi \\int_a^b [f(x)]^2\\,dx \\) Rotating about the x-axis, each slice is a disk of radius f(x) Volume by shells \\( V = 2\\pi \\int_a^b x\\cdot f(x)\\,dx \\) Rotating about the y-axis, each slice is a thin shell of radius x Arc length \\( L = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2}\\,dx \\) Each tiny piece is the hypotenuse of dx and dy Surface of revolution \\( S = 2\\pi \\int_a^b f(x)\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + [f']^2}\\,dx \\) Each tiny piece generates a ring The method is always the same, and it is worth remembering more than the formulas: cut into small pieces, write the contribution of a generic piece, and add up with an integral. This is also how you compute the work of a variable force, \\( W = \\int F\\,dx \\), the mass of a rod of variable density or the center of gravity of a part."},{"t":"Where \\( \\tfrac{1}{2}CV^2 \\) and \\( \\tfrac{1}{2}LI^2 \\) come from","d":"Applications of the definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-integral#energia","x":"03 Where \\( \\tfrac{1}{2}CV^2 \\) and \\( \\tfrac{1}{2}LI^2 \\) come from The two energy formulas used from memory in electronics are, in fact, one-line definite integrals: \\[ W_C = \\int p \\, d t = \\int v i \\, d t = \\int v \\cdot C \\frac{d v}{d t} d t = C \\int_0^V v \\, d v = \\frac{1}{2} C V^2 \\] The key step is changing the variable of integration from \\( t \\) to v: the dt cancels. With the inductor, the same procedure with i gives \\( W = \\tfrac{1}{2}L \\cdot I^2 \\). The same reasoning explains why charging a capacitor through a resistor dissipates exactly the same energy as the amount that ends up stored, \\( \\tfrac{1}{2}CV^2 \\), regardless of the resistor’\\( s \\) value: you integrate the power dissipated over the whole transient and that is the number you get. It is a result that surprises you the first time and that decides the efficiency of switching power supplies."},{"t":"Average and RMS values of any waveform","d":"Applications of the definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-integral#valores","x":"04 Average and RMS values of any waveform The definite integral gives the two numbers used to describe any periodic signal, and two more that are hardly ever taught and are used all the time: the form factor (RMS over rectified average) and the crest factor (peak over RMS), which tell how far the wave departs from a sine wave and how much headroom an amplifier needs. Lab · average value, RMS value and factors The four numbers are computed by numerical integration over one full period, just as a true RMS instrument does. Sine wave Half-wave rectified Full-wave rectified Square wave with duty cycle Triangle wave Sawtooth Duty cycle 50 % Peak 10 V"},{"t":"More applications in electronics","d":"Applications of the definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-integral#electronica","x":"05 More applications in electronics Power supply ripple. The ripple voltage of a capacitor filter comes from integrating the load current during the time the diode does not conduct: \\( \\Delta V = I\\cdot \\Delta t/C \\). It is the same computation as in the regulated power supply. Average power. The power that actually heats a component is the average value of \\( v\\cdot i \\) over a period, not the product of the average values. With out-of-phase signals, \\( \\cos \\varphi \\) appears. Battery charge. Capacity in ampere-hours is \\( \\int i\\,dt \\): the area under the current curve. Total harmonic distortion. It is defined with integrals of the square of each harmonic: it is a ratio between RMS values. Radiation dose, accumulated consumption, wear: any quantity that accumulates over time is a definite integral."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Applications of the definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-integral#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Exercise 1 · Areas and volumes Compute the area between \\( y = 4 - x^2 \\) and the x-axis, and the volume of the solid it generates when rotated about the x-axis. Check the volume against the paraboloid formula if you know it. Exercise 2 · The energy of a capacitor, measured Charge a capacitor of known value through a resistor and record \\( v(t) \\) and \\( i(t) \\). Numerically compute \\( \\int v\\cdot i\\,dt \\) up to the end of the transient and compare with \\( \\tfrac{1}{2}CV^2 \\). Also compute the energy dissipated in the resistor and check that the two are equal. Exercise 3 · Factors of a real signal Measure the average value, RMS value, form factor and crest factor of three signals from the generator with a digital oscilloscope, and compare with the values computed in the lab above. Discuss what an average-responding multimeter would read in each case."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Applications of the definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-integral#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Subtracting the wrong way round when computing areas between curves and getting a negative area. Not looking for the crossing points and using arbitrary limits. Forgetting the \\( \\pi \\) in volumes of revolution, or the square of the radius. Computing average power as the product of the average values of voltage and current. Using the factor 1.11 for any waveform: it is the form factor of the rectified sine wave. Integrating over an incomplete period and getting average values that mean nothing."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Applications of the definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-integral#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Area between \\( y = x^2 \\) and \\( y = 2x \\). They intersect at 0 and 2; the line is on top: \\( \\int_0^2 (2x - x^2)\\,dx = 4 - 8/3 = \\) \\( 4/3 \\). Volume when \\( y = \\sqrt{x} \\), with \\( 0 \\le x \\le 4 \\), is rotated about the x-axis. \\( V = \\pi \\int_0^4 x\\,dx = \\pi \\cdot 8 = \\) \\( 8\\pi \\approx 25.1 \\). How much energy does a 470 µF capacitor charged to 25 V store? \\( \\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 470\\cdot 10^{-6}\\cdot 625 \\approx \\) \\( 0.147\\text{ J} \\). Enough to give a noticeable spark when short-circuited: that is why they are discharged before being handled. A square wave of 10 V peak with a 25% duty cycle between 0 and 10 V. Average and RMS value? Average: \\( 10\\cdot 0.25 = 2.5\\text{ V} \\). RMS: \\( 10\\cdot \\sqrt{0.25} = \\) \\( 5\\text{ V} \\). The RMS value is much larger than the average, and that is why a PWM signal heats more than its average value suggests. What is the crest factor and what is it for? The ratio of the peak to the RMS value. It indicates how much voltage headroom an amplifier or power supply needs to avoid clipping: for the sine wave it is \\( \\sqrt{2} \\), and for audio or data signals it can reach 4 or more."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Applications of the definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-integral#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading James Stewart. Single Variable Calculus: Early Transcendentals. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo de una variable. Trascendentes tempranas”), Cengage Learning, 2012. Chapter 6 goes through areas, volumes, work and average value with the “cut and add up” method. Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Fundamentos de circuitos eléctricos”), McGraw-Hill, 2013. Average and RMS values, stored energy and average power, with the integrals set up. Louis Leithold. The Calculus 7. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “El cálculo”), Oxford University Press. Many geometric and physical application problems solved in detail."},{"t":"Architecture and organization","d":"Processor architecture and organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/arquitectura-procesadores#niveles","x":"01 Architecture and organization There are two ways of looking at a processor. The architecture is the contract with the software: the instruction set, the visible registers, the addressing modes, the memory model. The organization (or microarchitecture) is how that contract is fulfilled in silicon: how many stages the pipeline has, which caches there are, how many instructions are executed at once. Two processors with the same architecture, such as a Cortex-M0 and a Cortex-M7, run the same code with very different organizations and performance. The general view of CPU, memory and buses is in the computer system page of Computer Science I and in microprocessor architecture, from the technician program. The first organization decision is how instructions and data arrive. In the von Neumann architecture they share memory and bus; in the Harvard architecture, they have separate memories and buses and can be read at the same time. Almost all current processors are modified Harvard: a single main memory, but separate instruction and data caches."},{"t":"The instruction set","d":"Processor architecture and organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/arquitectura-procesadores#isa","x":"02 The instruction set CISC RISC Instructions Many, some of them complex, of variable length Few and simple, of fixed length (32 bits) Memory access Almost any instruction can operate on memory Only the load and store instructions Registers Few, with special uses Many general-purpose ones (32 in RISC-V) Examples x86, 8051 ARM, RISC-V, AVR, MIPS The simplicity of RISC is what allows execution to be pipelined efficiently: all instructions go through the same stages and take the same time in each one. Modern x86 processors translate their complex instructions internally into RISC-like micro-operations."},{"t":"The performance equation","d":"Processor architecture and organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/arquitectura-procesadores#rendimiento","x":"03 The performance equation \\[ T_{CPU} = N_{instr} \\cdot CPI \\cdot T_{clk} \\] The execution time is the number of instructions times the average cycles per instruction times the clock period. One billion instructions with a CPI of 1.5 at 500 MHz take 3 s. Improving one factor at the expense of another does not always pay off. The compiler and the instruction set decide \\( N_{instr} \\); the organization decides the CPI; the technology and the depth of the pipeline decide the clock. Comparing processors by their clock frequency alone ignores the other two factors."},{"t":"Pipelining","d":"Processor architecture and organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/arquitectura-procesadores#pipeline","x":"04 Pipelining A pipeline divides execution into stages, and each stage works on a different instruction at the same time, like an assembly line. The classic five stages: instruction fetch (IF), decode and register read (ID), execute (EX), memory access (MEM) and write-back of the result (WB). Ideally it finishes one instruction per cycle, and the clock can be up to five times faster than without pipelining. Hazard Cause Solution Structural Two stages need the same resource Duplicate the resource: separate instruction and data memories Data An instruction needs a result that has not been written yet Forwarding from the later stages; if that is not enough, a bubble Control A branch is resolved only in EX, with instructions already fetched behind it Branch prediction; if it fails, the fetched instructions are discarded"},{"t":"Memory hierarchy and cache","d":"Processor architecture and organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/arquitectura-procesadores#cache","x":"05 Memory hierarchy and cache A processor executes an instruction per nanosecond, and a DRAM takes tens of nanoseconds to respond. The solution is a hierarchy: a small, fast memory, the cache, keeps copies of what was used recently. It works because programs have locality: temporal (what was used is used again) and spatial (after one address, the next one is used). That is why the cache brings in entire lines of 32 or 64 bytes. The address is divided into three fields: the offset chooses the byte within the line, the index chooses the set where it can be, and the tag is compared to find out whether the stored line is the one sought. In a direct-mapped cache each line has only one possible place; in an n-way set-associative cache, n places. \\[ t_{medio} = t_{acierto} + m \\cdot t_{penalidad} \\] The average access time: with 1 cycle for a hit, 100 of penalty and a 5 % miss rate, 6 cycles. Each point of miss rate costs one cycle per access. Misses have three causes: compulsory (the first access to a line), capacity (the program uses more data than fits) and conflict (lines that compete for the same set even though there is room in others). The last depend on the addresses, and the same algorithm can be fast or slow depending on how it traverses the data. Lab · a cache facing four programs A data cache with LRU replacement receives the address sequence of a short program. The strip at the top shows the first 512 accesses (green, hit; red, miss) and the grid shows which array occup"},{"t":"Beyond one instruction per cycle","d":"Processor architecture and organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/arquitectura-procesadores#paralelismo","x":"06 Beyond one instruction per cycle Technique What it does Where Superscalar Several instructions per cycle, with out-of-order execution Computer and phone processors SIMD One instruction operates on several data items at once Vector extensions for audio, video and neural networks Multicore Several complete processors on one chip, with shared caches Almost everything that is not a simple microcontroller DSP Multiply-accumulate in one cycle and circular addressing Digital filters and signal processing"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Processor architecture and organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/arquitectura-procesadores#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Rows versus columns Write in C the sum of a 2048 × 2048 matrix traversed by rows and by columns, measure the times on a computer and explain the difference in terms of the cache. Activity 2 · Cycles per instruction On a microcontroller with a cycle counter, measure how many cycles a loop takes and compare it with the assembly code the compiler generated. Activity 3 · A pipeline by hand Draw the diagram of a five-stage pipeline for a sequence of six instructions with two data dependencies, with and without forwarding, and count the cycles."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Processor architecture and organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/arquitectura-procesadores#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Comparing processors by clock alone. The CPI and the number of instructions weigh just as much. Traversing a matrix against the way it is stored. In C it is stored by rows; traversing it by columns can multiply the time. Using power-of-two sizes and spacings without thinking. They make the cache indices coincide and cause conflict misses. Confusing architecture with organization. The same ARM binary runs on very different processors."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Processor architecture and organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/arquitectura-procesadores#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment How is a 32-bit address divided in a 4 KB direct-mapped cache with 32 B lines? 5 offset bits, 7 index bits (128 lines) and 20 tag bits. What average access time does a 2 % miss rate give with 1 cycle for a hit and 80 of penalty? \\( 1 + 0.02 \\cdot 80 = 2.6 \\) cycles. A program goes from CPI 2 to CPI 1.2, but the clock drops from 1 GHz to 800 MHz. Is it an improvement? Yes: the time per instruction goes from 2 ns to 1.5 ns. What type of miss does increasing associativity eliminate? Conflict misses. Capacity misses need a larger cache, and compulsory misses, longer lines or prefetching."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Processor architecture and organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/arquitectura-procesadores#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading David A. Patterson and John L. Hennessy. Computer Organization and Design: RISC-V Edition. 2nd ed., Morgan Kaufmann, 2020. The course text: instruction set, pipeline and memory hierarchy on RISC-V. John L. Hennessy and David A. Patterson. Computer Architecture: A Quantitative Approach. 6th ed., Morgan Kaufmann, 2017. The next level: instruction-level parallelism, advanced caches and accelerators. Sarah L. Harris and David Harris. Digital Design and Computer Architecture: RISC-V Edition. Morgan Kaufmann, 2021. Builds a complete RISC-V processor in HDL, starting from the gates."},{"t":"The technical aspect","d":"Technical, legal, economic, social and environmental aspects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aspectos-proyecto#tecnico","x":"01 The technical aspect The technical analysis confirms that the product can be made with the available technology, within the deadline and with the project team. It reviews the architecture alternatives, the availability of components and their risk of obsolescence, the existence of more than one supplier for critical parts, the development and test tools needed, and the points of greatest uncertainty, which are best attacked first with prototypes. A list of technical risks with their probability, their impact and a planned action is part of the project, not an appendix."},{"t":"The legal aspect","d":"Technical, legal, economic, social and environmental aspects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aspectos-proyecto#legal","x":"02 The legal aspect A product rests on legal decisions: the legal form of its manufacturer, covered in commercial companies; contracts with customers and suppliers; compliance with technical standards; and intellectual property. In Argentina, inventions are protected with patents from the INPI (National Institute of Industrial Property) under Law 24,481, and software, as a work, by the copyright regime of Law 11,723. If the product uses open-source libraries or systems, their licenses must be respected, which may require publishing your own code. The professional liability of whoever signs the project was covered in professional liability. Like any rule, the laws cited must be consulted in their current text on InfoLEG."},{"t":"The economic aspect","d":"Technical, legal, economic, social and environmental aspects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aspectos-proyecto#economico","x":"03 The economic aspect The economic analysis builds the cash flow and calculates the net present value and the internal rate of return, as seen in project evaluation. But each number in the flow is an estimate. Sensitivity analysis moves one variable at a time and measures how much the NPV changes; the tornado diagram ranks the variables from the most influential to the least, and the critical change of each is the one that takes the NPV to zero: \\[ NPV = -I + F \\cdot \\frac{1 - (1 + k)^{-n}}{k} \\] \\[ F = V\\,(p - c_v) - CF \\] With an annual flow F, V units per year, price p, variable cost c v, annual fixed costs CF, investment I, n years and cutoff rate k. Since the NPV is linear in each variable, the critical change is solved directly. Lab · sensitivity of the net present value A product with a price of ARS 250, 4000 units per year, a variable cost of ARS 150, fixed costs of ARS 150,000 per year, an investment of ARS 600,000 and a five-year life. Each bar goes from the NPV with the variable decreased to the NPV with the variable increased by the chosen percentage; the center line is the base NPV and the dashed one, zero. Variation of each variable = Cutoff rate k ="},{"t":"The social and environmental aspects","d":"Technical, legal, economic, social and environmental aspects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aspectos-proyecto#social","x":"04 The social and environmental aspects The social analysis asks whom the product benefits and whom it may harm: the users, the workers who make it, the community. It includes accessibility, the privacy of the data it collects, the safety of people and the employment it creates or replaces, and it rests on professional ethics. The environmental analysis covers the life cycle: the materials and the manufacturing energy, the consumption during use —which in many devices is the largest impact— and the end-of-life fate as electronic waste, with the criteria seen in environmental management."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Technical, legal, economic, social and environmental aspects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aspectos-proyecto#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Risk list Build the list of technical risks of the final project with probability, impact and planned action, and rank it. Activity 2 · Licenses Review the licenses of all the software libraries the project uses and note what obligations they impose. Activity 3 · Your own tornado Make the tornado diagram of the cash flow of the final project and decide which estimate is best to improve first."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Technical, legal, economic, social and environmental aspects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aspectos-proyecto#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Presenting a single NPV number. Without sensitivity, you do not know how fragile the conclusion is. Using open source without reading the license. Some require releasing the product’s code. Forgetting the consumption during use in the environmental analysis. It often weighs more than manufacturing. Treating the social analysis as a filler paragraph. The privacy and safety of users are requirements."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Technical, legal, economic, social and environmental aspects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aspectos-proyecto#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What NPV does the lab project have at 15 %? About ARS 238,000. How much can the price fall before the NPV turns negative? 7.1 %. And how much can the investment increase? 39.7 %. With which body is an invention patented in Argentina? The National Institute of Industrial Property (INPI)."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Technical, legal, economic, social and environmental aspects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aspectos-proyecto#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Nassir Sapag Chain and Reinaldo Sapag Chain. Preparación y evaluación de proyectos. McGraw-Hill. (In Spanish.) The technical, legal, economic and organizational studies, and sensitivity. Karl T. Ulrich and Steven D. Eppinger. Product Design and Development. McGraw-Hill. (Spanish edition: “Diseño y desarrollo de productos”.) Intellectual property and the economics of product development. International Organization for Standardization. ISO 14040: Environmental management — Life cycle assessment — Principles and framework. ISO. The framework for assessing the environmental impact of a product from end to end."},{"t":"Lowering the level without causing a mismatch","d":"Attenuators and compensators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/atenuadores-compensadores#atenuador","x":"01 Lowering the level without causing a mismatch An attenuator reduces the amplitude of a signal by a fixed number of decibels, and also presents the correct impedance on both sides. A simple resistive divider attenuates, but it changes the impedance seen by the generator and the load: in a 50 Ω system that produces reflections, as seen in transmission lines, and the real attenuation is no longer the calculated one. They are used to protect the input of an instrument, to lower the level of a generator below its minimum, to improve the match between two pieces of equipment and to measure gains by comparison."},{"t":"T and π attenuators","d":"Attenuators and compensators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/atenuadores-compensadores#t-pi","x":"02 T and π attenuators A symmetric attenuator designed for an impedance \\( Z_0 \\) has an image impedance of \\( Z_0 \\) on both sides: terminated in \\( Z_0 \\), it presents \\( Z_0 \\) at the input. With \\( K = 10^{A/20} \\), the voltage ratio corresponding to an attenuation of \\( A \\) decibels: \\[ R_{serie} = Z_0\\,\\frac{K - 1}{K + 1} \\] \\[ R_{der} = Z_0\\,\\frac{2K}{K^2 - 1} \\] For the T attenuator: two equal series resistors and one shunt resistor in the middle. \\[ R_{der} = Z_0\\,\\frac{K + 1}{K - 1} \\] \\[ R_{serie} = Z_0\\,\\frac{K^2 - 1}{2K} \\] For the π attenuator: two equal shunt resistors at the ends and one series resistor between them. The T is convenient for small attenuations, where the shunt resistor is large; the π, for large ones. Both are symmetric two-ports and, as seen in two-port networks, in cascade they add their decibels only if each one is properly terminated. The exact values almost never exist as commercial resistors, and rounding them changes the attenuation and the match slightly. Lab · designing an attenuator with commercial resistors You choose the topology, the impedance and the attenuation. The exact values are calculated, rounded to the nearest E24 (5 %) or E96 (1 %) series value and the real attenuator is recalculated between a generator and a load of \\( Z_0 \\). The schematic shows the rounded values. T attenuator π attenuator Z₀ = 50 Ω Z₀ = 75 Ω Z₀ = 600 Ω E24 series E96 series Attenuation ="},{"t":"Other attenuators","d":"Attenuators and compensators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/atenuadores-compensadores#otros","x":"03 Other attenuators Type What it is for Bridged T Variable attenuation by changing only two resistors Asymmetric minimum-loss Matching two different impedances, for example 50 and 75 Ω, with the least possible attenuation (5.7 dB) Stepped Switched attenuators in steps of 1, 2, 4, 8 dB, in generators and analyzers Voltage-controlled PIN diodes or integrated circuits, in automatic gain control"},{"t":"Compensators","d":"Attenuators and compensators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/atenuadores-compensadores#compensadores","x":"04 Compensators A compensator is a network that corrects the frequency response or the phase of another block. The simplest case was already seen in the oscilloscope: the compensated attenuator, with a capacitor in parallel with each resistor so that the divider is the same at all frequencies. In control, phase lead and lag networks are used: \\[ H(s) = \\alpha\\,\\frac{1 + s R_1 C_1}{1 + s\\,\\alpha R_1 C_1} \\] \\[ \\sin\\varphi_m = \\frac{1 - \\alpha}{1 + \\alpha} \\] The lead network: \\( R_1 \\) in parallel with \\( C_1 \\), in series, and \\( R_2 \\) to ground, with \\( \\alpha = R_2/(R_1 + R_2) \\). Between the zero and the pole it advances the phase, with a maximum \\( \\varphi_m \\) at the geometric mean of the two frequencies. With \\( \\alpha = 0.1 \\), the maximum phase lead is 55°. Cable equalizers are compensators of the same kind: a long cable attenuates high frequencies more, and a network with the inverse response restores them. Every high-speed data receiver has one, fixed or adaptive."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Attenuators and compensators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/atenuadores-compensadores#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · A 20 dB attenuator Build a 20 dB π for 50 Ω with E24 resistors, measure the attenuation with a generator and an oscilloscope terminated in 50 Ω, and compare with the lab. Activity 2 · Matching 50 to 75 Ω Design and build a minimum-loss attenuator between 50 and 75 Ω and measure the reflection with and without it. Activity 3 · Lead network Build a lead network with \\( \\alpha = 0.1 \\) and measure the phase as a function of frequency. Locate the maximum."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Attenuators and compensators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/atenuadores-compensadores#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Using a simple divider where an attenuator is needed. It changes the impedance and reflections appear. Forgetting the power rating of the resistors. The first resistor of an attenuator connected to a transmitter dissipates almost all the power. Terminating the attenuator in a different impedance. The attenuation and the match are no longer the design values. Ignoring parasitic inductances. At hundreds of megahertz, surface-mount resistors and short traces are essential."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Attenuators and compensators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/atenuadores-compensadores#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What resistors does a 6 dB T for 50 Ω have? With \\( K = 2 \\): series \\( 50 \\cdot 1/3 \\approx 16.7\\ \\Omega \\) and shunt \\( 50 \\cdot 4/3 \\approx 66.7\\ \\Omega \\). How much power does a 10 dB attenuator receiving 10 W dissipate? 1 W comes out: it dissipates 9 W. What maximum phase lead does a network with \\( \\alpha = 0.25 \\) give? \\( \\arcsin(0.75/1.25) = \\arcsin 0.6 \\approx 37° \\). Why do two badly terminated 3 dB attenuators not give 6 dB? Because the impedance each one sees is no longer the design one, and their attenuation and match change."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Attenuators and compensators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/atenuadores-compensadores#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Mac E. Van Valkenburg. Network Analysis. 3rd ed., Prentice Hall, 1974 (Spanish edition, “Análisis de redes”, Limusa). Image impedances and symmetric networks, the basis of attenuator design. Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed., Prentice Hall, 2010 (Spanish edition, “Ingeniería de control moderna”, Pearson). Lead and lag networks applied to compensator design. H. Ward Silver (ed.). The ARRL Handbook for Radio Communications. ARRL, annual editions. Tables and practical construction of attenuators for radio frequency."},{"t":"From the bench instrument to the test system","d":"Advanced measurement automation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-avanzada#plataformas","x":"01 From the bench instrument to the test system The measurement automation of Measurements I connected bench instruments to a computer with SCPI commands. In production, where thousands of devices are tested per day, that is not enough: compact, fast and easy-to-maintain systems are needed. There are three main modular platforms: Platform Idea Advantage PXI Instruments as modules inside a chassis with a PCI Express bus, shared synchronization and triggers Speed and density: dozens of instruments in one chassis LXI Standalone instruments over Ethernet, with a web interface and synchronization through IEEE 1588 Distributed instruments, no chassis AXIe High-performance modules in a larger chassis High-end instruments, such as many-channel oscilloscopes On top of the hardware a layer of interchangeable drivers is used, such as IVI, which present a common interface per instrument class: a program written for one multimeter works with another from a different manufacturer without changes."},{"t":"Sequencers and parallel testing","d":"Advanced measurement automation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-avanzada#secuencia","x":"02 Sequencers and parallel testing A test sequencer organizes the test of a product as a list of steps, each with its measurement, its limits and its action on failure, and stores the results with the serial number. The same measurement code is reused across different products. To increase the number of units tested per hour, testing is done in parallel: several units at once, sharing the expensive instruments and using the cheap ones in parallel. The bottleneck is usually the shared instrument, and the design of the system consists of balancing the times."},{"t":"Process capability","d":"Advanced measurement automation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-avanzada#capacidad","x":"03 Process capability The test system decides whether each unit passes or not, but the accumulated data say something more: how well the manufacturing process meets the specification. If the measured quantity has mean \\( \\mu \\) and standard deviation \\( \\sigma \\), and the specification runs from \\( LSL \\) to \\( USL \\): \\[ C_p = \\frac{USL - LSL}{6\\sigma} \\] \\[ C_{pk} = \\frac{\\min(USL - \\mu,\\ \\mu - LSL)}{3\\sigma} \\] \\( C_p \\) compares the width of the specification with the spread; \\( C_{pk} \\) also takes into account the shift of the mean. With \\( C_{pk} = 1 \\), 0.27% falls outside the specification; with 1.33, about 63 parts per million if the process is centered (about 30 if it is shifted and only the tail nearest one limit counts); with 1.67, fewer than one. The automotive industry usually demands 1.33 or more. Lab · process capability and yield The output voltage of a power supply, specified as 5.00 ± 0.10 V. The bell curve is the distribution of production; the red zones, the units out of specification. The histogram shows 2000 simulated units. Production mean = Standard deviation = The uncertainty of the measurement itself has to be added: with a narrow specification and an instrument that scatters, good units are rejected and bad ones accepted. The guard bands from testing and standards also apply here."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Advanced measurement automation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-avanzada#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · A sequence Program in Python a test sequence for a laboratory power supply with a multimeter and an electronic load over LXI or USB: no-load voltage, load regulation and ripple, with limits and logging of results. Activity 2 · Real Cpk Measure the resistance of 50 1% resistors from the same bag and calculate Cp and Cpk with respect to their tolerance. Activity 3 · Test time Measure the time of each step of the sequence and propose how to reduce it: fewer averages, range changes, parallel testing."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Advanced measurement automation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-avanzada#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Looking only at Cp. A narrow but shifted process has a good Cp and a bad Cpk. Calculating the capability with few samples. The estimated standard deviation varies a lot; tens or hundreds of units are needed. Forgetting the test uncertainty. It adds to the spread of the process. Writing the test tied to an instrument model. Interchangeable drivers avoid rewriting it when a device is changed."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Advanced measurement automation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-avanzada#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment Specification of 10 ± 0.3 mA, mean 10.1 mA and \\( \\sigma = 0.05\\ \\text{mA} \\). What are Cp and Cpk? \\( C_p = 0.6/0.3 = 2 \\); \\( C_{pk} = 0.2/0.15 = 1.33 \\). What fraction falls outside the specification with \\( C_{pk} = 1 \\) and the process centered? 0.27%. What advantage does LXI have over PXI? The instruments can be distributed over a network, with no chassis, and are synchronized through IEEE 1588. What are IVI drivers for? So that the test program does not depend on the instrument model within a given class."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Advanced measurement automation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-avanzada#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Douglas C. Montgomery. Introduction to Statistical Quality Control. 8th ed., Wiley, 2019. Process capability, the Cp and Cpk indices and statistical control. PXI Systems Alliance and LXI Consortium. PXI and LXI Specifications. Available on each consortium’s website. The definitions of the modular platforms and their synchronization. IVI Foundation. IVI Specifications. IVI Foundation. The instrument classes and the interchangeable drivers."},{"t":"Why automate a measurement","d":"Measurement automation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-mediciones#por-que","x":"01 Why automate a measurement Sweeping the frequency response of a filter at a hundred points, recording a temperature every minute for a week or testing a thousand boards at the end of a production line cannot be done by hand. An automatic measurement system controls the instruments from a computer: it configures them, triggers the measurements, reads the results and stores them with all their data. It gains speed, repeatability and record-keeping: each measurement is made exactly the same way, and it is documented which instrument, under which conditions and when."},{"t":"Controller, bus and instruments","d":"Measurement automation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-mediciones#arquitectura","x":"02 Controller, bus and instruments Interface Characteristics Use GPIB (IEEE 488) 8-bit parallel bus, up to 15 instruments, about 20 m, around 1 MB/s Laboratory benches since the 1970s; still very widespread RS-232 Point-to-point serial, slow Simple instruments and old equipment USB-TMC USB with a standardized class for instruments Current benchtop instruments LAN / LXI Ethernet with common discovery and trigger rules Distributed systems and remote control PXI Instruments on cards inside a chassis with a PCI Express bus and synchronized triggering Production testing, high speed So as not to write a different program for each interface, the software uses an intermediate library, VISA, which opens a session with any instrument from an address such as GPIB0::22::INSTR, USB0::…::INSTR or TCPIP0::192.168.1.20::INSTR, and offers the same functions for writing and reading. In Python, the PyVISA package uses it; in industry LabVIEW is also used."},{"t":"The SCPI language","d":"Measurement automation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-mediciones#scpi","x":"03 The SCPI language Almost all programmable instruments understand SCPI ( Standard Commands for Programmable Instruments ), a text language with hierarchical commands separated by colons. Each word has a long form and a short one, the part in capitals: MEASure:VOLTage:DC? can be written MEAS:VOLT:DC?. Commands ending in ? are queries and return a response. The IEEE 488.2 standard adds common commands that start with an asterisk: Command What it does *IDN? Returns manufacturer, model, serial number and version *RST Brings the instrument to a known state *OPC? Responds 1 when the pending operations have finished: used to synchronize SYST:ERR? Reads the next error from the queue; a robust program empties it at the end"},{"t":"The structure of a measurement program","d":"Measurement automation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-mediciones#programa","x":"04 The structure of a measurement program A well-made program always follows the same steps: bring the instrument to a known state, configure it explicitly (without depending on how the last user left it), trigger, read, check that there are no errors and store the data with its context. In Python with PyVISA: import pyvisa rm = pyvisa.ResourceManager() dmm = rm.open_resource(\"USB0::0x1234::0x5678::MY001::INSTR\") dmm.timeout = 5000 # milliseconds print(dmm.query(\"*IDN?\")) dmm.write(\"*RST\") dmm.write(\"CONF:VOLT:DC 10\") # fixed function and range dmm.write(\"VOLT:DC:NPLC 10\") # integration of 10 mains cycles dmm.write(\"TRIG:COUN 5\") readings = [float(x) for x in dmm.query(\"READ?\").split(\",\")] print(dmm.query(\"SYST:ERR?\")) # should be +0,\"No error\" The speed of an automatic measurement is almost always set by the instrument, not the bus. A multimeter that integrates 10 mains cycles takes 200 ms per reading at 50 Hz, and autoranging and auto-zero add more. It is the same trade-off seen in interference: more integration time, less noise and more 50 Hz rejection, but fewer readings per second. Lab · an SCPI console A simulated programmable multimeter, connected to a 1.234567 V source and a 1000.12 Ω resistor. You tap a command or type it, and the instrument responds like a real one, with its error queue and the time each reading would take. The model is fictional. send"},{"t":"Acquisition boards and good practices","d":"Measurement automation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-mediciones#daq","x":"05 Acquisition boards and good practices For many signals at once, data acquisition boards (DAQ) are used: a converter with an input multiplexer, digital inputs and outputs, counters and sometimes analog outputs, connected over USB, PCI Express or Ethernet. With multiplexing, the sampling rate is shared among the channels, and switching channels requires a settling time: with high-impedance sources, one channel contaminates the next. Automatic data is worth as much as its context. Each file should store, along with the readings, the identification and configuration of the instruments, the environmental conditions, the date of the last calibration and the version of the program."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Measurement automation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-mediciones#laboratorio","x":"06 In the laboratory Activity 1 · First contact Connect a multimeter or an oscilloscope over USB, identify it with *IDN? from PyVISA and read a measurement. Activity 2 · Automatic frequency response Program a generator and an oscilloscope to sweep a filter from 100 Hz to 100 kHz in 50 logarithmic points and plot the Bode diagram. Activity 3 · Long-duration logging Record the voltage of a discharging battery every 10 s for hours, storing the data with its context in a CSV file."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Measurement automation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-mediciones#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Not configuring everything explicitly. The program works until someone leaves the instrument in another mode. Reading before the measurement finishes. Without *OPC? or a suitable wait time, old data is read. Ignoring the error queue. A badly written command does not stop the program: it only leaves an error that nobody reads. Saving numbers without units or context. A file of readings without configuration is useless a month later."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Measurement automation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-mediciones#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment How long does a multimeter take to take 100 readings with NPLC 10 on a 50 Hz mains? At least \\( 100 \\cdot 200\\ \\text{ms} = 20\\ \\text{s} \\), plus the own time of each conversion. What is the short form of SENSe:VOLTage:DC:RANGe ? SENS:VOLT:DC:RANG, and since SENSe is optional, also VOLT:DC:RANG. What is *OPC? for? To wait for the instrument to finish what is pending before continuing: it responds 1 only then. What advantage does VISA give over programming each interface? That the same program works with the instrument connected over GPIB, USB or network, changing only the address."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Measurement automation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-mediciones#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading SCPI Consortium. Standard Commands for Programmable Instruments (SCPI), Volume 1: Syntax and Style. SCPI Consortium, 1999. The language standard: syntax, command hierarchy and response format. John Park and Steve Mackay. Practical Data Acquisition for Instrumentation and Control Systems. Newnes, 2003. Acquisition boards, interfaces and software for measurement systems. Alan S. Morris and Reza Langari. Measurement and Instrumentation: Theory and Application. 3rd ed., Academic Press, 2020. Computerized measurement systems in the context of complete instrumentation."},{"t":"Directions that do not turn","d":"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/autovalores-autovectores#idea","x":"01 Directions that do not turn A linear transformation, in general, moves every vector and changes its direction. But there are almost always a few privileged directions along which the vector does not turn: it is only stretched, shortened or flipped. Those directions are the eigenvectors, and the factor by which they are stretched, the eigenvalues. \\[ A \\vec{v} = \\lambda \\vec{v} \\quad \\operatorname{with} \\; \\vec{v} \\ne 0 \\] The zero vector is excluded on purpose: it would satisfy the equality for any λ and would carry no information. They are also called proper (or characteristic) values and vectors. The prefix comes from the German eigen, which means “own”: they are characteristic of the transformation, not of the coordinate system used to write it."},{"t":"How they are computed","d":"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/autovalores-autovectores#calculo","x":"02 How they are computed The condition \\( A \\cdot \\vec{v} = \\lambda \\cdot \\vec{v} \\) is rewritten as \\( (A - \\lambda I) \\cdot \\vec{v} = 0 \\), which is a homogeneous system. That system has solutions other than the trivial one only if its determinant vanishes: \\[ \\det (A -\\lambda I) = 0 \\] This is the characteristic polynomial: a polynomial of degree n in λ, whose roots are the eigenvalues. For 2 × 2 it is especially compact, using the trace (the sum of the diagonal) and the determinant: \\[ \\lambda^2 -\\operatorname{tr} (A) \\lambda + \\det (A) = 0 \\] Once each λ is found, the eigenvector is obtained by solving the homogeneous system \\( (A - \\lambda I) \\cdot \\vec{v} = 0 \\). The solution is never unique: if v is an eigenvector, any multiple of it is one too. What matters is the direction, not the length. Worked example \\( A = \\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\end{bmatrix} \\): \\( \\operatorname{tr} = 7 \\) and \\( \\det = 12 - 2 = 10 \\), so \\( \\lambda ^{2} - 7\\lambda + 10 = 0 \\) and the eigenvalues are \\( \\lambda _{1} = 5 \\) and \\( \\lambda _{2} = 2 \\). For \\( \\lambda = 5 \\): \\( (A - 5I) = \\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\end{bmatrix} \\), and the equation \\( -x + y = 0 \\) gives the direction \\( \\vec{v}_1 = (1,\\ 1) \\). Check: \\( A\\cdot (1,1) = (5,\\ 5) = 5\\cdot (1,1) \\) ✔ For \\( \\lambda = 2 \\): \\( \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\), and \\( 2x + y = 0 \\) gives \\( \\vec{v}_2 = (1,\\ -2) \\). Check: \\( A\\cdot (1,-2) = (2,\\ -4) = 2\\cdot (1,-2) \\) ✔ Two checks that cost nothing The su"},{"t":"Seeing them","d":"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/autovalores-autovectores#laboratorio-auto","x":"03 Seeing them The circle of unit vectors is transformed into an ellipse. The directions where the blue and the orange arrows are aligned are the eigenvectors, and the ratio of lengths is the eigenvalue. Lab · eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a 2 × 2 matrix A =; Two real eigenvalues Rotation 90°: complex Symmetric Multiple of the identity Shear: repeated eigenvalue Iterate v ← A·v"},{"t":"Diagonalization","d":"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/autovalores-autovectores#diagonalizacion","x":"04 Diagonalization If a matrix of order n has n linearly independent eigenvectors, they can be used as a basis. In that basis the transformation only stretches each axis: its matrix is diagonal, with the eigenvalues on the diagonal. \\[ A = P D P^{-1} \\quad A^k = P D^k P^{-1} \\] P has the eigenvectors as columns and D the eigenvalues on the diagonal. Raising D to the k is raising each number on the diagonal: that is why diagonalization makes powers easy. If the n eigenvalues are distinct, the matrix is always diagonalizable. If any of them is repeated, it may not be: the shear \\( \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\) has a double \\( \\lambda = 1 \\) and only one eigen-direction. Every symmetric matrix with real entries is diagonalizable, its eigenvalues are real and its eigenvectors are perpendicular to each other. This is the theorem behind the reduction to principal axes in quadratic forms. The eigenvalues can be complex, and then no real direction is preserved: this is what happens with a rotation, and in a circuit it means oscillation."},{"t":"What they represent in a physical system","d":"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/autovalores-autovectores#ingenieria","x":"05 What they represent in a physical system A circuit with inductors and capacitors is described by a system of first-order differential equations, written in matrix form as \\( \\dot{x} = A\\cdot x \\), where x gathers the inductor currents and the capacitor voltages. The solution is built from exponentials \\( e^{\\lambda t} \\), and those λ are the eigenvalues of A: the natural modes of the circuit, which are the same poles that appear in the frequency response. Eigenvalues of A Behavior Example Negative real Exponential decay without oscillating: stable, overdamped RC with large resistance Complex with negative real part Oscillation that dies out: stable, underdamped RLC with little damping Purely imaginary Oscillation that does not die out Ideal lossless LC With positive real part Grows without bound: unstable Poorly compensated feedback loop; an oscillator at start-up Hence the stability criterion is “all eigenvalues with negative real part”. The same idea reappears in control, in the resonant frequencies of a mechanical structure, in the modes of a waveguide and in the principal components of a data set."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/autovalores-autovectores#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Exercise 1 · By hand and verified Compute the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of \\( \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix} \\) and of \\( \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3 \\end{bmatrix} \\). Verify in each case with the trace and the determinant, and check \\( A \\cdot \\vec{v} = \\lambda \\cdot \\vec{v} \\) by substitution. Compare with the lab. Exercise 2 · The modes of an RLC circuit Write \\( \\dot{x} = A\\cdot x \\) for a series RLC circuit with \\( x = (i_{L},\\ v_{C}) \\), compute the eigenvalues for three values of R —overdamped, critical and underdamped— and compare with the waveform obtained in a simulation. Exercise 3 · The power method Take any vector and multiply it repeatedly by A, normalizing each time. Observe that it aligns with the eigenvector of the eigenvalue of largest magnitude. It is the “Iterate” button of the lab, and it is the algorithm with which Google used to rank its results."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/autovalores-autovectores#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Looking for the eigenvector before the eigenvalue. First solve \\( \\det (A - \\lambda I) = 0 \\). Subtracting λ only from the first element of the diagonal instead of from the whole diagonal. Giving the zero vector as an eigenvector: it is excluded by definition. Believing the eigenvector is unique: the whole line it spans is. Assuming that every matrix is diagonalizable. With repeated eigenvalues you have to check how many independent eigenvectors there are. Discarding complex eigenvalues: they are precisely the ones that describe oscillations."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/autovalores-autovectores#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Eigenvalues of \\( \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{bmatrix} \\). It is diagonal: 3 and −2, with eigenvectors î and ĵ. If \\( \\operatorname{tr}(A) = 6 \\) and \\( \\det (A) = 8 \\) for a 2 × 2 matrix, what are its eigenvalues? \\( \\lambda^2 - 6\\lambda + 8 = 0 \\to \\lambda = 4 \\) and \\( \\lambda = 2 \\). What does a zero eigenvalue mean? That the matrix is singular: there is a direction that ends up at the origin, the kernel is not trivial and \\( \\det (A) = 0 \\). Why does a 90° rotation in the plane have no real eigenvectors? Because no direction is preserved: every vector turns. Its eigenvalues are ±i, purely imaginary, which is the signature of a pure oscillation. A circuit has eigenvalues \\( -100 \\pm \\mathrm{j}2000\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}} \\). How does it respond? It oscillates at about 2000 rad/s (318 Hz) with an envelope that decays with a time constant of \\( 1/100 = 10\\text{ ms} \\): it is stable and underdamped. What is diagonalizing good for? For working in the basis where the transformation only stretches each axis: powers, matrix exponentials and the solution of systems of differential equations become immediate."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/autovalores-autovectores#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Stanley I. Grossman. Álgebra lineal. 6th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2008 (in Spanish). Eigenvalues, diagonalization and applications to systems of differential equations. Gilbert Strang. Linear Algebra and Its Applications. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Álgebra lineal y sus aplicaciones”), Cengage Learning, 2007. Chapter 5 connects eigenvalues with stability, powers and the matrix exponential. Norman S. Nise. Control Systems Engineering. 3rd ed. (Spanish edition, “Sistemas de control para ingeniería”), CECSA, 2006. Where they are used in practice: state variables, poles and stability of feedback systems."},{"t":"What “low level” means","d":"Using the language for low-level applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bajo-nivel#bajo-nivel","x":"01 What “low level” means A low-level program is one that talks directly to the hardware: it lights an LED by writing a bit into a register, reads a sensor by waiting for a status bit to change, builds a frame bit by bit to send it over a wire. In the stack of layers from the first topic, it means working just above the instruction set architecture, with no operating system in between. C is suited to this because it has three things that high-level languages hide: bit operators, pointers to fixed addresses and types of known size. With them, the same language used to write a spreadsheet takes control of the pins of a chip."},{"t":"Bit operators and masks","d":"Using the language for low-level applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bajo-nivel#bits","x":"02 Bit operators and masks Operator What it does What it is used for a & b Bitwise AND Setting bits to 0 and testing a bit a | b Bitwise OR Setting bits to 1 a ^ b Bitwise exclusive OR Toggling bits ~a Complement of all the bits Building the mask for clearing a << n, a >> n Shift Building masks and extracting fields A mask is a number that has 1s in the bits you want to act on. (1 << 5) is 0010 0000: only bit 5. With it, you get the four operations that are used all the time: reg |= ( 1 << n); // set bit n to 1 reg &= ~( 1 << n); // clear bit n to 0 reg ^= ( 1 << n); // toggle bit n if (reg & ( 1 << n)) // is bit n set to 1? All four touch only the chosen bit and leave the others as they were. That is what matters when each bit of a register controls a different pin: turning on one LED must not turn off the one next to it. Lab · an 8-LED port The PORTB register drives eight LEDs. Choose the operation and the bit, and look at the C line, the binary calculation and the result. Set to 1: |= (1 << n) Clear to 0: &= ~(1 << n) Toggle: ^= (1 << n) Test: & (1 << n) Assign (the mistake): = (1 << n) n = 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Run PORTB = 0x96 Fields of more than one bit To read a field, shift and mask: bits 4 to 6 of reg are (reg >> 4) & 0x07. To write it without touching the rest, first clear it and then set it: reg = (reg & ~(0x07 << 4)) | ((v & 0x07) << 4). This is how you configure, for example, the clock divider of a timer or the channel of an A/D converter."},{"t":"Registers at fixed addresses","d":"Using the language for low-level applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bajo-nivel#registros","x":"03 Registers at fixed addresses In a microcontroller, peripherals are controlled through registers located at fixed memory addresses, published in the datasheet. In the ATmega328P (the one in the Arduino Uno), the output register of port B is at data address 0x25. In C it is reached with a pointer to that address: #include <stdint.h> #define PORTB (*( volatile uint8_t *) 0x25 ) #define DDRB (*( volatile uint8_t *) 0x24 ) DDRB |= ( 1 << 5 ); // PB5 as an output (the on-board LED) PORTB |= ( 1 << 5 ); // and turned on Read from the inside out: 0x25 is a number; (volatile uint8_t *) turns it into the address of a byte; the outer * accesses the byte that is there. From then on, PORTB is used like any other variable. The manufacturer’s header files ( <avr/io.h>, the CMSIS ones on ARM) are built exactly this way. Why volatile The compiler optimizes on the assumption that a variable only changes when the program writes it. A hardware register changes on its own: the “data received” bit of a UART is set to 1 when a byte arrives. Without volatile, a loop while (!(UCSR0A & (1 << RXC0))); may be compiled to read the register just once and hang forever. volatile forces the real memory to be read and written on every access. It is needed for hardware registers and for variables modified by an interrupt service routine."},{"t":"Exact-width types","d":"Using the language for low-level applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bajo-nivel#tipos","x":"04 Exact-width types The C standard only guarantees minimums: an int has at least 16 bits. On an AVR it has 16; on a PC, 32. A program that assumes 32 bits breaks when ported to a microcontroller. For work with hardware, the types from <stdint.h> are used, which state their size in the name: Type Bits Range uint8_t / int8_t 8 0 … 255 / −128 … 127 uint16_t / int16_t 16 0 … 65,535 / −32,768 … 32,767 uint32_t / int32_t 32 0 … 4,294,967,295 / ±2,147,483,647 Example: scaling an A/D converter reading A 10-bit converter with a 5 V reference delivers 0 to 1023. To convert it to millivolts: uint32_t mv = (uint32_t)reading * 5000 / 1023;. Without the conversion to 32 bits, on an AVR the multiplication would be done in 16 bits: \\( 1023 \\cdot 5000 = 5\\,115\\,000 \\) does not fit in 65,535 and the result comes out wrong with no warning. With the conversion, 1023 gives exactly 5000 mV. Converters are covered in A/D and D/A converters."},{"t":"Byte order and bit fields","d":"Using the language for low-level applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bajo-nivel#orden","x":"05 Byte order and bit fields A uint32_t takes up four bytes, but in what order? In a little-endian processor (x86, almost all ARM, AVR) the least significant byte goes at the lowest address; in a big-endian one, the other way around. The value 0x12345678 stored at 0x100 ends up like this: Address 0x100 0x101 0x102 0x103 Little-endian 78 56 34 12 Big-endian 12 34 56 78 Inside a program it goes unnoticed. It shows up when sending data to another machine: network protocols use big-endian, which is why htons and ntohl exist. The portable way is to build the bytes with shifts, b[0] = x >> 24; b[1] = x >> 16; …, which gives the same result on any processor. This applies to any frame of serial communication. C also allows declaring bit fields inside a structure, unsigned mode: 3;. They are convenient, but the standard leaves the order of the bits within the byte to the compiler. To describe hardware registers that must match the datasheet bit for bit, masks are safer."},{"t":"The program facing the hardware","d":"Using the language for low-level applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bajo-nivel#hardware","x":"06 The program facing the hardware A program for a microcontroller never ends: it initializes the peripherals and enters an infinite loop. Inside the loop it polls inputs and updates outputs; whatever is urgent is handled by an interrupt service routine, which communicates with the main loop through volatile variables: volatile uint8_t data_ready = 0; volatile uint8_t data; ISR (USART_RX_vect) // called by the hardware when a byte arrives { data = UDR0; data_ready = 1; } int main ( void ) { init (); for (;;) { if (data_ready) { data_ready = 0; process (data); } /* rest of the work, without long delays */ } } Accesses that get cut in half On an 8-bit processor, reading a uint16_t takes two instructions. If the interrupt modifies it between the two, the program reads the old half and the new half. Such reads are done with interrupts disabled for an instant (a critical section ). It is an error that appears once in a thousand runs and is not found by testing: it is avoided by knowing that it exists. Interrupts and peripherals are covered in interrupts and in microcontroller peripherals."},{"t":"What the compiler does with this","d":"Using the language for low-level applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bajo-nivel#ensamblador","x":"07 What the compiler does with this The best way to convince yourself that C is close to the machine is to look at what it generates. For the AVR, with optimization: C AVR assembly Comment PORTB |= (1 << 5); sbi 0x05, 5 One instruction that sets a bit of an I/O register to 1 PORTB &= ~(1 << 5); cbi 0x05, 5 And another that clears it to 0 if (PINB & (1 << 0)) sbis 0x03, 0 Skips the next instruction if the bit is 1 The 0x05 is the address of PORTB in the I/O space, which in the data space is the 0x25 from the previous example: there is a difference of 0x20 because the first 32 bytes of the data space are the working registers. One line of C, one machine instruction: that is why there is no need to program in assembly to have fine control. Microcontrollers are covered in microprocessor architecture and the buses driven with these techniques, in serial buses."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Using the language for low-level applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bajo-nivel#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Exercise 1 · Blinking without libraries On an Arduino Uno, blink the PB5 LED by writing directly to DDRB and PORTB, without pinMode or digitalWrite. Use the oscilloscope to measure the maximum frequency achieved with PORTB ^= (1 << 5) in an empty loop and compare it with that of digitalWrite. You can practice with the oscilloscope beforehand in the instrument simulator. Exercise 2 · Bit functions on the PC Write and test on the PC set_bit, clr_bit, toggle_bit, get_field and set_field on a uint8_t, printing each result in binary. Test the extreme cases: bit 0, bit 7, a field that takes up the whole byte. Exercise 3 · Discovering the byte order Store 0x12345678 in a uint32_t, point a uint8_t * at it and print the four bytes. Deduce whether the machine is little- or big-endian."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Using the language for low-level applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bajo-nivel#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes reg = (1 << n) instead of reg |= (1 << n): it turns one bit on and turns all the others off. reg &= (1 << n) to turn off: without the ~ it clears all the bits except n. Forgetting volatile on a register or on a variable shared with an interrupt. Shifting by more bits than the type has: 1 << 31 with a 16-bit int is undefined; write 1UL << 31. Confusing && with &: 4 && 2 is 1; 4 & 2 is 0. Writing magic numbers bare: PORTB |= 0x20 works, but (1 << PB5) is understandable."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Using the language for low-level applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bajo-nivel#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment Which line clears bits 2 and 3 of reg to 0 without touching the others? reg &= ~((1 << 2) | (1 << 3));, that is, reg &= ~0x0C; If reg = 0xB4, what is the value of bits 4 to 6? 0xB4 = 1011 0100. (0xB4 >> 4) & 0x07 = 1011 & 111 = 011 = 3. Why does a loop that waits for a status bit need volatile ? Because without it the compiler may read the register just once, assume it does not change and turn the wait into an infinite loop. How is 0xA1B2 laid out in memory on a little-endian machine? First B2 and then A1. Why can reading * 5000 come out wrong on an AVR if reading is uint16_t ? Because the calculation is done in int, which on an AVR has 16 bits, and the product exceeds 65,535. You have to convert to 32 bits before multiplying. What is a critical section? A stretch of code that cannot be interrupted halfway without leaving the data inconsistent. On a microcontroller it is protected by disabling interrupts during that stretch."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Using the language for low-level applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bajo-nivel#ampliar","x":"11 Further reading Michael Barr and Anthony Massa. Programming Embedded Systems: With C and GNU Development Tools. 2nd ed., O'Reilly, 2006. Registers, interrupts and peripherals in C, with examples on real hardware. Elecia White. Making Embedded Systems. 2nd ed., O'Reilly, 2024. How to design the software of an embedded system from end to end, with an engineering mindset. Randal E. Bryant and David R. O'Hallaron. Computer Systems: A Programmer's Perspective. 3rd ed., Pearson, 2016. Chapter 3 shows, line by line, what the compiler generates from C. Microchip Technology. ATmega328P Datasheet. Official datasheet, on the manufacturer’s website. The I/O register map and the instruction set that were used in this topic."},{"t":"Small signal and large signal","d":"The bipolar transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-amplificador#dos-regimenes","x":"01 Small signal and large signal The bipolar transistor is an exponential device: the collector current follows the base-emitter voltage as \\( i_C = I_S\\, e^{v_{BE}/V_T} \\). No exponential is a straight line, so no amplifier built with transistors is exactly linear. What you do is work around a quiescent point and separate two regimes: Small signal: the swing is so small that the exponential looks like its tangent. The linear hybrid-π model from the unit on the bipolar transistor applies, and the gain does not depend on the amplitude. Large signal: the swing covers an appreciable part of the curve. Distortion appears, the gain changes with the amplitude, and if the signal is large the output is clipped against saturation or cutoff. The boundary between the two is not a fixed number: it depends on how much distortion is tolerated. This page calculates it."},{"t":"The quiescent point","d":"The bipolar transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-amplificador#polarizacion","x":"02 The quiescent point Before amplifying you have to bias: set a stable collector current and a collector voltage that leaves room to swing both up and down. Biasing with a voltage divider at the base and an emitter resistor sets the current almost independently of β, because \\( I_C \\approx (V_B - V_{BE})/R_E \\). For the maximum symmetrical swing, the quiescent collector voltage is set near the midpoint between saturation and the supply. The technician program develops these circuits in small-signal amplifiers."},{"t":"The three small-signal configurations","d":"The bipolar transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-amplificador#debil","x":"03 The three small-signal configurations Depending on which terminal is common to the input and the output, the transistor gives three amplifiers with very different properties. With \\( g_m = I_C / V_T \\), \\( r_\\pi = \\beta / g_m \\) and an unbypassed emitter resistance \\( R_E \\): Configuration Voltage gain Input resistance Output resistance Use Common emitter \\( -\\dfrac{g_m R_C}{1 + g_m R_E} \\) \\( r_\\pi + (\\beta + 1) R_E \\) \\( \\approx R_C \\) Voltage gain Common collector (follower) \\( \\approx 1 \\) \\( r_\\pi + (\\beta + 1) R_L \\), high \\( \\approx 1/g_m + R_s/(\\beta+1) \\), low Buffer, driving low-resistance loads Common base \\( g_m R_C \\) \\( \\approx 1/g_m \\), very low \\( \\approx R_C \\) High frequency, current input The unbypassed emitter resistor is the central tool of the design: it reduces the gain by the factor \\( 1 + g_m R_E \\), but in exchange it makes the gain depend on resistors and not on the transistor (\\( A_v \\approx -R_C / R_E \\) if \\( g_m R_E \\gg 1 \\)), raises the input resistance and, as you will see shortly, greatly reduces the distortion. It is local negative feedback."},{"t":"Large signal: where the distortion comes from","d":"The bipolar transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-amplificador#fuerte","x":"04 Large signal: where the distortion comes from With an input voltage \\( v_{be} = \\hat V \\sin \\omega t \\) at the base, the current is \\( I_C\\, e^{x \\sin \\omega t} \\) with \\( x = \\hat V / V_T \\). Expanding the exponential gives terms in \\( \\sin^2 \\), \\( \\sin^3 \\)…, which are the second and third harmonics. For small amplitudes, the second harmonic relative to the fundamental is \\[ HD_2 \\approx \\frac{x}{4} = \\frac{\\hat V}{4\\, V_T} \\] \\[ HD_2 \\approx \\frac{\\hat V}{4\\, V_T\\, (1 + g_m R_E)^2} \\quad \\text{with degeneration} \\] With a 1 mV peak at the base, 1 %; with 10 mV, almost 10 %. With \\( g_m R_E = 3.87 \\), the same input gives about 24 times less distortion; to obtain the same output you have to drive 4.87 times harder, and the net improvement is about 5 times. That is why a common emitter without degeneration is only “linear” for input signals of a few millivolts: \\( V_T = 25.85 \\) mV is the natural scale of the bipolar transistor. The exact expression uses Bessel functions, and the lab calculates it numerically, sample by sample. If the amplitude keeps growing, the collector voltage hits its limits: it cannot rise higher than the supply (the transistor cuts off) nor fall below about 0.2 V above the emitter (it saturates). The output is clipped, and the distortion jumps. Lab · the common emitter with small and large signals A common emitter powered from 12 V. The collector current is calculated with the full exponential for each instant (without linearizing), along with the"},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"The bipolar transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-amplificador#electronica","x":"05 Where it appears in electronics Case Which regime matters Microphone preamplifier Small signal: noise and gain; distortion barely appears with millivolts Output stage of an audio amplifier Large signal: clipping and distortion define the usable power Radio receiver with a strong interfering signal The nonlinearity mixes signals and generates intermodulation products Mixer and multiplier The exponential nonlinearity is used on purpose"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The bipolar transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-amplificador#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring the gain Build a common emitter with a 2N3904 at 1 mA and \\( R_C = 4.7 \\) kΩ, with the emitter bypassed. Measure the gain with 1 mV at the input and compare it with \\( g_m R_C \\). Activity 2 · Seeing the distortion Raise the input to 10 and 20 mV and observe the asymmetry of the output on the oscilloscope. With the oscilloscope's FFT function, measure the second harmonic and compare it with \\( \\hat V / 4 V_T \\). Activity 3 · The effect of degeneration Remove the bypass capacitor to leave 100 Ω of unbypassed emitter resistance. Adjust the input to obtain the same output as before and compare the distortion."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The bipolar transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-amplificador#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Applying the small-signal model at any amplitude. It holds for a few millivolts at the base. Biasing without room to swing. A quiescent collector near the supply clips right away on one half-cycle. Designing the gain with β. With degeneration, the resistors set it. Thinking that more gain is always better. More gain without degeneration means more distortion."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The bipolar transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-amplificador#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What is the gain of a common emitter at 2 mA with \\( R_C = 2.2 \\) kΩ and the emitter bypassed? \\( g_m R_C = (2/25.85) \\times 2200 \\approx 170 \\). What second harmonic is expected with a 4 mV peak at the base, without degeneration? \\( 4 / (4 \\times 25.85) \\approx 3.9 \\) %. What advantages does the unbypassed emitter resistor have? Gain set by resistors, higher input resistance and less distortion, at the cost of less gain. Which configuration suits driving a low-resistance load? The common collector, because of its low output resistance."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The bipolar transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-amplificador#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 7th ed., Oxford University Press, 2014. The small-signal bipolar configurations, with all the parameters derived. Paul R. Gray, Paul J. Hurst, Stephen H. Lewis and Robert G. Meyer. Analysis and Design of Analog Integrated Circuits. 5th ed., Wiley, 2009. The chapter on distortion, with the series expansion of the exponential and the effect of feedback. Douglas Self. Audio Power Amplifier Design. 6th ed., Focal Press, 2013. The distortion of bipolar stages measured in real amplifiers."},{"t":"Two junctions very close together","d":"Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-dispositivo#estructura","x":"01 Two junctions very close together An NPN bipolar transistor is two PN junctions back to back, but it is not the same as two diodes: its trick is that the middle base is much thinner than the diffusion length of the electrons. With the base-emitter junction forward biased, the emitter injects electrons into the base; almost none of them recombine, and on reaching the edge of the base-collector junction, which is reverse biased, the field of that junction sweeps them into the collector. A small base current controls a large collector current. The geometry and the doping levels are designed for that: the emitter heavily doped, so that the current of the base-emitter junction is carried almost entirely by electrons (in the PN junction the current is dominated by the less doped side), the base thin and lightly doped, and the collector lightly doped and wide, to withstand voltage. The Electronics Technician program page on bipolar transistors covers their use in circuits; here we look inside."},{"t":"Where the gain comes from","d":"Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-dispositivo#ganancia","x":"02 Where the gain comes from The emitter current has two components: electrons that go to the base, which are the useful ones, and holes that the base returns to the emitter, which are lost. Of the electrons, in addition, some recombine in the base. Two factors measure those losses: \\[ \\alpha = \\gamma\\, \\alpha_T \\qquad \\beta = \\frac{I_C}{I_B} = \\frac{\\alpha}{1 - \\alpha} \\] \\[ \\alpha_T \\approx 1 - \\frac{W_B^2}{2 L_B^2} \\qquad \\beta \\approx \\frac{D_B\\, N_E\\, W_E}{D_E\\, N_B\\, W_B} \\] \\( \\gamma \\) is the injection efficiency of the emitter; \\( \\alpha_T \\), the base transport factor. The expression for \\( \\beta \\) holds with emitter and base short compared with their diffusion lengths and no recombination in the base. With a 0.5 µm base and a diffusion length of 20 µm, \\( \\alpha_T = 0.9997 \\): the base alone would allow a \\( \\beta \\) of more than 3000. What limits the actual gain, 100 to 300 in a small-signal transistor, is the injection efficiency, which is why the emitter is doped a hundred times more than the base. Since \\( \\beta \\) comes from a ratio of doping levels and thicknesses, it varies a lot between transistors of the same type: the 2N3904 datasheet allows 100 to 300 at 10 mA. A well-designed circuit does not depend on the exact value of β."},{"t":"Operating modes and the Ebers–Moll model","d":"Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-dispositivo#modos","x":"03 Operating modes and the Ebers–Moll model Mode Base-emitter Base-collector Use Forward active Forward Reverse Amplification Saturation Forward Forward Closed switch Cutoff Reverse Reverse Open switch Reverse active Reverse Forward Almost never: \\( \\beta_R \\) is less than 1 The Ebers–Moll model describes all four modes with a single pair of equations. In its transport form, with \\( V_{BC} = V_{BE} - V_{CE} \\): \\[ I_C = I_S \\left( e^{V_{BE}/V_T} - e^{V_{BC}/V_T} \\right) - \\frac{I_S}{\\beta_R} \\left( e^{V_{BC}/V_T} - 1 \\right) \\] \\[ I_B = \\frac{I_S}{\\beta_F} \\left( e^{V_{BE}/V_T} - 1 \\right) + \\frac{I_S}{\\beta_R} \\left( e^{V_{BC}/V_T} - 1 \\right) \\] In forward active mode the \\( V_{BC} \\) term vanishes and what remains is \\( I_C = I_S e^{V_{BE}/V_T} = \\beta_F I_B \\). In saturation, the base-collector junction also injects and the collector current falls below \\( \\beta_F I_B \\). The Early effect completes the picture: as \\( V_{CE} \\) rises, the depletion region of the base-collector junction eats into the base, the effective base narrows and \\( I_C \\) grows. It is modeled with a factor \\( (1 + V_{CE}/V_A) \\), where the Early voltage \\( V_A \\) ranges from 50 to 150 V in small-signal transistors. Lab · output curves and load line The transistor has Ebers–Moll parameters of the order of a 2N3904 (\\( I_S = 6.7 \\) fA, \\( \\beta_F = 200 \\), \\( \\beta_R = 0.74 \\), \\( V_A = 74 \\) V). The curves are \\( I_C \\) versus \\( V_{CE} \\) for base currents from 20 to 100 µA. You choose the supply, t"},{"t":"Small-signal model","d":"Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-dispositivo#senal","x":"04 Small-signal model Around the operating point in forward active mode, the transistor is replaced by the hybrid-π model. All of its parameters come from the collector current: \\[ g_m = \\frac{I_C}{V_T} \\qquad r_\\pi = \\frac{\\beta}{g_m} \\qquad r_o = \\frac{V_A}{I_C} \\] \\[ f_T = \\frac{g_m}{2\\pi\\,(C_\\pi + C_\\mu)} \\] At 1 mA, \\( g_m = 38.7 \\) mS; with \\( \\beta = 200 \\), \\( r_\\pi = 5.2 \\) kΩ, and with \\( V_A = 74 \\) V, \\( r_o = 74 \\) kΩ. The transconductance depends only on the current and the temperature, not on the transistor: all bipolar transistors in the world have \\( g_m = 38.7 \\) mS at 1 mA. And the product \\( g_m r_o = V_A / V_T \\) is the intrinsic gain, the maximum voltage gain that a single transistor can give: with \\( V_A = 74 \\) V, about 2900 times, almost 70 dB. The transition frequency \\( f_T \\), where the current gain falls to 1, is 300 MHz in the 2N3904 at 10 mA."},{"t":"The transistor as a switch","d":"Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-dispositivo#conmutacion","x":"05 The transistor as a switch In saturation the transistor is a closed switch with a drop of 0.1 to 0.3 V: the 2N3904 guarantees \\( V_{CE(sat)} \\leq 0.2 \\) V with 10 mA of collector current and 1 mA of base current. To ensure saturation the base is overdriven, giving it considerably more current than \\( I_C / \\beta \\); the ratio \\( I_C / I_B \\) in saturation is called forced β, and it is designed to be between 10 and 20. The cost of overdriving is the stored charge: with both junctions forward biased, the base fills with excess minority carriers, and when the base current is cut off the transistor keeps conducting until that charge is removed. This is the storage time, which in the 2N3904 reaches 200 ns compared with a 35 ns rise time. It is fought with: A Schottky diode between base and collector (the “Baker clamp”): it diverts the excess base current and keeps the base-collector junction from becoming forward biased. It is the S in 74LS logic. A speed-up capacitor in parallel with the base resistor, which gives a current spike on turn-on and turn-off. Not saturating: ECL logic always works in the active region, and for decades it was the fastest family."},{"t":"The limits of the device","d":"Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-dispositivo#limites","x":"06 The limits of the device Limit What causes it \\( BV_{CEO} \\) lower than \\( BV_{CBO} \\) With the base open, the carriers of the incipient avalanche act as base current and the transistor amplifies them: in the 2N3904, 40 V versus 60 V Maximum power The junction temperature: \\( P = V_{CE} I_C \\) and the thermal resistance to ambient Second breakdown A hotter spot on the junction conducts more, heats up more, and the current concentrates until it melts it; that is why the safe operating area is cut back at high voltage β drop at high current High injection in the base (the Webster effect) and the expansion of the base into the collector (the Kirk effect)"},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-dispositivo#electronica","x":"07 Where it appears in electronics Case Why a bipolar Precision operational amplifiers Better \\( V_{BE} \\) matching than the MOS threshold matching, and less low-frequency noise Bandgap voltage references The sum of a \\( V_{BE} \\), which falls with temperature, and a \\( V_{BE} \\) difference, which rises, gives a stable 1.2 V Radio frequency (SiGe) Silicon-germanium transistors with \\( f_T \\) of hundreds of GHz Audio output stages Complementary NPN-PNP pairs in class AB Driving relays and loads A cheap switch controlled from a microcontroller"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-dispositivo#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Activity 1 · Plotting the output curves With a 2N3904, a source for the base through 100 kΩ and another for the collector, measure \\( I_C \\) versus \\( V_{CE} \\) for three base currents. Extend the lines of the active region until they cross the voltage axis and estimate \\( V_A \\). Activity 2 · Sixty millivolts per decade, again With \\( V_{CE} \\) fixed at 5 V, measure \\( V_{BE} \\) for collector currents from 10 µA to 10 mA and verify that each decade raises the voltage by about 60 mV. Calculate \\( g_m \\) at each point. Activity 3 · The storage time Drive the transistor as a switch with a square wave, first with a forced β of 10 and then of 50, and use the oscilloscope to measure the delay between the falling edge of the base and the rise of the collector. Add a Schottky diode between base and collector and repeat."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-dispositivo#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Thinking of the transistor as two diodes. Two diodes soldered together do not amplify: the base has to be thin. Designing with an exact value of β. It varies from 100 to 300 between samples. Assuming that in saturation \\( I_C = \\beta I_B \\). In saturation the current is set by the external circuit. Using \\( BV_{CBO} \\) as the maximum voltage. With the base open or at high resistance, the lower one applies, \\( BV_{CEO} \\). Forgetting the stored charge. A heavily saturated transistor is slow to turn off."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-dispositivo#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment Why is the emitter doped much more heavily than the base? So that the current of the base-emitter junction is carried almost entirely by the electrons injected into the base, which are the ones that reach the collector: the injection efficiency goes up and with it β. What is \\( g_m \\) at 2 mA? \\( 2 \\text{ mA} / 25.85 \\text{ mV} \\approx 77 \\) mS, whatever the transistor. With \\( \\alpha = 0.995 \\), what is β? \\( \\beta = 0.995 / 0.005 = 199 \\). What is forced β and why is it designed to be low? It is \\( I_C / I_B \\) with the transistor saturated. It is made much lower than the minimum β of the transistor to guarantee saturation with any sample. Why is \\( BV_{CEO} \\) lower than \\( BV_{CBO} \\)? Because with the base open the current of the incipient avalanche acts as base current and the transistor amplifies it, bringing the breakdown forward."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-dispositivo#ampliar","x":"11 Further reading Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 7th ed., Oxford University Press, 2014. The hybrid-π model and the use of the bipolar transistor in circuits, with just the right amount of physics. Donald A. Neamen. Semiconductor Physics and Devices. 4th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2012. Injection efficiency, the transport factor and the Ebers–Moll model derived from the physics. Paul R. Gray, Paul J. Hurst, Stephen H. Lewis and Robert G. Meyer. Analysis and Design of Analog Integrated Circuits. 5th ed., Wiley, 2009. The device models chapter is the reference for the Early effect, the capacitances and high-current effects."},{"t":"Partial derivatives: moving one variable at a time","d":"Differential calculus of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-diferencial-varias#parciales","x":"01 Partial derivatives: moving one variable at a time With several variables, “the” derivative is not enough: you have to say with respect to which one. The partial derivative is computed by holding the others fixed, as if they were constants: \\[ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{f (x + h, y) -f (x, y)}{h} \\] Geometrically: the surface is cut with the plane \\(y = \\text{const}\\) and the slope of the resulting curve is measured. What it means in the lab A partial derivative is a sensitivity: how much the output changes per unit change in one component, with the others held still. If \\(P = V^2/R\\), then \\(\\partial P/\\partial V = 2V/R\\) and \\(\\partial P/\\partial R = -V^2/R^2\\). Doubling the voltage quadruples the power; doubling the resistance cuts it in half. That, which we know by heart, is exactly what the two partials say. The second derivatives include the mixed ones, \\(\\partial^2 f/\\partial x\\,\\partial y\\). Schwarz’s theorem guarantees that, if they are continuous, the order does not matter: \\(\\partial^2 f/\\partial x\\,\\partial y = \\partial^2 f/\\partial y\\,\\partial x\\)."},{"t":"Tangent plane, differential and error propagation","d":"Differential calculus of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-diferencial-varias#diferencial","x":"02 Tangent plane, differential and error propagation Just as in one variable the tangent line approximated the curve, here the tangent plane approximates the surface: \\[ z = f (x_0, y_0) + f_x \\cdot (x -x_0) + f_y \\cdot (y -y_0) \\quad d f = f_x d x + f_y d y \\] The total differential \\(df\\) estimates how much \\(f\\) changes for small changes in the two variables. Worked example · uncertainty of a measurement \\(V = 10\\text{ V} \\pm 0.1\\text{ V}\\) and \\(R = 100\\ \\Omega \\pm 1\\ \\Omega\\) are measured, and \\(P = V^2/R = 1\\text{ W}\\) is computed. \\[ dP = \\frac{2V}{R}\\,dV + \\left(-\\frac{V^2}{R^2}\\right)dR = 0.2\\cdot(\\pm 0.1) + (-0.01)\\cdot(\\pm 1) \\] In the worst case the errors add up in absolute value: \\(|\\Delta P| \\le 0.02 + 0.01 = \\mathbf{0.03\\text{ W}}\\), that is, 3%. It is the same 3% given by the rule of thumb “relative error of \\(P\\) = twice that of \\(V\\) plus that of \\(R\\)”: \\(2\\cdot 1\\% + 1\\% = 3\\%\\). That rule comes from the total differential."},{"t":"The gradient","d":"Differential calculus of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-diferencial-varias#gradiente","x":"03 The gradient The two partial derivatives together form a vector that concentrates all the information about how f changes: \\[ \\nabla f = (\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}) \\quad D_{\\hat{u}} f = \\nabla f \\cdot \\hat{u} = \\left| \\nabla f \\right| \\cos \\theta \\] The directional derivative in the direction û is the projection of the gradient onto it: a dot product. The three properties you need to know 1. The gradient points in the direction of maximum increase, and its magnitude is that maximum slope. 2. It is perpendicular to the level curves. That is why the electric field, which is \\(-\\nabla V\\), crosses the equipotentials at right angles. 3. At a local extremum, the gradient is zero: it is the several-variable version of \\(f' = 0\\). Lab · gradient and directional derivative Tap the map to choose a point and rotate the direction arrow. The directional derivative is maximum when the direction coincides with the gradient. f = x² + y² f = x² − y² f = 3·e^(−x²−y²) f = 0.8x + 0.4y Potential of two opposite charges direction 30 °"},{"t":"The chain rule","d":"Differential calculus of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-diferencial-varias#cadena","x":"04 The chain rule If the variables in turn depend on another one —for example, on time— the contributions add up: \\[ \\frac{d f}{d t} = \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\frac{d x}{d t} + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\frac{d y}{d t} \\quad = \\; \\nabla f \\cdot {\\vec{r}}' (t) \\] This is what happens when a resistor changes value while the voltage also varies: the power changes for both reasons at once, and the chain rule adds them up, weighted by each sensitivity."},{"t":"Extrema: free and constrained","d":"Differential calculus of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-diferencial-varias#extremos","x":"05 Extrema: free and constrained The candidates for an extremum are the critical points, where \\(\\nabla f = 0\\). To classify them we use the Hessian, which is the matrix of second derivatives: \\[ H = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc} f_{x x} & f_{x y} \\\\ f_{x y} & f_{y y} \\end{array} \\right] \\quad D = \\det (H) \\] \\(D > 0\\) and \\(f_{xx} > 0\\): minimum. \\(D > 0\\) and \\(f_{xx} < 0\\): maximum. \\(D < 0\\): saddle point. \\(D = 0\\): the test is inconclusive. It is the same symmetric matrix as in quadratic forms, and the test is the one on the signs of its eigenvalues, written with determinants. Lagrange multipliers When the maximum is sought subject to a constraint —optimizing with a condition \\(g(x,y) = 0\\)—, at the optimum the gradients are parallel: \\(\\nabla f = \\lambda\\nabla g\\). You solve the system formed by that equality and the constraint. It is the method used to prove, for example, maximum power transfer with a dissipation constraint, or the minimum-cost design that meets a specification."},{"t":"What it is good for in electronics","d":"Differential calculus of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-diferencial-varias#electronica","x":"06 What it is good for in electronics Tolerances and sensitivity. \\(\\partial(\\text{output})/\\partial(\\text{component})\\) tells you which resistor is worth buying at 1% and which can be 5%. It is sensitivity analysis, and it is computed with partial derivatives. Field and potential. \\(\\vec{E} = -\\nabla V\\): the electric field is minus the gradient of the potential. All of the electrostatics of Physics II rests on this. Design optimization. Maximum gain with limited consumption, minimum noise for a given bandwidth: these are constrained extrema. Model fitting. Least-squares methods and the training of neural networks look for the minimum of a function of many variables by following the gradient downhill: this is gradient descent. Maximum power point. In a solar panel, the power depends on voltage and current; the MPPT tracker climbs the gradient up to the maximum."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Differential calculus of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-diferencial-varias#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Exercise 1 · Measured sensitivity For a resistive divider, compute \\(\\partial V_{\\text{out}}/\\partial R_1\\) and \\(\\partial V_{\\text{out}}/\\partial R_2\\). Then replace each resistor with another that differs by 5% and verify that the measured change matches the one predicted by the differential. Exercise 2 · Error propagation Measure the voltage and current of a load with their uncertainties and compute the power with its error, using the total differential. Compare with the error reported by a wattmeter. Exercise 3 · Following the gradient With the lab in section 3, choose a point and find by hand the direction of maximum increase; verify that it coincides with the gradient and that the directional derivative is zero in the perpendicular direction, which is that of the level curve."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Differential calculus of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-diferencial-varias#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Differentiating with respect to one variable without holding the others fixed. Believing that the existence of the partial derivatives implies continuity. In several variables it does not. Forgetting to normalize the direction when computing the directional derivative: û has to be a unit vector. Confusing a zero gradient with an extremum: it can also be a saddle point. Using the Hessian test when \\(D = 0\\), where it decides nothing. Adding errors with their signs when propagating uncertainties: in the worst case the absolute values are added."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Differential calculus of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-diferencial-varias#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment For \\(f(x,y) = x^2y + 3y\\), find the two partial derivatives. \\(f_x = 2xy\\) and \\(f_y = x^2 + 3\\). What direction does the gradient have relative to the level curves? It is perpendicular to them, and points toward increasing values. Classify the critical point of \\(f(x,y) = x^2 - y^2\\) at the origin. \\(f_{xx} = 2\\), \\(f_{yy} = -2\\), \\(f_{xy} = 0\\), so \\(D = -4 < 0\\): it is a saddle point. \\(I = 2\\text{ A} \\pm 2\\%\\) and \\(R = 10\\ \\Omega \\pm 1\\%\\) are measured. What is the error of \\(P = I^2R\\)? The relative error is \\(2\\cdot 2\\% + 1\\% = \\mathbf{5\\%}\\), on \\(P = 40\\text{ W}\\): \\(\\pm 2\\text{ W}\\). What condition does a constrained optimum satisfy, according to Lagrange? That the gradients are parallel: \\(\\nabla f = \\lambda\\nabla g\\), together with the constraint itself, \\(g = 0\\). Why is \\(\\vec{E} = -\\nabla V\\)? Because the field points toward where the potential decreases fastest, and its magnitude is that maximum variation per unit of length."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Differential calculus of several variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-diferencial-varias#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading James Stewart. Multivariable Calculus. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo de varias variables. Trascendentes tempranas”), Cengage Learning, 2012. Chapter 14 covers partial derivatives, gradient, tangent plane, extrema and Lagrange with many applications. Jerrold E. Marsden and Anthony J. Tromba. Vector Calculus. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo vectorial”), W. H. Freeman, 2003. Treats the gradient with the care it deserves and connects it to conservative fields. John R. Taylor. An Introduction to Error Analysis. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Introducción al análisis de errores”), University Science Books, 1997. Propagation of uncertainties, which is the most immediate application of the total differential in the lab."},{"t":"Undoing the derivative","d":"Integral calculus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-integral#primitiva","x":"01 Undoing the derivative Differentiating is easy and mechanical: there are rules for everything. The inverse operation —given the derivative, find the function— is called finding an antiderivative, and it is not mechanical: it is a craft. \\[ F (x) \\; \\text{is an antiderivative of }\\; f \\; \\iff \\; F' (x) = f (x) \\quad \\int f (x) d x = F (x) + C \\] The constant of integration is not decoration: since the derivative of a constant is zero, there are infinitely many antiderivatives, all parallel to one another. The fact that all the antiderivatives of a function differ only by a constant is a direct consequence of the mean value theorem: if two functions have the same derivative, their difference has zero derivative and is therefore constant. Verification is always available Every integral is checked by differentiating the result. If it does not give the integrand, it is wrong. It is the only branch of calculus where you can check your own answer in thirty seconds: it is worth doing it every time."},{"t":"Basic integrals","d":"Integral calculus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-integral#tabla","x":"02 Basic integrals The table of derivatives read backwards: \\( \\int f \\, dx \\) Result \\( \\int f \\, dx \\) Result \\( \\int x^n \\, dx \\quad (n \\ne -1) \\) \\( x^{n+1}/(n + 1) + C \\) \\( \\int \\operatorname{sin} x \\, dx \\) −\\( \\cos x + C \\) \\( \\int \\dfrac{dx}{x} \\) \\( \\ln |x| + C \\) \\( \\int \\cos x \\, dx \\) \\( \\sin x + C \\) \\( \\int e^x \\, dx \\) \\( e^x + C \\) \\( \\int \\sec^2 x \\, dx \\) \\( \\tan x + C \\) \\( \\int a^x \\, dx \\) \\( a^x/\\ln a + C \\) \\( \\int \\dfrac{dx}{1 + x^{2}} \\) \\( \\arctan x + C \\) The absolute value in the logarithm \\( \\int\\,dx/x = \\ln |x| + C \\), with the bars. Without them, the result would not hold for negative \\( x \\), where the function \\( 1/x \\) exists just the same. The integral is linear: the integral of a sum is the sum of the integrals and constants come out front. On the other hand, there is no product rule or quotient rule: that is where the methods begin."},{"t":"The three methods that solve almost everything","d":"Integral calculus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-integral#metodos","x":"03 The three methods that solve almost everything Substitution: the chain rule in reverse If the integrand contains a function and, as a factor, something resembling its derivative, it pays to call that function u: \\[ \\int f (g (x)) g' (x) d x = \\int f (u) d u \\quad \\operatorname{with} \\; u = g (x), \\; d u = g' (x) d x \\] By parts: the product rule in reverse \\[ \\int u \\, d v = u v -\\int v \\, d u \\] Choose as u the part that simplifies when differentiated —a polynomial, a logarithm— and as dv the part that is easy to integrate. Partial fractions: for quotients of polynomials A rational function is decomposed into fractions with first- or second-degree denominators, which are already in the table. It is the method that later solves the inverse Laplace transforms of any circuit. Lab · integrals step by step Each step appears when you ask for it. At the end, the numerical check differentiates the result and compares it with the integrand. Next step Show all Check by differentiating"},{"t":"Integrating in a circuit","d":"Integral calculus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-integral#electronica","x":"04 Integrating in a circuit Relation Differentiating Integrating Capacitor \\( i = C\\cdot \\dfrac{dv}{dt} \\) \\( v = \\dfrac{1}{C}\\cdot \\int i \\, dt + v(0) \\) Inductor \\( v = L\\cdot \\dfrac{di}{dt} \\) \\( i = \\dfrac{1}{L}\\cdot \\int v \\, dt + i(0) \\) Charge \\( i = \\dfrac{dq}{dt} \\) \\( q = \\int i \\, dt \\) Energy \\( p = \\dfrac{dW}{dt} \\) \\( W = \\int p \\, dt = \\int v\\cdot i \\, dt \\) The constants of integration in that right-hand column are the initial conditions: the voltage the capacitor already had or the current that was already flowing through the inductor. Mathematically they are “any constant”; physically, the state of the circuit at time zero. Worked example · capacitor with constant current If a 1 µF capacitor receives a constant current of 2 mA starting from 0 V: \\( v(t) = (1/C)\\int i\\,dt = (1/10^{-6})\\cdot (2\\cdot 10^{-3}\\cdot t) = 2000\\cdot t \\) volts, a ramp of 2 V per millisecond. This is the principle of the sawtooth generator and of the dual-slope ADC. The op amp integrator does this computation in real time: \\( v_o = -(1/RC)\\int v_i\\,dt \\). Its practical version appears in the operational amplifiers course."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Integral calculus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-integral#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Exercise 1 · Integrate and check Solve \\( \\int x\\cdot \\cos x\\,dx \\), \\( \\int x/(x^2 + 1)\\,dx \\) and \\( \\int\\,dx/(x^2 - 4) \\) by the appropriate method, and check all three by differentiating the result. Exercise 2 · The real integrator Build an op amp integrator \\( (R = 10\\ \\mathrm{k}\\Omega, C = 100\\text{ nF}) \\) and drive it with a 1 kHz square wave. Check that the output is triangular and measure its slope; compare it with \\( V/(RC) \\). Explain why a resistor in parallel with C is needed. Exercise 3 · Accumulated charge Charge a capacitor with constant current and measure the voltage ramp. Compute the accumulated charge as the area under the current curve and verify \\( q = C\\cdot V \\)."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Integral calculus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-integral#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Forgetting the + C. In a circuit, that means forgetting the initial condition. Inventing a product rule: \\( \\int f\\cdot g\\,dx \\) is not \\( \\int f\\,dx \\cdot \\int g\\,dx \\). Substituting without changing the dx to du: the substitution includes the differential. Choosing u and dv badly in integration by parts, and getting an integral harder than the original. Writing \\( \\ln x \\) without the absolute value. Applying \\( x^{n+1}/(n+1) \\) with \\( n = -1 \\), which gives a division by zero: that case is the logarithm."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Integral calculus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-integral#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment \\( \\int (3x^2 - 4x + 5)\\,dx \\). \\( x^3 - 2x^2 + 5x + C \\). Check: differentiating returns the integrand. ✔ \\( \\int e^{-2x}\\,dx \\). With \\( u = -2x \\): \\( -e^{-2x}/2 + C \\). \\( \\int x\\cdot e^x\\,dx \\). By parts, with \\( u = x \\) and \\( dv = e^x\\,dx \\): \\( x\\cdot e^x - e^x + C \\). What does the constant of integration represent in \\( v = (1/C)\\int i\\,dt + C_1 \\)? The initial voltage of the capacitor, v(0). \\( \\int \\sin (\\omega t)\\,dt \\). −\\( \\cos (\\omega t)/\\omega + C \\). The \\( 1/\\omega \\) is the factor from the chain rule in reverse. A 10 nF capacitor receives a constant 5 µA. How fast does its voltage rise? \\( dv/dt = i/C = 5\\cdot 10^{-6}/10^{-8} = \\) \\( 500\\text{ V/s} \\), or 0.5 V per millisecond."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Integral calculus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-integral#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading James Stewart. Single Variable Calculus: Early Transcendentals. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo de una variable. Trascendentes tempranas”), Cengage Learning, 2012. Chapters 5 and 7 cover antiderivatives and all the integration techniques, with a strategy for choosing among them. Murray R. Spiegel. Advanced Calculus (Schaum’s Outline Series). (Spanish edition, “Cálculo superior (serie Schaum)”), McGraw-Hill. Tables and hundreds of solved integrals: useful as a desk reference. Hebe Rabuffetti. Introducción al análisis matemático (Cálculo 1) (in Spanish). El Ateneo. Integration methods explained with the detail of each substitution."},{"t":"The computer does not calculate with real numbers","d":"Applied numerical computation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-numerico#flotante","x":"01 The computer does not calculate with real numbers A double stores 64 bits: one sign bit, 11 of exponent and 52 of mantissa. With that it represents about 16 significant decimal digits and nothing more. Everything else is rounded. \\[ x = (-1)^s \\cdot 1.m \\cdot 2^{e-1023} \\] The same IEEE 754 format studied in Computer Science I, now seen from its consequences. Three consequences that bite 0.1 + 0.2 does not give 0.3. It gives 0.30000000000000004, because 0.1 does not exist exactly in binary. Floats are never compared with ==: you compare \\( |a-b| \\lt \\varepsilon \\). Catastrophic cancellation. Subtracting two similar numbers destroys the significant digits: if each has 16 good digits and they agree in 15, the subtraction leaves just one. Order matters. Adding a million small numbers to a large one loses the small ones; adding from smallest to largest preserves more. The machine epsilon is the smallest number that, added to 1, gives something different from 1: about \\( 2.2 \\times 10^{-16} \\) in double precision. It sets the floor of the relative error of any calculation."},{"t":"Error, convergence and stopping criterion","d":"Applied numerical computation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-numerico#error","x":"02 Error, convergence and stopping criterion Type of error Where it comes from Round-off From the machine’s finite arithmetic Truncation From cutting off an infinite process: a series, a limit In the data From the measurement that went into the calculation \\[ \\varepsilon_{abs} = |x - \\tilde{x}| \\qquad \\varepsilon_{rel} = \\frac{|x - \\tilde{x}|}{|x|} \\] A method converges linearly if each step multiplies the error by a constant, and quadratically if it squares it: in that case the correct digits double with each iteration. Since the exact answer is almost never known, the practical stopping criterion combines three things: the change between iterations is small, the residual \\( |f(x)| \\) is small, and an iteration limit so as not to keep spinning if it does not converge. Lab · finding a root, step by step Choose the function and the method, and advance iteration by iteration watching the error fall. f(x) = x² − 2 · root at √2 f(x) = cos x − x f(x) = x³ − x − 2 f(x) = e^x − 3x Bisection Newton-Raphson Secant Iteration 0"},{"t":"Roots of an equation","d":"Applied numerical computation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-numerico#raices","x":"03 Roots of an equation Method Iteration Convergence Requires Bisection \\( x_{k+1} = \\dfrac{a_k + b_k}{2} \\) Linear, one bit per step A sign change Newton-Raphson \\( x_{k+1} = x_k - \\dfrac{f(x_k)}{f'(x_k)} \\) Quadratic The derivative Secant \\( x_{k+1} = x_k - f(x_k)\\dfrac{x_k - x_{k-1}}{f(x_k) - f(x_{k-1})} \\) Order 1.618 Two starting points Fixed point \\( x_{k+1} = g(x_k) \\) Linear, if \\( |g'| \\lt 1 \\) Rewriting the equation Bisection never fails if there is a sign change, but it is slow: each step gains one bit, so about 50 iterations are needed to exhaust a double. Newton flies —it doubles the correct digits at each step— but it can go anywhere if the derivative is small or the starting point is far away. In practice they are combined: bisection to get close, Newton to finish."},{"t":"The other three calculations that always show up","d":"Applied numerical computation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-numerico#otros","x":"04 The other three calculations that always show up Numerical integration \\[ \\int_a^b f \\approx h\\left(\\frac{f_0}{2} + f_1 + \\cdots + f_{n-1} + \\frac{f_n}{2}\\right) \\qquad \\int_a^b f \\approx \\frac{h}{3}\\left(f_0 + 4f_1 + 2f_2 + \\cdots + f_n\\right) \\] Trapezoidal rule, with error \\( O(h^2) \\), and Simpson’s rule, with error \\( O(h^4) \\): for the same work, Simpson is far more accurate. Least-squares fitting For a line \\( y = mx + b \\) that passes as close as possible to \\( n \\) measured points: \\[ m = \\frac{n\\sum x_i y_i - \\sum x_i \\sum y_i}{n\\sum x_i^2 - \\left(\\sum x_i\\right)^2} \\qquad b = \\frac{\\sum y_i - m \\sum x_i}{n} \\] It is the calculation behind every sensor calibration: pairs are measured and the line is fitted that converts converter counts into physical units. Systems of equations For a few nodes, Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting is enough —choosing as pivot the largest element of the column— which avoids dividing by small numbers. For large, sparse systems, such as those that come from a resistor network, the iterative Jacobi and Gauss-Seidel methods are the better choice."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Applied numerical computation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-numerico#electronica","x":"05 Where it appears in electronics Calibrating a sensor. Least squares on the measured pairs. Operating point of a nonlinear circuit. Solving for the intersection of the load line with the diode curve is exactly the problem of this page. Simulation. SPICE solves a nonlinear system at each step with Newton-Raphson, and advances in time by integrating numerically. Digital filtering. Moving averages, median filters and accumulators, taking care not to lose precision through the order of the sums. Microcontrollers without a floating-point unit. There you work in fixed point: faster, but the scale has to be managed by hand."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Applied numerical computation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-numerico#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Exercise 1 · Seeing the rounding Print 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3, and then the value with twenty decimals. Calculate the machine epsilon with a loop that divides by two until 1 + e == 1. Exercise 2 · Comparing methods Solve \\( x^2 = 2 \\) by bisection and by Newton, tabulating the error at each iteration. Plot the logarithm of the error: bisection gives a straight line and Newton, a curve that plunges. Exercise 3 · Calibrating for real With a temperature sensor and a reference thermometer, measure ten pairs of values, fit the line by least squares and program that conversion into the microcontroller. Compare the error before and after calibrating."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Applied numerical computation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-numerico#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Comparing floats with ==. Subtracting similar quantities without realizing that digits are lost. Iterating without a limit and ending up in an infinite loop when the method does not converge. Using Newton with an arbitrary starting point and ending up at another root, or diverging. Confusing precision with accuracy: printing fifteen decimals of a quantity measured with two significant figures. Not checking the result by substituting it into the original equation."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Applied numerical computation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-numerico#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Why does 0.1 + 0.2 not give exactly 0.3? Because 0.1 and 0.2 have no finite binary representation: they are stored rounded and the error shows up when adding. How many bisection iterations are needed to gain three decimals? Each iteration divides the interval by two, so it gains one bit. For a factor of 1000 about 10 iterations are needed, because \\( 2^{10} = 1024 \\). What does it mean that Newton converges quadratically? That the new error is proportional to the square of the previous one: the correct digits double at each step. From 2 to 4, from 4 to 8, from 8 to 16. When does Newton fail? When the derivative is small or zero near the point, when the starting point is far away, or when the function oscillates: it can diverge or jump to another root. What error does Simpson’s rule have compared with the trapezoidal rule? \\( O(h^4) \\) versus \\( O(h^2) \\): when the step is halved, Simpson’s error falls by a factor of 16 and the trapezoidal rule’s by 4. What is partial pivoting for in Gaussian elimination? To avoid dividing by a very small number, which would amplify the round-off error. The largest element of the column is chosen as the pivot."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Applied numerical computation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-numerico#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Richard L. Burden and J. Douglas Faires. Numerical Analysis. 10th ed. (Spanish edition, “Análisis numérico”), Cengage Learning, 2016. The standard text for the subject, with algorithms and error analysis. Steven C. Chapra and Raymond P. Canale. Numerical Methods for Engineers. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Métodos numéricos para ingenieros”), McGraw-Hill, 2015. Strongly application-oriented, with code and engineering cases. William H. Press et al. Numerical Recipes. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2007. Real implementations, with honest comments on when each method fails. David Goldberg. What Every Computer Scientist Should Know About Floating-Point Arithmetic. ACM Computing Surveys, 1991. The article to read once in your life on floating point."},{"t":"When power is “good quality”","d":"Power quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-energia#que-es","x":"01 When power is “good quality” The ideal grid delivers a perfect sinusoidal voltage, of constant amplitude and frequency and balanced three-phase. Power quality measures how far the real one departs from that ideal, and how those deviations affect equipment. The responsibility is shared: the utility answers for the voltage it delivers, and users, for the disturbances their loads inject. Regulators set quality limits for the technical product —the voltage and its disturbances— and for the technical service —the number and duration of outages—."},{"t":"The disturbances","d":"Power quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-energia#perturbaciones","x":"02 The disturbances Disturbance What it is Typical causes Effects Voltage sag A drop of 10 to 90% lasting milliseconds to seconds Short circuits on the grid, starting of large motors Restart of computers and drives, production stoppages Transient overvoltage Spikes of microseconds to milliseconds Lightning, capacitor switching Breakdown of insulation and semiconductors Interruption Voltage almost zero Faults and protection operation Loss of service Harmonics Components with a frequency that is a multiple of 50 Hz Rectifiers, switching power supplies, drives, LED lighting Heating, neutral overload, resonances Flicker Slow amplitude fluctuations Arc furnaces, welders Visible flickering of lighting Imbalance Phases of different amplitude or phase shift Poorly distributed single-phase loads Heating of three-phase motors The reference standards are IEC 61000-4-30, which defines how to measure each parameter, and EN 50160, which sets the characteristics of the voltage of the public grid. For the harmonics injected by users, IEEE 519 is also used."},{"t":"Harmonics","d":"Power quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-energia#armonicas","x":"03 Harmonics A nonlinear load draws a non-sinusoidal current even if the voltage is sinusoidal. The most common case is the rectifier with a filter capacitor, at the input of almost every electronic power supply: the capacitor charges only near the peak of the voltage, and the current arrives in narrow pulses. That current is decomposed into a Fourier series with odd harmonics, and its content is summarized in the total harmonic distortion: \\[ THD = \\frac{\\sqrt{I_3^2 + I_5^2 + I_7^2 + \\cdots}}{I_1} \\] \\[ I_{rms} = I_1\\sqrt{1 + THD^2} \\] With the RMS values of each harmonic. A switching power supply without correction has a current THD of 80 to 150%: its RMS current is 1.28 to 1.80 times that of its fundamental. The harmonics that are multiples of three (3rd, 9th, 15th…) of the three phases are in phase with each other, so in the neutral of a three-phase system they do not cancel: they add. An office building full of computers can have more current in the neutral than in the phases."},{"t":"The power factor with harmonics","d":"Power quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-energia#factor","x":"04 The power factor with harmonics With sinusoidal voltage, only the fundamental of the current produces active power. Harmonics increase the RMS current and the apparent power without contributing useful power. The true power factor has two factors: \\[ PF = \\frac{P}{S} = \\cos\\varphi_1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + THD^2}} \\] The displacement factor \\( \\cos\\varphi_1 \\), due to the phase shift of the fundamental, and the distortion factor, due to the harmonics. A capacitor corrects the first, as seen in sizing, but not the second, and can make it worse by resonating with the grid inductance. The solution is in the power supply: active power factor correction (PFC), a boost converter that forces the input current to follow the shape of the voltage. The harmonic emission limits of IEC 61000-3-2 make it necessary in practice in equipment above 75 W. In existing installations, tuned passive filters or active filters that inject the opposite harmonic current are used. Lab · harmonics, power factor and neutral A current with a fundamental in phase with the voltage and 3rd, 5th and 7th harmonics, with the signs of a rectifier with a capacitor (pulses at the voltage peak). At the top, one period of the voltage (gray) and the current; below, its spectrum. The same load is assumed on the three phases of a system with neutral. 3rd harmonic = 5th harmonic = 7th harmonic ="},{"t":"How it is measured","d":"Power quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-energia#medicion","x":"05 How it is measured Power quality analyzers record for days the voltage and current of each phase and calculate with standardized methods the RMS values, the harmonics up to the 50th, sags, flicker and imbalance. Class A instruments, according to IEC 61000-4-30, give results that are comparable between instruments and can be used for complaints. All of that depends on measuring true RMS values, as seen in non-sinusoidal signals: an average-responding clamp greatly underestimates the current of a switching power supply."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Power quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-energia#laboratorio","x":"06 In the laboratory Activity 1 · The current of a power supply With a power analyzer, measure the input current of a charger without PFC and of a computer power supply with PFC: waveform, THD, power factor and crest factor. Activity 2 · The neutral Feed three identical nonlinear loads, one per phase of a three-phase system with neutral, and measure the phase and neutral currents. Activity 3 · A week-long recording Install a recorder in a laboratory’s panel and analyze the sags and voltage harmonics recorded."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Power quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-energia#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Correcting a distorting load with capacitors. It does not improve the power factor and can cause resonance. Sizing the neutral thinner than the phases. With electronic loads it can carry more current. Measuring the current with an average-responding instrument. With narrow pulses it reads far less. Blaming the grid for what the installation itself injects. Voltage harmonics often come from the loads of the same building."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Power quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-energia#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What THD does a current with 30% 3rd and 40% 5th have? \\( \\sqrt{0.09 + 0.16} = 0.5 \\): 50%. What power factor does that load have if its fundamental is in phase with the voltage? \\( 1/\\sqrt{1.25} \\approx 0.89 \\). Three identical loads each have 5 A of 3rd harmonic. How much flows in the neutral? 15 A at 150 Hz. Why does a three-phase drive without a neutral not load the neutral with third harmonics? Because without a connection to the neutral the zero-sequence currents, such as the triplens, have nowhere to flow: a six-pulse three-phase rectifier does not produce a 3rd harmonic."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Power quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-energia#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Roger C. Dugan, Mark F. McGranaghan, Surya Santoso and H. Wayne Beaty. Electrical Power Systems Quality. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 2012. The reference on disturbances, their measurement and their mitigation. Math H. J. Bollen. Understanding Power Quality Problems: Voltage Sags and Interruptions. IEEE Press, 2000. Voltage sags and their effects on equipment, in depth. Francisco C. De La Rosa. Harmonics and Power Systems. CRC Press, 2006. Harmonics in power systems, their sources, effects and filters."},{"t":"What quality is","d":"Quality control and total quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-total#calidad","x":"01 What quality is For Juran, quality is fitness for use; for Crosby, conformance to requirements. The two views complement each other: a product has to meet its specifications and, in addition, the specifications have to reflect what the customer needs. The way of ensuring it changed over time: at first the finished product was inspected to separate the bad; then the process was statistically controlled so that it would not produce the bad; today quality is managed throughout the organization."},{"t":"Inspection and acceptance sampling","d":"Quality control and total quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-total#muestreo","x":"02 Inspection and acceptance sampling Inspecting every unit is expensive and, if the test is destructive, impossible. In acceptance sampling a sample of \\( n \\) units is inspected from each lot and the lot is accepted if \\( c \\) or fewer defective units appear. The probability of accepting a lot with a defective fraction \\( p \\) is the operating characteristic curve of the plan: \\[ P_a(p) = \\sum_{k=0}^{c} \\binom{n}{k}\\, p^k (1-p)^{n-k} \\] The producer’s risk \\( \\alpha \\) is rejecting a good lot, one with the acceptable quality level (AQL); the consumer’s risk \\( \\beta \\), accepting a bad one, with the limiting quality. The tables of the ISO 2859-1 standard give plans according to lot size and AQL. Lab · operating characteristic curve of a sampling plan The probability of accepting the lot as a function of its defective fraction. An acceptable quality of 1 % defective is taken (green line) and a limiting quality of 5 % (red line). Sample size n = Acceptance number c = Lot quality p ="},{"t":"Statistical process control","d":"Quality control and total quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-total#proceso","x":"03 Statistical process control Acceptance sampling improves nothing: it only separates. Statistical process control, which Shewhart invented in the 1920s, monitors the process while it produces by means of control charts, with limits at three standard deviations from the mean: a point outside, or a suspicious run, indicates an assignable cause that has to be found and eliminated. The capability of the process against the tolerances is measured with the C p and C pk indices, which were covered in measurement automation. Ishikawa popularized the seven basic tools: check sheet, histogram, Pareto chart, cause-and-effect diagram, scatter diagram, stratification and control chart."},{"t":"Total quality","d":"Quality control and total quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-total#total","x":"04 Total quality Total quality management extends responsibility for quality to the whole organization, with customer focus, data-based decisions and continuous improvement according to the PDCA cycle —plan, do, check, act— popularized by Deming. The costs of quality are classified into prevention and appraisal, which are chosen, and internal and external failures, which are suffered: investing in the former usually reduces the latter far more. Six Sigma aims at processes so capable that they produce about 3.4 defects per million opportunities. The ISO 9001 standard sets out the requirements of a quality management system that an external body can certify."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Quality control and total quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-total#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Defect Pareto Classify the faults found when testing the boards assembled in the course and build the Pareto chart. Activity 2 · Cause and effect Build the Ishikawa diagram of the most frequent defect with the categories of method, machine, material, manpower, measurement and environment. Activity 3 · Sampling plan Look up in a sampling table the plan for a lot of 2000 resistors with an AQL of 1 % and compare its curve with the lab."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Quality control and total quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-total#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing that inspecting improves quality. It only separates; quality is built into the process. Expecting a sampling plan to be infallible. There is always a risk of accepting bad lots and rejecting good ones. Confusing control limits with tolerances. The former are set by the process; the latter, by the customer. Taking ISO 9001 certification as a guarantee of a good product. It certifies a management system, not each product."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Quality control and total quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-total#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment With \\(n = 50\\) and \\(c = 1\\), what is the probability of accepting a lot with 2 % defective? 73.6 %. What is the producer’s risk? The probability of rejecting a lot that has the acceptable quality. What happens to the curve when n is increased while keeping the ratio \\(c/n\\)? It becomes steeper: it discriminates better between good and bad lots. What do the letters of the PDCA cycle stand for? Plan, do, check and act."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Quality control and total quality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-total#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Douglas C. Montgomery. Introduction to Statistical Quality Control. Wiley. (Spanish edition: “Control estadístico de la calidad”, Limusa Wiley.) Control charts, capability and acceptance sampling. Kaoru Ishikawa. What Is Total Quality Control? The Japanese Way. Prentice-Hall, 1985. (Spanish edition: “¿Qué es el control total de calidad? La modalidad japonesa”, Norma.) Total quality told by one of its creators. W. Edwards Deming. Out of the Crisis. MIT Press, 1986. (Spanish edition: “Calidad, productividad y competitividad: la salida de la crisis”, Díaz de Santos.) Deming’s fourteen points and his critique of inspection."},{"t":"Temperature is not heat","d":"Heat and temperature","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calor-temperatura#temperatura","x":"01 Temperature is not heat They are two different things, and they get confused all the time. Temperature measures the mean kinetic energy of the particles: it is a property of the state. Heat is energy in transit between two bodies at different temperatures: it exists while it flows and afterwards it ceases to exist. A body does not “have” heat, just as a capacitor does not have current. It has internal energy, and heat is the way that energy enters or leaves. The Zeroth Law If A is in thermal equilibrium with C, and B is too, then A and B are in equilibrium with each other. It looks obvious, and it is what makes measurement possible: the thermometer is that C. Without this property, two bodies that read the same on a thermometer would not necessarily be at the same temperature, and measuring would mean nothing. Scale Zero at Conversion Where it is used Celsius Melting of water — Datasheets, everyday use Kelvin Absolute zero \\( T_K = T_C + 273.15 \\) Every physical formula: gases, Arrhenius, thermal noise Fahrenheit Historical convention \\( T_F = 1.8\\,T_C + 32 \\) US datasheets The second row is not a whim. In \\( PV = nRT \\), in \\( e^{-E_a/kT} \\) and in the thermal noise \\( \\overline{v^2} = 4kTRB \\), temperature enters in kelvin. Using Celsius there does not give a scale error: it gives nonsense."},{"t":"How much energy it takes","d":"Heat and temperature","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calor-temperatura#calor","x":"02 How much energy it takes To raise the temperature of a body you have to give it energy, and how much depends on its mass and on what it is made of: \\[ Q = m\\,c\\,\\Delta T \\qquad\\qquad C = m\\,c \\] \\( c \\) is the specific heat —energy per kilogram and per degree— and \\( C \\) the heat capacity of the whole body. In a change of state the temperature does not move and the energy goes into latent heat: \\( Q = m L \\). Material \\( c \\) in J/kg·K \\( \\alpha \\) in 10⁻⁶/K Why it matters Water 4186 — By far the best coolant there is Aluminum 897 23 The typical heat sink: light and cheap Copper 385 17 Better conductor, more expensive and heavier Silicon 705 2.6 Expands ten times less than aluminum FR-4 epoxy glass 1100 14 to 18 The laminate of circuit boards The third column is the thermal expansion, and there lies one of the central problems of packaging: a silicon chip expands 2.6 parts per million per degree and the FR-4 board it is soldered to expands six times more. Every power-on and power-off cycle stretches and compresses the solder joints, and after thousands of cycles they crack. That is thermomechanical fatigue, and it is the cause of failure of a great many devices that “just stopped working on their own”. \\[ \\Delta L = L_0\\,\\alpha\\,\\Delta T \\]"},{"t":"The four temperature sensors","d":"Heat and temperature","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calor-temperatura#sensores","x":"03 The four temperature sensors Measuring temperature, in practice, means converting it into voltage or resistance. There are four usual ways, and none is the best at everything: each one wins in one range and loses in another. Sensor What it delivers Range Its problem Thermocouple type K About 41 µV/°C −200 to 1300 °C Tiny signal, and it needs cold-junction compensation Pt100 0.385 Ω/°C −200 to 850 °C It has to be excited, and the current heats it NTC thermistor Exponential drop −40 to 150 °C Very sensitive but not at all linear Semiconductor junction −2 mV/°C −55 to 150 °C Small range, but it is integrated on the same chip The last row is the one that explains why almost every microcontroller has a temperature sensor inside: the forward voltage of a junction drops about 2 mV per degree, and that was already there. The most linear is the Pt100, the most sensitive is the NTC, and the one with the widest reach is the thermocouple. Lab · the four sensors You move the temperature and each sensor responds with its own law: the Pt100 with Callendar–Van Dusen, the NTC with its B equation, the thermocouple and the junction with their slope. See which one is straight and which is not. Temperature = 25 °C Overlay the normalized curves The cold junction A thermocouple does not measure temperature: it measures the difference between its tip and the point where it connects to the instrument. That second point is the cold junction, and it has to be measured separately with another sensor "},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Heat and temperature","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calor-temperatura#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Curve of an NTC Measure the resistance of a thermistor between 0 and 80 °C with a water bath and a reference thermometer. Linearize with \\( \\ln R \\) versus \\( 1/T \\), obtain the constant \\( B \\) and compare it with the one in the datasheet. Activity 2 · Specific heat by mixing Determine the specific heat of an aluminum piece with an insulated-cup calorimeter, measuring the equilibrium temperature when it is dropped hot into cold water. Compare with the tabulated 897 J/kg·K and discuss the losses. Activity 3 · The junction as a thermometer Measure the forward voltage of a diode at constant current while heating it with air. Verify the slope of −2 mV/°C and estimate what error a 10% variation in the bias current introduces."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Heat and temperature","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calor-temperatura#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Saying that a body “has” heat. It has internal energy; heat is energy in transit. Using Celsius in physical formulas. In \\( PV=nRT \\), in Arrhenius and in thermal noise, it has to be kelvin. Confusing specific heat with heat capacity. One is per kilogram; the other is for the whole body. Forgetting latent heat. During a change of state energy is supplied and the temperature does not move. Ignoring cold-junction compensation. Without it a thermocouple measures differences, not temperatures. Assuming every sensor is linear. Only the Pt100 comes close; the NTC is not even remotely so."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Heat and temperature","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calor-temperatura#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What is the difference between heat and temperature? Temperature is a property of the state; heat is energy in transit between bodies at different temperatures. What is the Zeroth Law for? It is what makes measurement possible: it guarantees that two bodies that read the same on a thermometer are in equilibrium with each other. What is the resistance of a Pt100 at 100 °C? 138.5 Ω, according to Callendar–Van Dusen. At 0 °C it is 100 Ω, hence the name. Why do solder joints crack with thermal cycling? Because silicon and FR-4 expand differently —2.6 versus 16 parts per million per degree— and each cycle stretches the joint. What does a thermocouple really measure? The temperature difference between its tip and the cold junction. That is why the instrument carries a second sensor inside. Why is water such a good coolant? Because of its specific heat: 4186 J/kg·K, more than four times that of aluminum. It absorbs a lot of energy with little change in temperature."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Heat and temperature","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calor-temperatura#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed., Cengage, 2019 (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”). The chapters on temperature, thermal expansion and the first law, with many worked problems. David Halliday, Robert Resnick and Jearl Walker. Fundamentals of Physics. 11th ed., Wiley, 2018 (Spanish edition, “Fundamentos de física”). The classic treatment of heat, specific heat and changes of state. Jacob Fraden. Handbook of Modern Sensors. 5th ed., Springer, 2016. The four sensors of this unit with their physics, their equations and their signal-conditioning circuits."},{"t":"From physics to the link","d":"Electromagnetic fields for links: Maxwell in practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-maxwell#de-fisica","x":"01 From physics to the link Maxwell's equations already appeared in Physics II, on the page on Maxwell's equations, as the synthesis of all electricity and magnetism. In Transmission Media they are used for a concrete purpose: carrying a signal from one point to another, through the air, along a cable, through a waveguide or along a fiber. Every medium in this course is a solution of the same four equations with different boundary conditions. Since communication signals are almost always sinusoidal, or sums of sinusoids, it is convenient to write the equations in phasor form, where the time derivative is replaced by \\( j\\omega \\): \\[ \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{E} = -j\\omega\\mu\\, \\mathbf{H} \\] \\[ \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{H} = \\mathbf{J} + j\\omega\\varepsilon\\, \\mathbf{E} \\] \\[ \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{D} = \\rho \\] \\[ \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{B} = 0 \\] With \\( \\mathbf{J} = \\sigma \\mathbf{E} \\) in a conducting medium, the second equation becomes \\( \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{H} = (\\sigma + j\\omega\\varepsilon)\\,\\mathbf{E} \\): the conduction current and the displacement current compete."},{"t":"Conductor or dielectric: it depends on the frequency","d":"Electromagnetic fields for links: Maxwell in practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-maxwell#medios","x":"02 Conductor or dielectric: it depends on the frequency The ratio between the conduction current and the displacement current, \\( \\sigma/\\omega\\varepsilon \\), decides how a material behaves. If it is much greater than 1, the material is a good conductor; if it is much less, a good dielectric. The same material can be both, depending on the frequency: Material \\( \\sigma \\) (S/m) \\( \\varepsilon_r \\) Behaves as a conductor up to Copper \\( 5.8 \\times 10^7 \\) 1 Optical frequencies Seawater 4 81 About 900 MHz Moist soil \\( 10^{-2} \\) 15 About 12 MHz Dry soil \\( 10^{-3} \\) 4 About 4.5 MHz FR-4 (circuit board) Negligible 4.4 It is a dielectric; it loses through \\( \\tan\\delta \\approx 0.02 \\) Seawater is the classic example: at low frequencies it is a conductor that does not let waves through —which is why submerged submarines communicate on very low frequencies and with enormous antennas—, and above a gigahertz it behaves as a lossy dielectric."},{"t":"Boundary conditions","d":"Electromagnetic fields for links: Maxwell in practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-maxwell#bordes","x":"03 Boundary conditions What distinguishes a waveguide from a line or an antenna is the surfaces that bound it. At the boundary between two media, Maxwell's equations impose: Component Between two dielectrics On a perfect conductor Tangential \\( \\mathbf{E} \\) Continuous Zero Tangential \\( \\mathbf{H} \\) Continuous (no surface current) Equal to the surface current Normal \\( \\mathbf{D} \\) Continuous (no surface charge) Equal to the surface charge Normal \\( \\mathbf{B} \\) Continuous Zero The condition \\( E_{\\text{tan}} = 0 \\) on the metal is the one that fixes the modes of a waveguide: only the fields that vanish at the walls survive."},{"t":"The skin effect","d":"Electromagnetic fields for links: Maxwell in practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-maxwell#pelicular","x":"04 The skin effect Inside a good conductor, an alternating field decays exponentially from the surface. The current concentrates in a layer of characteristic thickness \\( \\delta \\), the penetration depth (skin depth): \\[ \\delta = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\pi f \\mu \\sigma}} \\] \\[ R_s = \\frac{1}{\\sigma \\delta} \\] In copper, \\( \\delta \\) is 9.3 mm at 50 Hz, 66 µm at 1 MHz and 2.1 µm at 1 GHz. \\( R_s \\) is the surface resistance, in ohms per square. The consequences are everywhere: the resistance of a wire grows with frequency, Litz wire —many thin insulated strands twisted together— is used in radio-frequency coils and in switching power supplies, high-frequency conductors are silver-plated because only the surface matters, and a shield a few \\( \\delta \\) thick attenuates almost the entire field. Lab · penetration depth and AC resistance Penetration depth as a function of frequency for several materials, and the increase in the resistance of a round wire calculated with the exact solution (Kelvin functions). The horizontal line is the radius of the wire: when δ drops below it, the current stops using the center of the conductor. copper aluminum steel (μr = 100) seawater Frequency = Wire diameter ="},{"t":"Where the energy travels","d":"Electromagnetic fields for links: Maxwell in practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-maxwell#poynting","x":"05 Where the energy travels The power density carried by an electromagnetic field is the Poynting vector: \\[ \\mathbf{S} = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\, \\mathrm{Re}\\{ \\mathbf{E} \\times \\mathbf{H}^* \\} \\] In watts per square meter, time-averaged for sinusoidal fields. Applied to a coaxial cable, it gives a surprising result: the electric field runs from the center conductor to the shield and the magnetic field encircles the center conductor, so the power travels through the dielectric between the two, not through the copper. The conductors guide the energy and take the losses, but the energy is carried by the field. This is the idea that connects transmission lines with waveguides and antennas."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Electromagnetic fields for links: Maxwell in practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-maxwell#electronica","x":"06 Where it appears in electronics Case The phenomenon Inductor of a switching power supply at 200 kHz Skin and proximity effects: Litz wire is used Traces of a high-speed board Conductor losses through \\( R_s \\) and dielectric losses through \\( \\tan\\delta \\) Shielding of an instrument Thickness of several \\( \\delta \\) at the frequency of the interference Communication with submarines Very low frequencies because of the conductivity of seawater Microwave oven The mesh in the door reflects 2.45 GHz because its holes are much smaller than the wavelength"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Electromagnetic fields for links: Maxwell in practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-maxwell#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Resistance versus frequency With an LCR meter, measure the series resistance of a piece of 1 mm copper wire at 1 kHz, 10 kHz and 100 kHz, and compare it with the lab value. Repeat with Litz wire of the same cross-section. Activity 2 · A shield that works or does not With a coil driven by a generator and another one as a pickup, measure the signal with a 0.1 mm aluminum foil between the two at 1 kHz, 100 kHz and 1 MHz. Explain the results with the penetration depth. Activity 3 · Dielectric constant of a board Measure the capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor formed by the two copper faces of an FR-4 board and deduce its relative permittivity."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Electromagnetic fields for links: Maxwell in practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-maxwell#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Using the DC resistance at high frequency. The skin effect multiplies it. Classifying a material as a conductor or a dielectric without looking at the frequency. It is decided by \\( \\sigma/\\omega\\varepsilon \\). Thinking that the power travels through the copper. It travels through the field, between the conductors. Shielding against low-frequency magnetic fields with a thin foil. A thickness of several \\( \\delta \\) is needed, or a magnetic material."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Electromagnetic fields for links: Maxwell in practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-maxwell#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the penetration depth of copper at 100 kHz? \\( 66 \\text{ µm} \\times \\sqrt{10} \\approx 209 \\) µm. At what frequency does seawater stop behaving as a conductor? When \\( \\sigma = \\omega\\varepsilon \\): \\( f = 4/(2\\pi \\times 81 \\varepsilon_0) \\approx 890 \\) MHz. What condition does a perfect conductor impose on the electric field? That its tangential component be zero at the surface. Why are radio-frequency conductors silver-plated? Because the current flows in a surface layer of thickness δ, and it is enough for that layer to be a good conductor."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Electromagnetic fields for links: Maxwell in practice","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-maxwell#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading David K. Cheng. Field and Wave Electromagnetics. 2nd ed., Addison-Wesley, 1989. The classic text on fields and waves for engineering, with the skin effect and boundary conditions. Simon Ramo, John R. Whinnery and Theodore Van Duzer. Fields and Waves in Communication Electronics. 3rd ed., Wiley, 1994. Maxwell applied to the problems of communications electronics. Howard Johnson and Martin Graham. High-Speed Signal Propagation: Advanced Black Magic. Prentice Hall, 2003. The skin effect and dielectric losses in real boards and cables, with measurements."},{"t":"A vector at every point","d":"Vector fields, curl and divergence","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-vectoriales#que-es","x":"01 A vector at every point A vector field assigns a vector to every point in space. It is neither a curve nor a surface: it is a distribution. The wind on a map, the velocity of water in a river, the electric field around a charge and the magnetic field inside a transformer are vector fields. \\[ \\vec{F} (x, y, z) = P \\hat{i} + Q \\hat{j} + R \\hat{k} \\] Each component is a scalar function of three variables. That is why everything from the previous page still holds, component by component. The field lines are the curves tangent to the field at every point: they show “where something would go” if it were carried along by the field. They never cross —at a crossing there would be two directions— and their density indicates the intensity. The most important field for electronics comes from differentiating: if V is the potential, then \\(\\vec{E} = -\\nabla V\\). A field like this, which is the gradient of something, is called conservative, and it has special properties that are seen in section 4."},{"t":"Divergence: are there sources?","d":"Vector fields, curl and divergence","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-vectoriales#divergencia","x":"02 Divergence: are there sources? \\[ \\operatorname{div} \\, \\vec{F} = \\nabla \\cdot \\vec{F} = \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} + \\frac{\\partial R}{\\partial z} \\] It is a scalar. It measures how much “flows out” of the neighborhood of a point per unit volume. div F > 0: there is a source; the field originates there. A positive charge. div F < 0: there is a sink. A negative charge. \\(\\operatorname{div}\\vec{F} = 0\\): a solenoidal field. Whatever goes in, comes out. Two of Maxwell’s equations are divergences \\(\\nabla\\cdot\\vec{E} = \\rho/\\varepsilon_0\\): charges are the sources of the electric field. \\(\\nabla\\cdot\\vec{B} = 0\\): the magnetic field has no sources, that is, monopoles do not exist. The lines of B always close on themselves, and that is why a magnet cut in half gives two magnets."},{"t":"Curl: is there circulation?","d":"Vector fields, curl and divergence","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-vectoriales#rotacional","x":"03 Curl: is there circulation? \\[ \\operatorname{rot} \\, \\vec{F} = \\nabla \\times \\vec{F} = \\left| \\begin{array}{ccc} \\hat{i} & \\hat{j} & \\hat{k} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z} \\\\ P & Q & R \\end{array} \\right| \\] It is a vector. It measures the tendency of the field to make things rotate: if a small paddle wheel were released at that point, the curl tells you about which axis and how fast it would spin. In the plane, with \\(\\vec{F} = (P,\\ Q)\\), only the \\(z\\) component survives: \\(\\operatorname{curl}\\vec{F} = \\partial Q/\\partial x - \\partial P/\\partial y\\). A field with zero curl is called irrotational. Swirling is not the same as having curl A field can have circular lines and zero curl everywhere except at the origin —this is the case of the field around a current-carrying wire, which decays as 1/ρ—, and conversely, a field of straight, parallel lines can have curl if its intensity changes from one side to the other. What matters is not the shape of the lines but the local circulation. Lab · fields, divergence and curl Tap a point of the field and the divergence and the curl are computed there, with a small wheel that spins if there is circulation. Radial: F = (x, y) · point charge Rigid rotation: F = (−y, x) Field of a current-carrying wire: F = (−y, x)/ρ² Uniform: F = (1, 0.3) Shear: F = (y, 0) Source and sink Color by divergence"},{"t":"Conservative fields","d":"Vector fields, curl and divergence","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-vectoriales#conservativos","x":"04 Conservative fields A field is conservative if it comes from a potential: \\(\\vec{F} = \\nabla f\\). Three equivalent properties, in a simply connected region —one without holes—: A potential exists There is a function f with F = ∇f. Zero curl ∇ × F = 0 throughout the region. Path-independent work The integral between two points does not depend on the path, and around a closed path it equals zero. The electrostatic field is conservative, and that is why it makes sense to speak of potential and of voltage between two points without specifying which path. When there are time-varying magnetic fields it stops being so —\\(\\nabla\\times\\vec{E} = -\\partial\\vec{B}/\\partial t \\ne 0\\)— and then the “voltage” between two points depends on the path: that is what causes an induced EMF to appear in a loop, and also what makes it harder to measure with the oscilloscope near a transformer. The hole condition The field of a current-carrying wire has zero curl at every point except the origin, and yet its circulation around the wire is not zero: it equals \\(\\mu_0 I\\). There is no contradiction: the region has a hole, it is not simply connected, and the theorem does not apply there. This is exactly Ampère’s law."},{"t":"The nabla operator and its combinations","d":"Vector fields, curl and divergence","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-vectoriales#nabla","x":"05 The nabla operator and its combinations \\(\\nabla\\) behaves like a vector of derivatives, and by combining it with the three operations —product by a scalar, dot product and cross product— the three operators of vector calculus appear: Operation Input Output Meaning \\(\\nabla f\\) · gradient Scalar Vector Direction of maximum increase \\(\\nabla\\cdot\\vec{F}\\) · divergence Vector Scalar Sources and sinks \\(\\nabla\\times\\vec{F}\\) · curl Vector Vector Local circulation \\(\\nabla^2 f = \\nabla\\cdot\\nabla f\\) · Laplacian Scalar Scalar How far a point departs from the average of its neighborhood Two identities worth memorizing, because they simplify long calculations: \\(\\nabla\\cdot(\\nabla\\times\\vec{F}) = 0\\) —the divergence of a curl is always zero— and \\(\\nabla\\times(\\nabla f) = 0\\) —the curl of a gradient is too—. The first is the mathematical reason why there are no magnetic monopoles if B is written as the curl of a vector potential. The Laplacian appears in Laplace’s equation \\(\\nabla^2 V = 0\\), which governs the potential in a region without charges, and in the wave equation, which governs propagation in a transmission line and in free space."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Vector fields, curl and divergence","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-vectoriales#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Compute and verify For \\(\\vec{F} = (x^2y,\\ xy^2,\\ 0)\\), compute the divergence and the curl by hand and verify the results at two points with the lab in section 3, comparing with its numerical values. Activity 2 · Drawing field lines With iron filings and a magnet, photograph the lines of B and verify that they close. Discuss why that implies \\(\\nabla\\cdot\\vec{B} = 0\\). Activity 3 · Finding the potential Given \\(\\vec{F} = (2xy,\\ x^2 + 1)\\), verify that it is conservative and find the potential function by integrating. Check that its gradient returns the original field."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Vector fields, curl and divergence","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-vectoriales#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Confusing divergence with curl: one gives a scalar, the other a vector. Computing the curl without respecting the order of the determinant, and ending up with the sign flipped. Assuming that curved lines imply curl, or that straight lines imply zero curl. Applying “\\(\\operatorname{curl}\\vec{F} = 0 \\Rightarrow\\) conservative” in a region with holes. Forgetting that \\(\\nabla\\) is not an ordinary vector: it does not commute, because it differentiates whatever is to its right."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Vector fields, curl and divergence","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-vectoriales#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Compute the divergence of \\(\\vec{F} = (x,\\ y,\\ z)\\). \\(1 + 1 + 1 = \\mathbf{3}\\): there is a source everywhere; it is the field of a uniform expansion. Curl of \\(\\vec{F} = (-y,\\ x,\\ 0)\\). \\(\\partial Q/\\partial x - \\partial P/\\partial y = 1 - (-1) = 2\\), in the \\(\\hat{k}\\) direction: \\((0,\\ 0,\\ 2)\\). It is a rigid rotation. What does \\(\\nabla\\cdot\\vec{B} = 0\\) say? That magnetic monopoles do not exist: magnetic field lines close on themselves. Is \\(\\vec{F} = (y,\\ x)\\) conservative? \\(\\partial Q/\\partial x = 1\\) and \\(\\partial P/\\partial y = 1\\): the curl is zero, and the domain is the whole plane, so yes. Its potential is \\(f = xy\\). What is \\(\\nabla\\cdot(\\nabla\\times\\vec{F})\\)? Zero, always, for any field with continuous derivatives. Why does the electrostatic field allow us to speak of voltage between two points? Because it is conservative: the work does not depend on the path, so the potential difference is well defined."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Vector fields, curl and divergence","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-vectoriales#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Jerrold E. Marsden and Anthony J. Tromba. Vector Calculus. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo vectorial”), W. H. Freeman, 2003. The classic treatment of fields, divergence and curl with their physical interpretations. Murray R. Spiegel. Vector Analysis (Schaum’s Outline Series). 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Análisis vectorial”), McGraw-Hill, 2009. Hundreds of solved exercises with the nabla operator and its identities. Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Elements of Electromagnetics. Spanish edition, “Elementos de electromagnetismo,” 5th ed., Alfaomega, 2018. Explains vector calculus from the standpoint of electromagnetism: exactly the approach an electronics engineer needs."},{"t":"The mole: a very large dozen","d":"Notation and amount of substance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cantidad-sustancia#mol","x":"01 The mole: a very large dozen Atoms are so small that counting them one by one is useless. Chemistry uses a package of fixed size, just as the dozen is used for eggs, only vastly larger: \\[ 1\\ \\text{mol} = 6.022\\times10^{23}\\ \\text{entities} \\qquad (\\text{Avogadro's number},\\ N_A) \\] Since 2019 that number has been an exact definition, like the speed of light: the mole is defined as that quantity, and not the other way around. The beauty of the number is that it was chosen so that the mass of one mole, in grams, coincides with the atomic mass in the periodic table. One mole of silicon weighs 28.09 g; one mole of copper, 63.55 g. That turns a balance into an atom counter. \\[ n = \\frac{m}{M}, \\qquad N = n\\,N_A \\] With \\( m \\) the mass in grams and \\( M \\) the molar mass in g/mol. From the balance to the number of atoms, in two steps."},{"t":"Saying how much of something there is","d":"Notation and amount of substance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cantidad-sustancia#concentracion","x":"02 Saying how much of something there is Concentration units are chosen according to the order of magnitude of the problem. In solution chemistry, molarity is used; in materials, parts per million; in semiconductors, atoms per cubic centimeter. Unit Definition Used in Molarity, M moles of solute / liter of solution Laboratory solutions Molality, m moles of solute / kg of solvent Colligative properties % by mass g of solute / 100 g of solution Alloys, commercial reagents ppm, ppb Parts per million or per billion Purity of materials Atoms/cm³ How many there are in one cubic centimeter Doping of semiconductors The last two are the ones in this field, and it pays to know how to convert from one to the other. All that is needed is a single piece of data: how many silicon atoms there are per cubic centimeter, which comes from its density and its molar mass. \\[ N_{\\text{Si}} = \\frac{\\rho\\,N_A}{M} = \\frac{2.33\\ \\mathrm{g/cm^3} \\times 6.022\\times10^{23}\\ \\mathrm{mol^{-1}}} {28.09\\ \\mathrm{g/mol}} \\approx 5.0\\times10^{22}\\ \\mathrm{atoms/cm^3} \\] You need to have that number at hand: it is the reference against which any doping is measured."},{"t":"From the mole to the resistivity of a wafer","d":"Notation and amount of substance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cantidad-sustancia#lab","x":"03 From the mole to the resistivity of a wafer With the atomic density of silicon, a doping in atoms/cm³ translates immediately into parts per million. And since each dopant atom contributes one free carrier, that concentration determines the resistivity, which is what is finally measured with an ohmmeter. Lab · doping, parts per million and resistivity Move the dopant concentration and watch the three ways of saying the same thing, plus what the instrument reads. The scale is logarithmic because useful doping spans five orders of magnitude. phosphorus · donor, n-type boron · acceptor, p-type Doping = 1.0·10¹⁶ atoms/cm³"},{"t":"Formulas and nomenclature","d":"Notation and amount of substance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cantidad-sustancia#formulas","x":"04 Formulas and nomenclature The empirical formula gives the simplest ratio between the atoms; the molecular formula, the actual number per molecule. Hydrogen peroxide is HO as an empirical formula and H₂O₂ as a molecular formula. The compounds that show up all the time in electronics are worth having identified: Formula Name Where it appears SiO₂ Silicon dioxide The gate oxide of a MOSFET; glass Si₃N₄ Silicon nitride Passivation layer of integrated circuits Al₂O₃ Aluminum oxide, alumina Ceramic substrate, power insulators GaAs Gallium arsenide Microwave devices, infrared LEDs Ta₂O₅ Tantalum pentoxide The dielectric of a tantalum capacitor Fe₂O₃, MnZn Iron oxides, ferrites Cores of inductors and transformers C₃H₈O Isopropyl alcohol Cleaning circuit boards The calculation that shows up in any process To oxidize silicon and form the gate of a MOSFET, the reaction is \\( \\mathrm{Si} + \\mathrm{O_2} \\to \\mathrm{SiO_2} \\). For each mole of silicon consumed, one mole of oxide is formed, but the oxide takes up more volume: the thickness ratio is 0.44 to 1. That is, to grow 100 nm of oxide, 44 nm of silicon is consumed, and the surface ends up lower than where it started. That calculation, which is pure stoichiometry, defines the geometry of a transistor."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Notation and amount of substance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cantidad-sustancia#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Counting atoms with a balance Weigh a piece of copper and calculate how many atoms it has. Then estimate how many conduction electrons it contributes —one per atom— and compare with the carrier density listed in the tables, \\( 8.5\\times10^{22}\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-3}} \\). Activity 2 · From doping to measurement For an n-type silicon wafer of \\( 10^{15}\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-3}} \\), calculate the expected resistivity and the resistance of a piece 1 cm long with a cross section of 1 mm². Measure it with a four-point ohmmeter and compare. Activity 3 · Oxidation Calculate how many grams of silicon are consumed when a 500 nm layer of SiO₂ grows on a wafer 200 mm in diameter, using the densities and the molar masses. Verify the thickness ratio of 0.44."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Notation and amount of substance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cantidad-sustancia#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Confusing molar mass with atomic mass. They are the same number with different units: u per atom, g/mol per mole. Mixing up molarity and molality. One divides by the volume of solution and the other by the mass of solvent. Giving ppm without saying of what. It is not the same by mass as by number of atoms. Using liters where the problem is in cm³. In semiconductors everything is counted per cubic centimeter. Forgetting that the empirical formula does not identify the compound. Several different substances share the same one."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Notation and amount of substance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cantidad-sustancia#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment How many atoms are there in 28.09 g of silicon? One mole: \\( 6.022\\times10^{23} \\). The molar mass is chosen precisely for that. How many silicon atoms are there per cm³? About \\( 5.0\\times10^{22} \\), which comes from the density times Avogadro's number divided by the molar mass. How many ppm does a doping of \\( 5\\times10^{16}\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-3}} \\) correspond to? To one: \\( 5\\times10^{16}/5\\times10^{22} = 10^{-6} \\). What happens to the resistivity if the doping is doubled? It falls to about half: there are twice as many carriers. The proportion is not exact because the mobility also drops as the doping increases. How much silicon is consumed when 100 nm of oxide grows? 44 nm. The ratio is 0.44 and it comes from the densities and the molar masses. Empirical or molecular formula of hydrogen peroxide? Empirical HO, molecular H₂O₂. The empirical formula only gives the ratio."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Notation and amount of substance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cantidad-sustancia#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Raymond Chang and Kenneth A. Goldsby. Chemistry. 12th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química”), McGraw-Hill, 2017. Chapters 2 and 3: the mole, molar mass, formulas and concentration calculations. Ben G. Streetman and Sanjay Banerjee. Solid State Electronic Devices. 7th ed., Pearson, 2015. Doping, carrier density and resistivity, with the curves used by this lab. S. M. Sze and Kwok K. Ng. Physics of Semiconductor Devices. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2007. The tables of mobility versus doping and the numerical data for silicon, an essential reference."},{"t":"What capacitance is","d":"Capacitance and capacitors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/capacitores#capacidad","x":"01 What capacitance is Two conductors separated by an insulator store charge when a potential difference is applied to them. Capacitance measures how much charge they store per volt, and it depends only on the geometry and on the material in between: not on the voltage or on the charge. \\[ C = \\frac{Q}{V} \\qquad\\qquad C = \\varepsilon_0\\varepsilon_r\\frac{A}{d} \\ \\text{ for the parallel-plate capacitor} \\] A farad is enormous: a 1 F parallel-plate capacitor with air and a 1 mm gap would need plates about 10.6 kilometers on a side (113 km² of area). That is why we work in microfarads, nanofarads and picofarads. The formula gives the only three ways to increase capacitance: more area, less separation or a better dielectric. The whole history of capacitor technology is a tug-of-war with those three variables, and each family chose one. \\[ U = \\tfrac{1}{2}CV^2 = \\tfrac{1}{2}QV \\qquad\\qquad u = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\varepsilon E^2 \\] The stored energy, and the energy density per unit volume. The second form makes clear that the energy is in the field, not in the plates."},{"t":"The dielectric, and its limit","d":"Capacitance and capacitors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/capacitores#dielectricos","x":"02 The dielectric, and its limit When an insulator is placed between the plates, its molecules polarize and create an opposing field that weakens the original one. With the same charge there is less voltage, so the capacitance goes up by a factor of \\( \\varepsilon_r \\), the relative permittivity. Dielectric \\( \\varepsilon_r \\) Strength in kV/mm Where it is used Vacuum or air 1.0 3 Variable capacitors, transmission lines Polypropylene 2.2 650 Film capacitors, audio and power filters Impregnated paper 3.5 16 Motor-start capacitors Mica 5.4 120 Precision RF capacitors Silicon dioxide 3.9 1000 The gate oxide of every MOSFET Aluminum oxide 8.5 710 Electrolytic capacitors Barium titanate 1000 to 5000 10 High-capacitance ceramics, X7R and Y5V Dielectric strength is the maximum field the material withstands before it suddenly conducts. Exceeding it does not degrade the capacitor: it punctures it, and the failure is permanent. The last row shows the central compromise: barium titanate has a very high permittivity and a poor strength, and that is why high-capacitance ceramics are low-voltage. Lab · parallel-plate capacitor Build the capacitor by choosing the dielectric, area and separation, and apply a voltage to it. Capacitance, charge, energy and the internal field are calculated, and you are warned if the field exceeds the strength of the material. air · εr 1.0 · strength 3 kV/mm polypropylene · εr 2.2 · 650 kV/mm mica · εr 5.4 · 120 kV/mm silicon dioxide · εr 3.9 · 1000 kV/mm alumi"},{"t":"The capacitors you actually buy","d":"Capacitance and capacitors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/capacitores#reales","x":"03 The capacitors you actually buy The ideal capacitor of the textbooks does not exist. The real one has series resistance, series inductance and leakage, and its capacitance changes with temperature, with voltage and with time. Type Typical range Its virtue Its trap Ceramic C0G/NP0 1 pF to 10 nF Extremely stable, no drift Little capacitance per volume Ceramic X7R 1 nF to 10 µF High capacitance, small and cheap Loses capacitance with applied voltage Ceramic Y5V 10 nF to 100 µF Maximum capacitance per volume Up to −80% with temperature and more with voltage Aluminum electrolytic 1 µF to 100 mF High capacitance and cheap Polarized, high ESR, dries out over the years Tantalum 100 nF to 1 mF Stable and compact Fails short-circuited, sometimes with flames Polypropylene film 1 nF to 100 µF Very low losses, handles current Bulky The X7R that does not have what it says A 10 µF, 16 V X7R ceramic can end up at less than 3 µF when 10 V is applied to it, and that does not appear in the printed tolerance: it is the voltage coefficient, characteristic of barium titanate, whose permittivity drops as it polarizes. It is the cause of switching power supplies that oscillate and of filters that do not filter, and the error is invisible when measuring the capacitor with an instrument that tests it at one millivolt. The practical solution is to choose a rated voltage two or three times higher than the working voltage. And on the losses side there is the ESR, the equivalent series resistance, whic"},{"t":"Combinations and the transient","d":"Capacitance and capacitors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/capacitores#asociacion","x":"04 Combinations and the transient \\[ C_{\\parallel} = C_1 + C_2 + \\ldots \\qquad\\qquad \\frac{1}{C_{series}} = \\frac{1}{C_1} + \\frac{1}{C_2} + \\ldots \\] The opposite of resistors, and for the same reason: in parallel the areas add, and in series the separations add. And charging it through a resistor produces the transient that governs half of a digital circuit: \\[ v(t) = V\\left(1 - e^{-t/RC}\\right) \\qquad \\tau = RC \\] In one time constant it reaches 63%, and in five 99.3%. That \\( RC \\) is what limits the speed of a digital line: the capacitance of the trace and the output resistance form this very circuit."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Capacitance and capacitors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/capacitores#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring the voltage coefficient Measure the capacitance of a 10 µF X7R ceramic with an LCR meter and then in a circuit with increasing DC bias voltage, obtaining the capacitance from the cutoff frequency of an RC filter. Plot capacitance against applied voltage. Activity 2 · The time constant Charge a known capacitor through different resistors and record the curve with the oscilloscope. Measure \\( \\tau \\) at 63% and compare with \\( RC \\). Estimate the error introduced by the input impedance of the probe. Activity 3 · ESR and temperature Measure the ESR of new electrolytics and of others salvaged from old equipment. Relate it to the condition of the equipment and estimate the heating with a ripple current of 1 A rms."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Capacitance and capacitors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/capacitores#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing capacitance depends on the applied voltage. In the model it does not; in a real ceramic, it does a lot. Adding capacitances in series. In series the reciprocals add, the opposite of resistors. Using \\( U = QV \\) without the medium. The stored energy is \\( \\tfrac{1}{2}QV \\). Reversing an electrolytic. It is polarized, and backwards it is destroyed, often noisily. Ignoring the ESR. It is what heats the capacitor and what ends up killing it. Choosing the rated voltage with no margin. Two or three times the working voltage is advisable, especially with ceramics."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Capacitance and capacitors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/capacitores#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What does the capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor depend on? On the area, the separation and the permittivity of the dielectric. Not on the voltage or the charge. What happens if the dielectric strength is exceeded? The material suddenly conducts and the capacitor is punctured. The failure is permanent. Why can a 10 µF X7R end up with 3 µF? Because of the voltage coefficient of barium titanate: its permittivity drops as it polarizes. How are capacitances combined in parallel? They add, because it is equivalent to adding areas. What fraction of the final voltage does an RC reach in one time constant? 63%. In five time constants it reaches 99.3%. What is ESR and why does it matter? The equivalent series resistance. With the ripple current it dissipates \\( I^2R \\) and heats the capacitor from the inside, which accelerates its degradation."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Capacitance and capacitors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/capacitores#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed., Cengage, 2019 (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”). Capacitance, dielectrics, stored energy and combinations, with the classic treatment. Edward M. Purcell and David J. Morin. Electricity and Magnetism. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2016 (Spanish edition, “Electricidad y magnetismo”). The energy of the electric field and the polarization of a dielectric, from first principles. Paul Horowitz and Winfield Hill. The Art of Electronics. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2015. The appendix on passive components: which capacitor to use in each application and what the traps of each family are."},{"t":"Two paths, one condition","d":"The Cauchy-Riemann equations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cauchy-riemann#deduccion","x":"01 Two paths, one condition In the previous topic it became clear that the complex derivative requires the difference quotient to give the same result from every direction. It is enough to impose that two directions agree to obtain a condition on the real and imaginary parts. Let us write \\( f(z) = u(x,y) + j\\,v(x,y) \\) and approach first along the real axis, with \\( \\Delta z = \\Delta x \\): \\[ f'(z_0) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{\\Delta u + j\\,\\Delta v}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} + j\\,\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} \\] And now along the imaginary axis, with \\( \\Delta z = j\\,\\Delta y \\). Dividing by \\( j \\) is the same as multiplying by \\( -j \\): \\[ f'(z_0) = \\lim_{\\Delta y \\to 0} \\frac{\\Delta u + j\\,\\Delta v}{j\\,\\Delta y} = \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} - j\\,\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} \\] The two expressions have to be the same complex number. Equating real part with real part and imaginary part with imaginary part gives the Cauchy-Riemann equations."},{"t":"The equations","d":"The Cauchy-Riemann equations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cauchy-riemann#ecuaciones","x":"02 The equations \\[ \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}, \\qquad\\qquad \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = -\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} \\] Two partial differential equations that tie the real part to the imaginary part: they cannot be chosen separately. That is the idea worth retaining: in a differentiable complex function, \\( u \\) and \\( v \\) are not independent. Once one is known, the other is determined up to a constant. It is what makes the complex case so different from the real one, where two functions of two variables owe nothing to each other. Once they hold, the derivative can be computed with either of the two forms: \\[ f'(z) = \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} + j\\,\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} - j\\,\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} \\] Put another way: the conjugate cannot appear If \\( z \\) and \\( \\bar{z} \\) are taken as independent variables, the two Cauchy-Riemann equations are equivalent to a single condition: \\( \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\bar{z}} = 0 \\). A differentiable function has to depend on \\( z \\) alone. That is why \\( \\bar{z} \\), \\( |z|^2 \\) and \\( \\operatorname{Re}(z) \\) fail: all of them hide a \\( \\bar{z} \\). Function \\( u,\\ v \\) Satisfied? \\( z^2 \\) \\( x^2 - y^2,\\ 2xy \\) Yes: \\( u_x = v_y = 2x \\), \\( u_y = -v_x = -2y \\) \\( e^z \\) \\( e^x\\cos y,\\ e^x \\operatorname{sin} y \\) Yes, on the whole plane \\( \\bar{z} \\) \\( x,\\ -y \\) No: \\( u_x = 1 \\) but \\( v_y = -1 \\) \\( |z|^2 \\) \\( x^2 + y^2,\\ 0 \\) Only at \\( z"},{"t":"The four derivatives, measured","d":"The Cauchy-Riemann equations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cauchy-riemann#lab","x":"03 The four derivatives, measured The level curves of \\( u \\) and those of \\( v \\) say everything there is to know. If the function satisfies Cauchy-Riemann, the two families cross at right angles and the gradients have the same magnitude, because \\( \\nabla v \\) is \\( \\nabla u \\) rotated by 90°. Where that breaks down, the derivative does not exist. Lab · Cauchy-Riemann, point by point Level curves of \\( u \\) in blue and of \\( v \\) in green, with the two gradients at the chosen point. Click or tap the canvas to move it. f(z) = z² f(z) = e^z f(z) = 1/z f(z) = z̄ · the conjugate f(z) = |z|² f(z) = Re(z) Show the level curves"},{"t":"Necessary, and almost sufficient","d":"The Cauchy-Riemann equations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cauchy-riemann#suficiente","x":"04 Necessary, and almost sufficient Cauchy-Riemann is a necessary condition: if \\( f \\) is differentiable at \\( z_0 \\), the equations hold there. The converse is not enough. There are functions that satisfy both equations at a point and still are not differentiable, because the partial derivatives behave badly around it. The complete statement If \\( u \\) and \\( v \\) have continuous first-order partial derivatives in a neighborhood of \\( z_0 \\) and the Cauchy-Riemann equations hold there, then \\( f \\) is differentiable at \\( z_0 \\), and it is analytic on the whole neighborhood where that holds. In practice —polynomials, exponentials, rational functions away from their poles— continuity is guaranteed and checking the two equations is enough. In polar coordinates, which is the convenient way to work with \\( z^n \\), \\( \\ln z \\) and anything with circular symmetry, the equations become: \\[ \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial r} = \\frac{1}{r}\\,\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial \\theta}, \\qquad\\qquad \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial r} = -\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial \\theta}, \\qquad r \\gt 0 \\]"},{"t":"Harmonic functions and the complex potential","d":"The Cauchy-Riemann equations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cauchy-riemann#armonicas","x":"05 Harmonic functions and the complex potential Differentiating the first equation with respect to \\( x \\), the second with respect to \\( y \\) and adding, the cross terms cancel and what is left is: \\[ \\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial x^2} + \\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial y^2} = 0 \\qquad\\text{and the same for } v \\] Laplace’s equation. The real and imaginary parts of every analytic function are harmonic functions. Here the course crosses paths with Physics and with Fields. The electrostatic potential in a region with no charge satisfies exactly that equation. So, if \\( u \\) is taken as the potential, the function \\( v \\) that goes with it —its harmonic conjugate — has the field lines as its level curves, because they are orthogonal to the equipotentials. The two families seen in the lab are, in a real problem, the map of equipotentials and the map of lines of force. Constructing the conjugate means integrating the equations. With \\( u = x^2 - y^2 \\): \\[ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = 2x \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; v = 2xy + g(x), \\qquad \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} = 2y + g'(x) = -\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = 2y \\] Hence \\( g'(x) = 0 \\) and \\( v = 2xy + C \\): the function was \\( f(z) = z^2 \\) plus an imaginary constant. That procedure is what makes it possible, in a field problem, to obtain the lines of force from the potential without solving anything else. What it is good for in electronics The capacitance per unit length of a two-conductor line, the "},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The Cauchy-Riemann equations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cauchy-riemann#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Checking by hand For \\( f(z) = z^3 \\), expand \\( u \\) and \\( v \\) in terms of \\( x \\) and \\( y \\), compute the four partial derivatives and check the two equations. Then obtain \\( f'(z) \\) with both forms from section 02 and verify that both give \\( 3z^2 \\). Activity 2 · Finding where it fails Take \\( f(z) = z\\,\\operatorname{Re}(z) = x^2 + jxy \\) and find the points where the equations hold. Check that they hold only at the origin, and that therefore the function is not analytic at any point. Activity 3 · Measured equipotentials Set up an electrolytic tank with two electrodes and map, with a probe, the curves of equal potential on paper. Draw the orthogonal family on top by hand: these are the field lines, and they are the level curves of the harmonic conjugate of the potential."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The Cauchy-Riemann equations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cauchy-riemann#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Getting the sign wrong. The second equation is \\( u_y = -v_x \\); writing it without the minus sign makes the conjugate come out analytic, which it is not. Believing that satisfying the equations is enough. Continuous partial derivatives on a neighborhood are also needed. Confusing a point with a neighborhood. \\( |z|^2 \\) satisfies Cauchy-Riemann at the origin and is not analytic anywhere. Choosing \\( u \\) and \\( v \\) separately. They are not independent: once one is fixed, the other is determined up to a constant. Using the Cartesian form where the polar form is better. With \\( \\ln z \\) or \\( z^{1/2} \\), the polar version saves half the work."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The Cauchy-Riemann equations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cauchy-riemann#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Write the two equations without looking. \\( u_x = v_y \\) and \\( u_y = -v_x \\). The minus sign goes in the second one. Why doesn’t \\( \\bar{z} \\) satisfy them? With \\( u = x \\) and \\( v = -y \\): \\( u_x = 1 \\) while \\( v_y = -1 \\). The first one fails at every point of the plane. If \\( u = x^2 - y^2 \\), what is its harmonic conjugate? \\( v = 2xy + C \\), which reconstructs \\( f(z) = z^2 \\) up to a constant. What geometric relationship is there between \\( \\nabla u \\) and \\( \\nabla v \\)? They are perpendicular and of equal magnitude: \\( \\nabla v \\) is \\( \\nabla u \\) rotated by 90°. That is why the level curves cross at right angles. What equation do \\( u \\) and \\( v \\) each satisfy on their own? Laplace’s: \\( u_{xx} + u_{yy} = 0 \\). They are harmonic functions, which is the same equation that an electrostatic potential without charges satisfies. Does satisfying the equations at a point guarantee that there is a derivative? No. In addition, the partial derivatives must be continuous on a neighborhood; without that, there are counterexamples."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The Cauchy-Riemann equations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cauchy-riemann#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Ruel V. Churchill and James W. Brown. Complex Variables and Applications. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja y aplicaciones”), McGraw-Hill, 2004. The derivation of the equations, the sufficient condition and the polar form, with complete proofs. A. David Wunsch. Complex Variables with Applications. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja con aplicaciones”), Addison-Wesley Iberoamericana, 1997. The chapter on harmonic functions and the complex potential, designed for field problems. Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Elements of Electromagnetics. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Elementos de electromagnetismo”), Oxford University Press, 2018. Laplace in two dimensions, to see from the other side the same thing that is derived here from the complex derivative."},{"t":"No guessing the future","d":"Causality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/causalidad#definicion","x":"01 No guessing the future A system is causal if its output at an instant depends only on what happened up to that instant, never on what is going to happen afterward: \\[ y(t_0) \\text{ depends on } x(t) \\text{ for } t \\le t_0 \\text{, and on nothing else} \\] They are also called non-anticipative. Every system that works in real time has to be one, because the future has not arrived yet. For an LTI system the condition translates into a single sentence about the impulse response. If the impulse arrives at \\( t = 0 \\), the output cannot have started earlier: \\[ \\text{causal} \\iff h(t) = 0 \\;\\text{ for all } t \\lt 0 \\] With that, the convolution integral no longer runs over the whole axis and starts at zero, which also explains why in the one-sided Laplace transform you integrate from \\( 0 \\) to \\( \\infty \\) without losing anything. Causality and memory are not the same thing A memoryless system responds only to the present value: \\( y(t) = 3x(t) \\). One with memory also uses earlier values —a capacitor, an averager— or later ones. Every memoryless system is causal, but a system with memory can be causal or not. They are two independent classifications."},{"t":"Which part of the input is involved","d":"Causality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/causalidad#lab","x":"02 Which part of the input is involved The clearest way to see causality is to ask, for a given instant, which stretch of the input that output depends on. In a causal system that stretch always lies to the left of the cursor; in a non-causal one, it reaches to the right. Lab · the stretch each output depends on Above, the input, with the shaded zone that is involved in computing the output at the marked instant. Below, the complete output. Move the cursor and see where each value comes from. y(t) = x(t − 1) · a delay y(t) = average of the last 1.5 s y(t) = average centered at t y(t) = x(t + 1) · an advance y(t) = response of an RC y(t) = linear-phase filter Instant t₀ = 4.0 s"},{"t":"Non-causal systems exist, and they are used","d":"Causality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/causalidad#nocausales","x":"03 Non-causal systems exist, and they are used A system not being causal does not make it useless. It makes it unrealizable in real time, which is something else. If the signal is already recorded, the “future” is available in the file: Case Why it is not causal How it is used anyway Centered average Uses later samples File and image processing Linear-phase FIR filter Its \\( h[n] \\) is symmetric about the center It is delayed until it becomes causal Ideal low-pass filter Its \\( h(t) \\) is a sinc extending to \\( -\\infty \\) It is not used: it is approximated Smoothing a data series Looks both forward and backward Post-processing, never online The second row is the most interesting in practice. A linear-phase FIR filter needs to be symmetric, and symmetry about time zero would make it non-causal. The solution is simple: shift it. Shifting \\( h[n] \\) by \\( (N-1)/2 \\) samples makes it causal, and the price is a fixed delay of that many samples. That delay is the one that appears on the datasheet of any digital filter, and it is the reason an audio system with FIR processing has latency. The ideal filter cannot be causal The Paley-Wiener criterion says it precisely: a causal and stable system cannot have a magnitude of exactly zero over a band of frequencies of nonzero width. An ideal low-pass filter, which attenuates infinitely beyond the cutoff, violates that condition. The practical consequence is that every transition of a real filter has a width: there is no vertical cutoff, a"},{"t":"In discrete time","d":"Causality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/causalidad#discreto","x":"04 In discrete time The condition is the same with square brackets: \\( h[n] = 0 \\) for \\( n \\lt 0 \\). And it can be read directly from the difference equation: \\[ y[n] = \\sum_{k=0}^{M} b_k\\,x[n-k] - \\sum_{k=1}^{N} a_k\\,y[n-k] \\] All the indices are \\( n - k \\) with \\( k \\ge 0 \\): only past or current samples enter. That filter is causal by construction. If an \\( x[n+1] \\) appeared in the expression, the system would need a sample that has not yet arrived. In a processor that works on a block of data this is solved without drama; in one that processes sample by sample, it is impossible."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Causality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/causalidad#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Exercise 1 · Classifying Decide whether these are causal: \\( y(t) = x(t)\\cos(t+1) \\), \\( y[n] = x[-n] \\), \\( y(t) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{t} x(\\tau)d\\tau \\) and \\( y[n] = x[n] - x[n+2] \\). Justify each one. Exercise 2 · The latency of an FIR Program a symmetric 51-coefficient FIR filter and measure the delay between input and output with the oscilloscope. Check that it matches 25 sampling periods, which is \\( (N-1)/2 \\). Exercise 3 · The cutoff that cannot be vertical Design three low-pass filters with the same cutoff frequency and increasingly abrupt transitions. Measure the group delay of each one and observe how it grows as the transition gets sharper."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Causality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/causalidad#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Confusing causal with stable. They are independent properties: there are causal systems that are unstable and stable systems that are non-causal. Confusing causal with memoryless. A capacitor has memory and is perfectly causal. Believing a non-causal system is a mistake. It is valid, and it is used whenever the signal is recorded. Forgetting that causality is defined on \\( h \\), not on \\( x \\). It is a property of the system. Expecting a vertical cutoff from a real filter. Paley-Wiener forbids it for any causal and stable system."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Causality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/causalidad#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What condition does \\( h(t) \\) satisfy in a causal system? \\( h(t) = 0 \\) for \\( t \\lt 0 \\): it does not respond before the impulse arrives. Is \\( y(t) = x(t)\\cos(t + 1) \\) causal? Yes. The cosine depends on time, which makes it time-varying, but it only uses the present value of \\( x \\). How is a linear-phase FIR made causal? By shifting it \\( (N-1)/2 \\) samples. The price is a fixed delay of that amount. Why is an ideal low-pass filter not realizable? Because its \\( h(t) \\) is a sinc that exists for \\( t \\lt 0 \\), and because its zero magnitude over a band violates the Paley-Wiener criterion. Can a memoryless system be non-causal? No. If it only uses the present value, it is causal by definition. What gives away a non-causal system in a difference equation? A term with index \\( n+k \\) with \\( k \\) positive: it needs a sample that has not yet arrived."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Causality","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/causalidad#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1998. The properties of systems, with the translation of each one into a condition on h. John G. Proakis and Dimitris G. Manolakis. Digital Signal Processing. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Tratamiento digital de señales”), Pearson, 2007. Linear-phase FIR filters and the delay introduced by making them causal. Alan V. Oppenheim and Ronald W. Schafer. Discrete-Time Signal Processing. 3rd ed. (Spanish edition, “Tratamiento de señales en tiempo discreto”), Prentice Hall, 2011. The Paley-Wiener criterion and the consequences of causality for the frequency response."},{"t":"Every quantity has its twin","d":"Kinematics and dynamics of the rigid body","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-dinamica-rigido#paralelo","x":"01 Every quantity has its twin A rigid body is a model: a solid whose particles always keep the same distance from one another. It does not deform. With that idealization, any motion breaks down into translation of the center of mass plus rotation about it. And the remarkable thing is that rotation repeats, term by term, the structure of translation: Translation Rotation Relation Position x Angle \\( \\theta \\) \\( s = \\theta \\cdot R \\) Velocity v Angular velocity \\( \\omega \\) \\( v = \\omega \\cdot R \\) Acceleration a Angular acceleration \\( \\alpha \\) \\( a = \\alpha \\cdot R \\) Mass m Moment of inertia I \\( I = \\sum m_i \\, r_i^2 \\) Force F Torque \\( M = F \\cdot d \\) \\( M = r \\times F \\) \\( \\Sigma F = m\\cdot a \\) \\( \\Sigma M = I\\cdot \\alpha \\) The second law, twice \\( p = m\\cdot v \\) \\( L = I\\cdot \\omega \\) Angular momentum \\( E_c = \\tfrac{1}{2} m v^2 \\) \\( E_c = \\tfrac{1}{2} I \\omega^2 \\) They add up if both are present \\( P = F\\cdot v \\) \\( P = M\\cdot \\omega \\) Power The power of a motor The last row is the one used every day: a 1.5 kW motor turning at 1500 rpm delivers a torque of \\(M = P/\\omega = 1500/(1500 \\cdot 2\\pi/60) =\\) \\(9.55\\,\\mathrm{N \\cdot m}\\). That calculation decides which motor is needed to move a load, and it is the same for a 3D-printer servo as for a grinding mill."},{"t":"Moment of inertia","d":"Kinematics and dynamics of the rigid body","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-dinamica-rigido#inercia","x":"02 Moment of inertia Mass measures the resistance to changes in velocity; the moment of inertia measures the resistance to changes in angular velocity. And unlike mass, it depends on the axis: the same part is harder or easier to spin depending on where you hold it. Body (axis through the center) Moment of inertia \\( k = \\dfrac{I}{m\\cdot R^{2}} \\) Hoop or thin-walled hollow cylinder \\( m\\cdot R^{2} \\) 1 Disk or solid cylinder \\( \\tfrac{1}{2}\\cdot m\\cdot R^{2} \\) 0.5 Hollow sphere \\( \\tfrac{2}{3}\\cdot m\\cdot R^{2} \\) 0.667 Solid sphere \\( \\tfrac{2}{5} m R^2 \\) 0.4 Rod about its center \\( \\dfrac{1}{12}\\cdot m\\cdot L^{2} \\) — Rod about one end \\( \\tfrac{1}{3}\\cdot m\\cdot L^{2} \\) — The parallel-axis theorem (Steiner) For an axis parallel to the one through the center of mass, at a distance d: \\(I = I_{ ext{cm}} + m \\cdot d^2\\). That is why a rod held at one end has \\( frac{1}{3} \\cdot m \\cdot L^2\\) and not \\(1/12\\): \\(1/12 + 1/4 = 1/3\\)."},{"t":"Rotational dynamics and rolling","d":"Kinematics and dynamics of the rigid body","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-dinamica-rigido#dinamica","x":"03 Rotational dynamics and rolling A body that rolls without slipping satisfies \\(v = \\omega \\cdot R\\), and its kinetic energy is split between translation and rotation. Writing the conservation of energy for a drop through a height h: \\[ m g h = \\frac{1}{2} m v^2 + \\frac{1}{2} I \\omega^2 \\quad \\implies \\quad a = \\frac{g \\, \\operatorname{sin} \\theta}{1 + k} \\] With \\(k = I/(m \\cdot R^2)\\). Neither the mass nor the radius appears: only the shape of the body. This gives a result that always surprises: in a race down an inclined plane, the solid sphere beats the disk, and the disk beats the hoop, regardless of how much each weighs or how big it is. The winner is the one that puts less energy into spinning. Lab · race of rolling bodies All five start together from the same height. Move with the time slider and watch the finishing order. Incline 20 ° Length of the plane 4.0 m Time 0.00 s"},{"t":"Angular momentum and its conservation","d":"Kinematics and dynamics of the rigid body","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-dinamica-rigido#angular","x":"04 Angular momentum and its conservation If the net external torque is zero, \\(L = I \\cdot \\omega\\) is conserved. Since \\(I\\) can change —by moving mass closer to or farther from the axis—, the angular velocity changes in the opposite direction: The skater who pulls in her arms reduces I and spins faster. The cat that falls on its back rights itself in midair with no external torque: it changes its shape. A satellite is stabilized by spinning an internal wheel; the spacecraft turns the opposite way. This is the principle of the reaction wheel, which orients space telescopes without using fuel. A gyroscope resists changes in the orientation of its axis, and that property is what a MEMS gyroscope in a phone or a drone measures. The gyroscopic effect When a torque is applied to a spinning body, the axis does not move in the direction the force pushes but perpendicular to it: this is precession. It is what keeps a moving bicycle upright and what makes a tilted spinning top turn instead of falling over."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Kinematics and dynamics of the rigid body","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-dinamica-rigido#electronica","x":"05 Where it shows up in electronics Sizing a motor. The starting torque must overcome the moment of inertia of the load: \\(M = I \\cdot \\alpha\\). A stepper motor that falls short skips steps, and the error accumulates. Encoders. They measure θ and, by differentiating, ω. The speed control of a motor is a loop around that measurement. MEMS gyroscopes. They detect rotation through the Coriolis effect on a vibrating mass: the image stabilization of a camera and the control of a drone depend on them. Disks and fans. The time a disk or a fan takes to reach operating speed comes from \\(I \\cdot \\alpha = M_{\\text{motor}} - M_{\\text{friction}}\\). Flywheels. They store energy as \\(\\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot I \\cdot \\omega^2\\), and are used as a reserve in large uninterruptible power supply systems."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Kinematics and dynamics of the rigid body","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-dinamica-rigido#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · The race, on the bench Let a hoop, a disk and a sphere of the same diameter roll down an inclined board. Verify the finishing order and check that it does not change when bodies of different mass are used. Activity 2 · Measuring the moment of inertia of a wheel Hang a weight from a string wound around the axle of a wheel and measure the acceleration with which it descends. Solve for \\(I\\) from \\(a = g/(1 + I/(m \\cdot r^2))\\). Compare with the theoretical value for a disk. Activity 3 · Reading a gyroscope With an MPU-6050, record the angular velocity while rotating the board 90°. Integrate ω numerically to obtain the angle and check how much it drifts because of the sensor’s drift: that accumulated error is the reason it is combined with the accelerometer in a complementary filter."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Kinematics and dynamics of the rigid body","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-dinamica-rigido#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Using a moment of inertia without saying about which axis. It makes no sense. Forgetting the parallel-axis theorem when the axis does not pass through the center of mass. Adding only the translational energy for a rolling body: \\(\\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot I \\cdot \\omega^2\\) is missing. Believing the heavier body wins the race. The mass cancels. Confusing the torque of a force with angular momentum. One is the cause, the other is what changes: \\(M = dL/dt\\). Applying \\(v = \\omega \\cdot R\\) when there is slipping. It only holds in pure rolling."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Kinematics and dynamics of the rigid body","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-dinamica-rigido#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Which reaches the bottom of a plane first: a solid sphere or a hoop? The sphere: \\(k = 0.4\\) versus \\(k = 1\\), so its acceleration is \\(g \\cdot \\sin heta/1.4\\) versus \\(g \\cdot \\sin heta/2\\). It arrives first: it takes 16% less time (and at the bottom it moves about 20% faster). A 2 kW motor turns at 3000 rpm. What is its torque? \\(\\omega = 3000 \\cdot 2\\pi/60 = 314\\text{ rad/s}\\), so \\(M = 2000/314 \\approx\\) \\(6.4\\,\\mathrm{N \\cdot m}\\). How much energy does a flywheel with \\(I = 0.5\\,\\mathrm{kg \\cdot m^2}\\) store at 6000 rpm? \\(\\omega = 628\\text{ rad/s}\\), \\(E = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 0.5 \\cdot 628^2 \\approx\\) \\(98.6\\text{ kJ}\\). Doubling the speed quadruples the energy. A 1 m, 2 kg rod rotates about one end. What is its moment of inertia? \\(I = \\tfrac{1}{3} \\cdot m \\cdot L^2 = \\tfrac{1}{3} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 =\\) \\(0.667\\,\\mathrm{kg \\cdot m^2}\\). Why does the skater speed up when she pulls in her arms? Because \\(L = I \\cdot \\omega\\) is conserved: as \\(I\\) decreases, \\(\\omega\\) increases in the same proportion. The kinetic energy, on the other hand, increases, and comes from the muscular work of pulling the arms in. In what proportion is the energy of a rolling disk divided? Two thirds in translation and one third in rotation, because \\(\\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot I \\cdot \\omega^2 = \\tfrac{1}{4} \\cdot m \\cdot v^2\\), half of the translational energy."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Kinematics and dynamics of the rigid body","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-dinamica-rigido#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics, Volume 1. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage Learning, 2014. Chapters 10 and 11: rotation, rolling and angular momentum. Paul A. Tipler and Gene Mosca. Physics for Scientists and Engineers, Volume 1. 6th ed., W. H. Freeman, 2008 (Spanish edition, “Física para la ciencia y la tecnología”, Reverté, 2010). Good development of the parallel-axis theorem and of gyroscopic precession. Ferdinand P. Beer and E. Russell Johnston. Vector Mechanics for Engineers: Dynamics. 10th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2013 (Spanish edition, “Mecánica vectorial para ingenieros: dinámica”). The engineering approach, with many cases of real machines. Marcelo Alonso and Edward J. Finn. Physics. Addison-Wesley, 1992 (Spanish edition, “Física”, Addison-Wesley Iberoamericana, 1995). Treats rotation starting from angular momentum, not from the moment of inertia."},{"t":"Describing a motion","d":"Kinematics of a particle","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-punto#marco","x":"01 Describing a motion Before asking why something moves, you have to be able to say how it moves. That takes three prior decisions, which are almost always made without noticing: A frame of reference. There is no absolute motion: a seated passenger is at rest relative to the bus and moving at 60 km/h relative to the street. Both descriptions are correct. A time origin. The instant called \\(t = 0\\). A model of the body. If the size of the object does not matter for the problem, it is treated as a particle (point mass): all its mass concentrated at a point. A satellite in orbit is a point; the same satellite pointing its antenna is not. The three functions of time The position \\(\\vec{r}(t)\\) is the primary datum. Velocity is its derivative and acceleration is the derivative of velocity: \\[ \\vec{v} = \\frac{d \\vec{r}}{d t}, \\quad \\vec{a} = \\frac{d \\vec{v}}{d t} = \\frac{d^2 \\vec{r}}{d t^2} \\] And the other way around, by integrating: from the acceleration you recover the velocity and from that the position, at the cost of two constants, which are the initial velocity and the initial position. This structure —a quantity, its derivative and its second derivative— is the same one found in the differential equations that describe the charging of a capacitor. It is not a forced analogy: it is literally the same mathematics with different names."},{"t":"Rectilinear motion","d":"Kinematics of a particle","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-punto#rectilineo","x":"02 Rectilinear motion Case Acceleration Velocity Position Uniform (constant velocity) 0 v constant \\( x = x_{0} + v\\cdot t \\) Uniformly accelerated (constant acceleration) a constant \\( v = v_{0} + a\\cdot t \\) \\( x = x_{0} + v_{0}\\cdot t + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\cdot a\\cdot t^{2} \\) Free fall \\( g \\approx 9.8 \\ \\mathrm{m/s^2} \\) downward \\( v = v_{0} - g\\cdot t \\) \\( y = y_{0} + v_{0}\\cdot t - \\tfrac{1}{2}\\cdot g\\cdot t^{2} \\) Eliminating time between the last two columns of the constant-acceleration case gives a very useful relation, because it does not require knowing how long the motion lasted: \\[ v^2 = v_0^2 + 2 a \\left(x -x_0 \\right) \\] Average velocity and instantaneous velocity Average velocity is displacement divided by time, and it can be zero even if the body has moved a great deal: it only has to return to its starting point. Average speed, on the other hand, uses the distance traveled and is never negative. On an x–t graph, the average velocity is the slope of the secant line and the instantaneous velocity is the slope of the tangent. Lab · motion simulator Choose the type of motion and move through time with the slider. The numbers on the right come from differentiating the position, not from a table. Uniform rectilinear motion Uniformly accelerated rectilinear motion Projectile motion Uniform circular motion Show the v and a vectors Initial velocity 20 m/s Launch angle 45 ° Time 0 s"},{"t":"Reading graphs","d":"Kinematics of a particle","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-punto#graficas","x":"03 Reading graphs A good share of kinematics problems are solved by looking rather than calculating, if you remember two rules: Slope and area The slope of a graph is the derivative and the area under it is the integral. Slope of x–t = velocity. Slope of v–t = acceleration. Area under v–t = displacement. Area under a–t = change in velocity. That is why, in constant-acceleration motion, the area under v–t is a trapezoid, and its formula —average base times height— is exactly \\(x = \\dfrac{v_0 + v}{2} \\cdot t\\). The area under the curve counts with sign: a stretch of v–t below the axis subtracts displacement. It is the same distinction as between the definite integral and geometric area."},{"t":"Motion in the plane: projectile motion","d":"Kinematics of a particle","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-punto#compuesto","x":"04 Motion in the plane: projectile motion The principle of independence of motions says that the x and y components can be treated separately. In projectile motion, with air resistance neglected: Axis Type of motion Equations Horizontal Constant velocity: there is no horizontal force \\( x = v_{0}\\cdot \\cos\\theta \\cdot t \\) Vertical Constant acceleration with \\( a = -g \\) \\( y = v_{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot t - \\tfrac{1}{2}\\cdot g\\cdot t^{2} \\) From these come the three results worth memorizing, for a launch from the ground: \\[ t_v = \\frac{2 v_0 \\operatorname{sin} \\theta}{g}, \\quad h_{\\max} = \\frac{v_0^2 {\\operatorname{sin}}^2 \\theta}{2 g}, \\quad A = \\frac{v_0^2 \\operatorname{sin} 2 \\theta}{g} \\] The range is maximum at 45°, and two complementary angles —30° and 60°, for example— give the same range with different trajectories."},{"t":"Circular motion","d":"Kinematics of a particle","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-punto#circular","x":"05 Circular motion In uniform circular motion the magnitude of the velocity does not change, but its direction does: there is acceleration, and it always points toward the center. \\[ v = \\omega r, \\quad a_c = \\frac{v^2}{r} = \\omega^2 r, \\quad T = \\frac{2 \\pi}{\\omega} \\] Why it matters to an electronics engineer The angular velocity ω in rad/s is the same ω as the angular frequency of a sinusoidal signal. Projecting onto an axis a point that rotates with constant angular velocity gives exactly a sinusoid: that is the origin of the phasor used to solve AC circuits, and the reason an alternator generates a sinusoidal voltage as it rotates."},{"t":"Relative motion","d":"Kinematics of a particle","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-punto#relativo","x":"06 Relative motion If a body moves relative to a frame that is itself moving, the velocities add as vectors: \\(\\vec{v}_{AC} = \\vec{v}_{AB} + \\vec{v}_{BC}\\). This explains why a swimmer who crosses a river perpendicular to the bank ends up downstream, and why you have to aim upstream to reach the point directly opposite. The same reasoning, applied to waves instead of bodies, produces the Doppler effect, and when the velocities approach that of light, simple addition no longer holds: that is where relativity begins."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Kinematics of a particle","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-punto#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring g with a phone Film an object falling at 240 frames per second against a vertical ruler. Measure the position every five frames, plot y–t and fit a parabola. The quadratic coefficient is \\(-g/2\\). Activity 2 · Projectile motion with a hose With a water jet at constant flow rate, measure the range for five angles between 15° and 75°. Verify that the maximum is at 45° and that complementary angles give equal ranges. Activity 3 · Velocity with an optical sensor Build two gates with an LED and a phototransistor 20 cm apart and use a microcontroller to measure the time between interrupts. It is the same principle as a motor encoder, and it lets you measure the instantaneous velocity of a cart on an inclined plane."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Kinematics of a particle","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-punto#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Confusing velocity with speed, or displacement with distance traveled. Believing that zero acceleration implies zero velocity. A body can move fast without accelerating, and be instantaneously at rest with maximum acceleration: that is what happens at the highest point of a vertical throw. Using the constant-acceleration formulas when the acceleration is not constant. Mixing up the axes in projectile motion and applying gravity to the horizontal motion. Assuming there is no acceleration in uniform circular motion because the speed does not change. The direction does change, and that is already acceleration. Forgetting the signs: once the positive direction of the axis is fixed, you have to stick to it to the end."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Kinematics of a particle","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-punto#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment A car goes from 0 to 100 km/h in 8 s. What is its average acceleration? \\(100\\text{ km/h} = 27.8\\text{ m/s}\\), so \\(a = 27.8/8 \\approx\\) \\(3.5\\text{ m/s}^2\\), a bit more than a third of \\(g\\). A stone is thrown upward at 20 m/s. How high does it go and how long does it take to come back? \\(h = v_0^2/(2g) = 400/19.6 \\approx\\) \\(20.4\\text{ m}\\); it takes \\(v_0/g \\approx 2.04\\text{ s}\\) to rise and twice that, 4.08 s, to return to the starting point. What does the area under a v–t graph represent? The displacement, counted with sign. Two projectiles leave at 30 m/s, one at 30° and the other at 60°. Which one lands farther away? Neither: the angles are complementary and the range is the same, about 79.5 m. The one at 60° goes higher and takes longer to land. A point rotates at 3000 rpm at a radius of 4 cm. What is its centripetal acceleration? \\(\\omega = 3000 \\cdot 2\\pi/60 = 314\\text{ rad/s}\\), so \\(a = \\omega^2 r = 314^2 \\cdot 0.04 \\approx\\) \\(3950\\text{ m/s}^2\\), about 400 times \\(g\\). That is why rotors are balanced so carefully. Why does a uniformly rotating point generate a sinusoid when projected? Because its coordinate is \\(x = r \\cdot \\cos(\\omega t)\\): the same expression as an AC signal of amplitude \\(r\\) and angular frequency \\(\\omega\\). This is where the phasor representation comes from."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Kinematics of a particle","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-punto#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics, Volume 1. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage Learning, 2014. Chapters 2 to 4: the standard text for the course, with graded problems. Paul A. Tipler and Gene Mosca. Physics for Scientists and Engineers, Volume 1. 6th ed., W. H. Freeman, 2008 (Spanish edition, “Física para la ciencia y la tecnología”, Reverté, 2010). More discursive explanations and good examples of relative motion. Richard P. Feynman. The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I: Mainly Mechanics, Radiation, and Heat. Addison-Wesley, 1963 (Spanish edition, “Física, volumen I: mecánica, radiación y calor”, Addison-Wesley / Pearson). Chapters 8 and 9 show where the differential calculus applied to motion comes from. Marcelo Alonso and Edward J. Finn. Physics. Addison-Wesley, 1992 (Spanish edition, “Física”, Addison-Wesley Iberoamericana, 1995). The classic of Argentine engineering schools, with a vector treatment from the start."},{"t":"How fast, not whether it happens","d":"Chemical kinetics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinetica#velocidad","x":"01 How fast, not whether it happens Thermodynamics says whether a reaction can occur; kinetics says how long it takes. They are independent questions, and confusing them leads to absurd conclusions. The oxidation of silicon in air is spontaneous and highly exothermic, and yet a wafer does not fall apart: the native layer grows two nanometers and stops, because at room temperature the rate is negligible. \\[ v = -\\frac{1}{a}\\frac{d[\\mathrm{A}]}{dt} = k\\,[\\mathrm{A}]^m[\\mathrm{B}]^n \\] The rate law is determined experimentally. The exponents \\( m \\) and \\( n \\) are the order with respect to each reactant, and they need not coincide with the coefficients of the balanced equation. That last warning is important. The coefficients come from balancing; the orders, from measuring. They only coincide when the reaction occurs in a single elementary step, which is the exception. Order Integrated law Half-life What plots as a straight line 0 \\( [A] = [A]_0 - kt \\) \\( [A]_0 / 2k \\) \\( [A] \\) against \\( t \\) 1 \\( [A] = [A]_0 e^{-kt} \\) \\( \\ln 2 / k \\) \\( \\ln[A] \\) against \\( t \\) 2 \\( 1/[A] = 1/[A]_0 + kt \\) \\( 1 / k[A]_0 \\) \\( 1/[A] \\) against \\( t \\) The last column is the working method: all three plots are tried and the one that gives a straight line reveals the order. The slope gives \\( k \\). Only first order has a constant half-life, which is how it is recognized immediately."},{"t":"The Arrhenius equation","d":"Chemical kinetics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinetica#arrhenius","x":"02 The Arrhenius equation For two molecules to react it is not enough that they collide: they have to collide with enough energy to overcome a barrier, the activation energy. And since the fraction of molecules that overcome a barrier follows an exponential, so does the rate constant: \\[ k = A\\,e^{-E_a/kT} \\ \\Longrightarrow\\; \\ln k = \\ln A - \\frac{E_a}{k}\\cdot\\frac{1}{T} \\] Plotting \\( \\ln k \\) against \\( 1/T \\) gives a straight line of slope \\( -E_a/k \\). That is how an activation energy is measured, and how a service life is extrapolated to a temperature at which nobody has time to run tests. From here comes the rule that everyone who repairs power supplies knows: for every 10 °C increase, the life of the electrolytic capacitor is cut in half. It is not a separate rule, it is Arrhenius written in a different way. With an activation energy of 0.83 eV, a 10 °C step around 100 °C gives exactly a factor of 2. Lab · Arrhenius and service life Four failure mechanisms with their measured activation energy. Move the operating temperature and the estimated life appears, the acceleration factor relative to the test and how many degrees it takes to double the life. aluminum electrolytic capacitor · Eₐ = 0.83 eV electromigration in aluminum traces · Eₐ = 0.60 eV electromigration in copper traces · Eₐ = 0.90 eV moisture corrosion, 85/85 test · Eₐ = 0.79 eV Operating temperature = 40 °C Why this is called accelerated testing Nobody can wait ten years to find out whether a product lasts t"},{"t":"Catalysts","d":"Chemical kinetics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinetica#catalisis","x":"03 Catalysts A catalyst offers an alternative path with a lower activation energy. It does not change the end point —neither the enthalpy nor the equilibrium constant moves— and it is not consumed either: it changes only how long it takes. Process Typical \\( E_a \\) Where it matters Reaction in solution at room temperature 0.4 to 0.8 eV Chemical etching, developing Electromigration in aluminum 0.6 eV Life of the traces of a chip Thermal oxidation of silicon, linear regime 2.0 eV Growth of the gate oxide Diffusion of phosphorus in silicon 3.7 eV Why dopants do not migrate on their own at 25 °C The last row explains something that is taken for granted: a transistor keeps its doping profile for decades because, with a 3.7 eV barrier, at room temperature diffusion is on the order of one atom every several ages of the universe. The same process at 1000 °C takes minutes, and that is what is used to manufacture it."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Chemical kinetics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinetica#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · The order by the straight-line method Follow the fading of permanganate with oxalic acid by measuring absorbance or color against time. Plot \\( [A] \\), \\( \\ln[A] \\) and \\( 1/[A] \\) against \\( t \\), and determine the order by which of the three gives a straight line. Activity 2 · Measuring an activation energy Repeat the same reaction at four temperatures between 10 and 50 °C. Plot \\( \\ln k \\) against \\( 1/T \\), obtain \\( E_a \\) from the slope and compare with the tabulated values. Activity 3 · What the datasheet says Look up the datasheets of three electrolytic capacitors of different quality and note their rated life and their test temperature. Extrapolate with Arrhenius to 40 °C and compare: the difference in price is usually explained there."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Chemical kinetics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinetica#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Reading the order from the balanced equation. The order is measured; the coefficients are balanced. Believing that a catalyst shifts the equilibrium. It speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally: the end point is the same. Confusing spontaneous with fast. Diamond is unstable relative to graphite and nothing happens. Extrapolating with Arrhenius outside the tested range. It holds as long as the failure mechanism does not change. Using degrees Celsius in the exponent. \\( T \\) goes in kelvin, and so does \\( 1/T \\). Assuming a constant half-life at any order. Only first order has one."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Chemical kinetics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinetica#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment How is the order of a reaction determined? Experimentally: you try which of the three plots —\\( [A] \\), \\( \\ln[A] \\) or \\( 1/[A] \\) against \\( t \\)— gives a straight line. What is obtained from the slope of \\( \\ln k \\) against \\( 1/T \\)? The activation energy, with its sign changed and divided by the Boltzmann constant. Why is the life of an electrolytic capacitor cut in half every 10 °C? Because its degradation mechanism follows Arrhenius with \\( E_a \\approx 0.83 \\) eV, and with that barrier ten degrees give exactly a factor of 2 around 100 °C. Does a catalyst change \\( \\Delta H \\)? No. It changes the path and the activation energy; the starting point and the end point stay where they were. Why does the doping of a transistor not diffuse on its own? Because the diffusion of phosphorus in silicon has an activation energy of 3.7 eV: at room temperature the rate is indistinguishable from zero. What does an accelerated test assume? That the activation energy does not change over the tested range and that the failure mechanism remains the same."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Chemical kinetics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinetica#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Raymond Chang and Kenneth A. Goldsby. Chemistry. 12th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química”), McGraw-Hill, 2017. Chapter 13: rate laws, orders, mechanisms, Arrhenius and catalysis. Peter Atkins and Julio de Paula. Physical Chemistry. 10th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química física”), Editorial Médica Panamericana, 2018. The complete treatment of reaction mechanisms and transition state theory. Michael Pecht et al. Physics-of-Failure-Based Reliability Prediction. Wiley, in Electronic Reliability Handbook. The Arrhenius, Peck and Black models applied to the service life of electronic components."},{"t":"Mutual inductance","d":"Inductively coupled circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-acoplados#mutua","x":"01 Mutual inductance When the magnetic flux of one coil passes through another, a varying current in the first induces a voltage in the second with no electrical connection between them. This relationship is measured by the mutual inductance \\( M \\), and it adds a term to the equation of each coil: \\[ v_1 = L_1\\frac{di_1}{dt} + M\\frac{di_2}{dt} \\qquad v_2 = M\\frac{di_1}{dt} + L_2\\frac{di_2}{dt} \\] \\[ M = k\\sqrt{L_1L_2}, \\quad 0 \\le k \\le 1 \\] \\( k \\) is the coupling coefficient: the fraction of the flux of one coil that reaches the other. With \\( k=1 \\) all the flux is common; with \\( k=0 \\) the coils cannot see each other. The sign of the \\( M \\) term depends on the winding directions, and it is indicated on the schematic with the dot convention: if both currents enter at the ends marked with a dot, the fluxes add and the mutual term takes a positive sign. Coupling Typical \\( k \\) Power transformer with laminated iron core 0.998 or more Ferrite transformer of a switched-mode power supply 0.99 Qi wireless charger, phone well seated 0.5 to 0.7 RFID coils a few centimeters apart 0.01 to 0.1"},{"t":"The ideal transformer","d":"Inductively coupled circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-acoplados#ideal","x":"02 The ideal transformer If the coupling is perfect (\\( k=1 \\)), the losses are zero and the inductances are so large that the current needed to magnetize the core is negligible, the equations reduce to purely algebraic relationships between the two coils, with \\( n = N_2/N_1 = \\sqrt{L_2/L_1} \\): \\[ \\frac{V_2}{V_1} = n \\qquad \\frac{I_2}{I_1} = \\frac{1}{n} \\] \\[ Z_{in} = \\frac{Z_L}{n^2} \\] The last relationship is the most useful: a load \\( Z_L \\) on the secondary is “seen” from the primary divided by the square of the turns ratio. The transformer is an impedance matcher. The output transformer of a tube amplifier A power tube performs well with a load of about 5 kΩ, and a loudspeaker is 8 Ω. A transformer with ratio \\( n = \\sqrt{8/5000} = 1/25 \\) makes the loudspeaker look like 5 kΩ from the tube. It is the reason for being of the heaviest part of any tube amplifier."},{"t":"From the real transformer to the ideal one","d":"Inductively coupled circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-acoplados#real","x":"03 From the real transformer to the ideal one A real transformer departs from the ideal in two ways, and each one rules at one end of the frequency range. At low frequency, the reactance \\( \\omega L_1 \\) of the primary stops being large compared with the reflected load, and the source has to deliver a considerable magnetizing current. At high frequency, the flux that does not couple —the leakage inductance, \\( (1-k^2)L_2 \\) seen from the secondary— ends up in series with the load and steals voltage from it. The transformer behaves as ideal only in the band that lies between those two limits. \\[ Z_{in} = j\\omega L_1 + \\frac{\\omega^2 M^2}{R_L + j\\omega L_2} \\] \\[ \\frac{V_2}{V_1} = \\frac{j\\omega M\\,R_L}{(R_L + j\\omega L_2)\\,Z_{in}} \\] The two exact expressions for a primary driven by a voltage source and a secondary loaded with \\( R_L \\). With \\( k=1 \\) the voltage ratio gives exactly \\( n \\) at any frequency; the input impedance additionally needs \\( \\omega L_1 \\) to be large. Lab · when a pair of coils is a transformer \\( L_1 = 10 \\) mH, \\( L_2 = 2.5 \\) mH (ratio 2:1, \\( n = 0.5 \\)) and a 100 Ω load: the ideal would promise 400 Ω at the input. You move the coupling and the frequency; the curves show how far the real pair departs from the ideal (1 = ideal). Coupling k = 0.999 Frequency = 50 kHz"},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Inductively coupled circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-acoplados#electronica","x":"04 Where it shows up in electronics Device What it takes advantage of in the coupling Transformer of a linear charger Stepping 220 V down to a low voltage and isolating the user from the mains Transformer of a switched-mode power supply The same, at tens of kHz: that is why it is much smaller than a 50 Hz one Current transformer of a meter Measuring hundreds of amperes without interrupting the conductor Data isolation in Ethernet Each port carries transformers that pass the signal and block DC and differing ground potentials Wireless charging Transferring energy with loose coupling, compensated with resonance on both sides The relationship between size and frequency in the table is no coincidence: to transfer the same power, the required core falls roughly in inverse proportion to the frequency. Going from 50 Hz to 100 kHz is what allowed a phone charger to fit in the plug."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Inductively coupled circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-acoplados#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Marking the dots With two coupled coils, a generator and a two-channel oscilloscope, determine which ends carry the dot by observing whether the voltages are in phase or in antiphase. Activity 2 · Measuring k Measure \\( L_1 \\) and \\( L_2 \\) with an inductance meter; then measure the inductance of the two coils in series one way and the other. With \\( L_a - L_b = 4M \\), calculate \\( M \\) and \\( k \\). Activity 3 · The useful band Using this page’s lab, find between which frequencies the input impedance stays within 10 % of 400 Ω for \\( k = 0.99 \\) and for \\( k = 0.9 \\)."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Inductively coupled circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-acoplados#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Ignoring the dot convention. A wrong sign on \\( M \\) turns the sum of fluxes into a subtraction. Applying the ideal transformer to DC. A transformer does not transfer DC: with \\( \\omega = 0 \\) there is no induction. Reflecting the impedance with \\( n \\) and not with \\( n^2 \\). Impedances are reflected with the square of the turns ratio. Believing that \\( k = 1 \\) is enough to be ideal. You also need a large magnetizing inductance compared with the reflected load. Forgetting galvanic isolation. It is often the main reason for using a transformer."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Inductively coupled circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-acoplados#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What does the coupling coefficient measure? The fraction of the flux of one coil that passes through the other: \\( k = M/\\sqrt{L_1L_2} \\). How is a load reflected to the primary of an ideal transformer? Divided by \\( n^2 \\), with \\( n = N_2/N_1 \\). What limits a real transformer at low frequency? The inductance of the primary: its reactance stops being large and the magnetizing current becomes considerable. And at high frequency? The leakage inductance, which ends up in series with the load. Why is a switched-mode power supply transformer so small? Because it works at tens of kHz, and the core required for the same power falls roughly with the inverse of the frequency."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Inductively coupled circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-acoplados#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 7th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2021. The chapter on magnetically coupled circuits, with the dot convention and the ideal transformer. Stephen J. Chapman. Electric Machinery Fundamentals. 5th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2011 (Spanish edition, “Máquinas eléctricas”). The real transformer, its equivalent circuit and the open-circuit and short-circuit tests. Colonel Wm. T. McLyman. Transformer and Inductor Design Handbook. 4th ed., CRC Press, 2011. Real transformer design for switched-mode power supplies, with the relationship between frequency and core size."},{"t":"Three voltages 120° apart","d":"Polyphase circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-polifasicos#trifasica","x":"01 Three voltages 120° apart A three-phase generator has three windings spaced 120° apart in space, and produces three voltages of equal amplitude 120° apart in time. The entire electrical grid of the world is generated, transmitted and distributed this way, for three reasons that appear in this unit: with three conductors you transmit three times the power of a single phase with two conductors, the total instantaneous power is constant —and motors turn without vibrating— and two different voltages are obtained from the same grid. \\[ \\mathbf{V}_a = V_f\\angle 0^\\circ \\qquad \\mathbf{V}_b = V_f\\angle{-120^\\circ} \\] \\[ \\mathbf{V}_c = V_f\\angle 120^\\circ \\qquad \\mathbf{V}_a + \\mathbf{V}_b + \\mathbf{V}_c = 0 \\] The sum of the three voltages is zero at every instant: this is what allows the neutral to carry no current when the loads are equal."},{"t":"Wye and delta","d":"Polyphase circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-polifasicos#estrella","x":"02 Wye and delta The three phases can be connected in two ways. In wye (Y) each load goes between one phase and the common neutral; in delta (Δ), between two phases. The voltages between phases —line voltages— are \\( \\sqrt3 \\) times the phase voltages: \\[ V_L = \\sqrt3\\,V_f \\qquad P = \\sqrt3\\,V_L I_L \\cos\\varphi \\] In Argentina the low-voltage grid is 3 × 380/220 V, 50 Hz: 220 V between each phase and neutral, 380 V between phases (the current standard takes it to 400/230 V). A house receives one phase and the neutral; a workshop or a building, all three. Wye Delta Voltage across each load \\( V_f = V_L/\\sqrt3 \\) \\( V_L \\) Line current Equal to that of each load \\( \\sqrt3 \\) times that of each load Neutral Available Does not exist Typical use Residential distribution Motors and industrial loads"},{"t":"The neutral and imbalance","d":"Polyphase circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-polifasicos#neutro","x":"03 The neutral and imbalance If the three loads of a wye are equal, their currents add up to zero and the neutral carries nothing. But residential consumption is never balanced: in a building, each apartment is on a different phase and each one draws what it wants. The neutral then carries the sum of the three currents, and it also performs a function that is noticed only when it is missing: it holds the common point of the loads at zero volts. If the neutral is cut, the common point of the loads is left floating and shifts toward the most heavily loaded phase. Its voltage comes directly from Millman’s theorem from the previous topic: \\[ \\mathbf{V}_{N'} = \\frac{\\mathbf{V}_a/Z_a + \\mathbf{V}_b/Z_b + \\mathbf{V}_c/Z_c}{1/Z_a + 1/Z_b + 1/Z_c} \\] Each load is subjected to \\( \\mathbf{V}_x - \\mathbf{V}_{N'} \\) instead of to its phase voltage. The phase with the least consumption is the one that receives the highest voltage. Lab · the neutral cut Three loads in wye on a 3 × 380/220 V grid, one per phase. You choose the power of each one and whether the neutral is connected. The phasor diagram shows the grid voltages, the common point of the loads and the voltage that reaches each one. Phase A = 2000 W Phase B = 100 W Phase C = 500 W neutral connected neutral cut A frequent accident A neutral cut at the service entrance of a building is one of the most common causes of appliances burned out en masse: the apartments on the least loaded phase suddenly receive more than 300 V. That is wh"},{"t":"Constant power","d":"Polyphase circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-polifasicos#potencia","x":"04 Constant power In a single phase the instantaneous power oscillates at twice the grid frequency and passes through zero a hundred times per second. In a balanced three-phase system, the three oscillations are out of phase and their sum is exactly constant. A three-phase motor then receives a constant torque and runs smoothly, whereas a single-phase one vibrates at twice the grid frequency. It is one of the reasons why industry uses three-phase even when the power could be transmitted in single-phase."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Polyphase circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-polifasicos#electronica","x":"05 Where it shows up in electronics Case What is used from this unit Motor variable-speed drive Rectifies the three-phase supply and synthesizes it again at another frequency with transistors Switched-mode power supplies in an office building Their 3rd-order harmonics add up in the neutral instead of canceling, and overheat it Inverter of a three-phase solar system Generates the three phases at 120° synchronized with the grid Three-phase energy meter Measures power in the three phases, or in two with the two-wattmeter method"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Polyphase circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-polifasicos#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Phase and line voltages With a three-phase practice panel and the appropriate protections, use a multimeter to measure the three phase voltages and the three line voltages. Verify the \\( \\sqrt3 \\) relationship. Activity 2 · Neutral current Using this page’s lab and the neutral connected, make the three powers equal and verify that the neutral current vanishes. Then leave a single load and verify that the neutral carries all its current. Activity 3 · Millman by hand For loads of 2000, 100 and 500 W with the neutral cut, calculate by hand the voltage of the common point and that of each load, and compare with the panel."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Polyphase circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-polifasicos#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Adding the phase voltages as plain numbers. They are phasors at 120°: the line voltage is \\( \\sqrt3 \\) times the phase voltage, not double. Believing the neutral “carries nothing.” Only with perfectly balanced loads and no harmonics. Protecting the neutral with its own fuse. If only the neutral opens, the common point floats and overvoltages appear. Using \\( P = 3VI\\cos\\varphi \\) with line voltages. With line values it is \\( \\sqrt3\\,V_L I_L\\cos\\varphi \\). Thinking a neutral cut only switches things off. The voltage on the least loaded phase rises."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Polyphase circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-polifasicos#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What is the line voltage of a grid with a 220 V phase voltage? \\( \\sqrt3 \\times 220 \\approx 380 \\) V. When does no current flow through the neutral? When the three loads are equal and linear: their currents add up to zero. What happens to the least loaded phase if the neutral is cut? The common point shifts toward the most loaded phase and the least loaded one receives a voltage higher than nominal. Why does a three-phase motor run more smoothly than a single-phase one? Because the instantaneous power of a balanced three-phase system is constant. Which theorem gives the voltage of the common point with the neutral cut? Millman’s."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Polyphase circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-polifasicos#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 7th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2021. The chapter on three-phase circuits, with balanced and unbalanced loads. Argentine Electrotechnical Association (AEA). Reglamentación para la ejecución de instalaciones eléctricas en inmuebles (AEA 90364). AEA (in Spanish). The standard that governs installations in Argentina, including the prohibition on disconnecting the neutral separately. Stephen J. Chapman. Electric Machinery Fundamentals. 5th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2011 (Spanish edition, “Máquinas eléctricas”). The appendix on three-phase circuits, written for what motors later demand."},{"t":"From the truth table to the minimal expression","d":"Combinational logic: minimization and hazards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/combinacional-minimizacion#minimizar","x":"01 From the truth table to the minimal expression Gates, Boolean algebra and the step from a truth table to a circuit are covered in the page on combinational logic in the Electronics Technician program. This unit starts from there and asks the engineering question: among all the circuits that implement the same function, which one is the best? Read straight from the table, any function comes out as a canonical sum of products: one AND term for each row that equals 1, with all four variables. It is correct and almost always needlessly expensive. Minimizing it reduces three things at once: the number of gates (the cost and the area on a chip), the number of inputs per gate, and the depth of the circuit, which is what decides how fast it responds. \\[ f = \\sum m(0,2,3,5,6,7,8,9) + \\sum d(10\\ldots15) \\] \\[ \\Longrightarrow\\; f = A + C + BD + \\overline{B}\\,\\overline{D} \\] Segment a of a seven-segment display driven in BCD: eight terms of four literals reduce to four terms, two of them with a single variable. Codes 10 to 15 do not exist in BCD and can be chosen as 0 or as 1: they are don’t-cares."},{"t":"The Karnaugh map","d":"Combinational logic: minimization and hazards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/combinacional-minimizacion#karnaugh","x":"02 The Karnaugh map The Karnaugh map rearranges the truth table into a grid where two neighboring cells differ in a single variable. To achieve this, rows and columns are numbered in Gray code —00, 01, 11, 10— and opposite edges are neighbors too: the map is really a torus. Two neighboring ones can be grouped because the variable that changes between them does not matter: \\( AB + A\\overline B = A \\). Rule Why Groups have 1, 2, 4, 8 or 16 cells Each doubling eliminates exactly one variable Groups are rectangles, and can wrap around the edges Opposite edges differ in a single variable The largest possible group is preferred More cells, fewer literals in the term Groups can overlap A 1 covered twice does not change the function (\\( X + X = X \\)) Don’t-cares (X) are used only if they enlarge a group They do not need to be covered Every group that is not contained in a larger one is a prime implicant. Those that cover some 1 that no other group covers are essential and must be included; the rest are chosen to complete the cover with the fewest possible terms."},{"t":"Quine-McCluskey: the same method, for a computer","d":"Combinational logic: minimization and hazards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/combinacional-minimizacion#qm","x":"03 Quine-McCluskey: the same method, for a computer The map works by eye up to five or six variables; beyond that it becomes impossible to read. The Quine-McCluskey method does the same thing in tabular form: it combines in pairs the terms that differ in one bit, repeats until nothing more can be combined, and what is left uncombined are the prime implicants. It then solves a covering problem: choosing the smallest set of primes that covers all the ones. That second stage is a set-cover problem, and it grows explosively with the number of variables. That is why real synthesis tools —from ESPRESSO in the 1980s to those that compile for an FPGA today— use heuristics that find very good solutions without guaranteeing the optimal one. The lab on this page applies the full Quine-McCluskey method, which with four variables is solved exactly. Lab · four-variable Karnaugh map Click or tap each cell to cycle it from 0 to 1, from 1 to don’t-care (X) and back to 0, or choose an example. The lab finds the prime implicants with Quine-McCluskey, chooses the minimum cover, draws it on the map and warns you if any static hazard remains. — choose an example — segment a of a BCD display (with don’t-cares) four-input majority: at least three inputs at 1 odd parity of four bits a function with a static hazard empty map"},{"t":"Hazards: when the minimal expression fails","d":"Combinational logic: minimization and hazards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/combinacional-minimizacion#riesgos","x":"04 Hazards: when the minimal expression fails Boolean algebra assumes that signals change instantaneously. Real gates take time: a 74HC inverter on the order of 10 ns. If the same variable reaches an OR gate by two paths with different delays —one direct and one through an inverter—, during those nanoseconds the two terms that use it can both be 0 at once, and the output, which should have stayed at 1, drops for an instant. That is a static-1 hazard, and the spurious pulse it produces is called a glitch. \\[ f = \\overline{B}D + BC \\quad \\text{(minimal, with hazard)} \\] \\[ f = \\overline{B}D + BC + CD \\quad \\text{(with the consensus term, hazard-free)} \\] With \\( C = D = 1 \\), the output should be 1 no matter how \\( B \\) changes. The term \\( CD \\) adds no 1 to the map: it only covers the transition between the two groups, and that is enough to keep the output from dropping. On the map, the hazard is easy to spot: it appears when two neighboring ones are covered by different groups with no group containing both of them. In a combinational circuit that feeds another combinational circuit the glitch usually does not matter; in one that drives the clock or the reset of a flip-flop, or an enable line, it can cause an intermittent fault that is very hard to find. That is why synchronous design —the subject of the next unit— samples the outputs only once they have settled."},{"t":"Delay and critical path","d":"Combinational logic: minimization and hazards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/combinacional-minimizacion#retardo","x":"05 Delay and critical path The output of a combinational circuit is valid only after the signal has traveled the slowest path, the critical path. Minimizing gates and minimizing delay do not always go together, and the adder shows this best. n-bit adder How the carry propagates Delay Gates Ripple-carry adder Each stage waits for the carry from the previous one Grows as \\( n \\) Few Carry-lookahead adder Computes all the carries in parallel with generate and propagate terms Grows as \\( \\log n \\) Many more A 64-bit ripple-carry adder would need on the order of 128 gate delays; a carry-lookahead one, a few levels. Every modern processor pays in area to gain speed right there."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Combinational logic: minimization and hazards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/combinacional-minimizacion#electronica","x":"06 Where it shows up in electronics Case What is used from this unit Seven-segment decoder Don’t-cares: the BCD codes that do not exist simplify each segment FPGA synthesis The tool minimizes on its own, and then maps the result onto lookup tables Memory enable logic A glitch on the chip select can write data where it should not ALU of a microprocessor Carry lookahead to keep addition fast"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Combinational logic: minimization and hazards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/combinacional-minimizacion#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Seven segments, complete Minimize the seven segments of a BCD decoder using the don’t-cares. Build one of them with 74HC gates and verify it with the ten digits. Activity 2 · Hunting a glitch Build \\( \\overline{B}D + BC \\) with real gates, hold \\( C = D = 1 \\), toggle \\( B \\) with a generator and look with the oscilloscope for the spurious pulse on the output. Add \\( CD \\) and check that it disappears. Activity 3 · Quine-McCluskey by hand Solve the four-variable majority function by tabulation and compare the prime implicants with those the lab finds."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Combinational logic: minimization and hazards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/combinacional-minimizacion#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Numbering the map in ordinary binary. With 00, 01, 10, 11 neighboring cells do not differ in a single bit and the groups stop being valid. Forgetting that the edges are neighbors. The four corners of a four-variable map form a group. Making groups of three or six. Only powers of two. Covering the don’t-cares as if they were ones. They are used if they help; they do not need to be covered. Believing the minimal expression is always the best. It can have hazards that one with an extra term does not have."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Combinational logic: minimization and hazards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/combinacional-minimizacion#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment Why is the Karnaugh map numbered in Gray code? So that two neighboring cells differ in a single variable, which is what makes it possible to group them. What is an essential prime implicant? A prime implicant that covers at least one 1 that no other prime covers; it must appear in every minimal expression. What does a don’t-care do on the map? It can be taken as 1 if it enlarges a group, or ignored if it does not: the specification does not require any value for that combination. When is there a static-1 hazard? When two neighboring ones end up in different groups with no group covering both of them: when the variable that separates them changes, the output can drop for an instant. Why is a carry-lookahead adder faster? Because it computes the carries in parallel instead of waiting for each stage to pass its carry to the next."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Combinational logic: minimization and hazards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/combinacional-minimizacion#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading M. Morris Mano and Michael D. Ciletti. Digital Design. 6th ed., Pearson, 2018. Karnaugh maps, Quine-McCluskey and combinational blocks, with the classic approach of digital techniques courses. John F. Wakerly. Digital Design: Principles and Practices. 5th ed., Pearson, 2018. The most careful treatment of hazards and of the real delays of logic families. Robert K. Brayton et al. Logic Minimization Algorithms for VLSI Synthesis. Kluwer, 1984. The ESPRESSO book: how a program minimizes when there are too many variables for Quine-McCluskey."},{"t":"The voltage-current relationship of each component","d":"Passive components: frequency and time","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/componentes-pasivos#relaciones","x":"01 The voltage-current relationship of each component Three passive elements are enough to build almost any linear circuit. Each one relates voltage and current in a different way, and that difference is what makes this whole course interesting. Component v-i relationship Does it have memory? Energy it stores Resistor \\( v = Ri \\) No: the voltage depends only on the current at that instant None; it dissipates as heat Inductor \\( v = L\\dfrac{di}{dt} \\) Yes: the voltage depends on how the current changes \\( \\tfrac12 Li^2 \\), in the magnetic field Capacitor \\( i = C\\dfrac{dv}{dt} \\) Yes: the current depends on how the voltage changes \\( \\tfrac12 Cv^2 \\), in the electric field “Having memory” is the key idea: the voltage of an inductor and the current of a capacitor cannot be calculated by looking only at the present instant; you need the history of how they got there. That is, exactly, what turns a circuit with \\( L \\) or \\( C \\) into a problem of differential equations."},{"t":"Phasors: turning a differential equation into algebra","d":"Passive components: frequency and time","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/componentes-pasivos#fasores","x":"02 Phasors: turning a differential equation into algebra Solving differential equations every time a circuit has a sinusoidal source would be exhausting. The phasor trick avoids it: in steady state, every voltage and every current of a linear circuit excited at a single frequency \\( \\omega \\) is a sinusoid of that same frequency, and a sinusoid is completely determined by two numbers, its amplitude and its phase. A phasor is, precisely, those two numbers packed into a single complex number. \\[ v(t) = V_m\\cos(\\omega t + \\varphi) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\mathbf{V} = V_m\\angle\\varphi \\] The phasor \\( \\mathbf{V} \\) has no “time” inside: the dependence on \\( \\omega t \\) is factored out, common to every voltage and current in the circuit, and so it can be left out while you operate. Differentiating with respect to time becomes multiplying by \\( j\\omega \\); integrating, dividing by \\( j\\omega \\). That correspondence —differentiating is multiplying by \\( j\\omega \\)— is what turns the circuit’s whole differential equation into an algebraic equation with complex numbers, as mechanical to solve as a purely resistive circuit."},{"t":"Complex impedance: the generalized Ohm’s law","d":"Passive components: frequency and time","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/componentes-pasivos#impedancia","x":"03 Complex impedance: the generalized Ohm’s law With phasors, each passive component behaves like a “complex resistance,” the impedance \\( Z = \\mathbf{V}/\\mathbf{I} \\), and Ohm’s law and Kirchhoff’s laws apply exactly the same as with pure resistances, now with complex numbers. \\[ Z_R = R \\qquad\\qquad Z_L = j\\omega L \\qquad\\qquad Z_C = \\frac{1}{j\\omega C} = -\\frac{j}{\\omega C} \\] The impedance of the inductor grows with frequency; that of the capacitor falls. This is the exact mathematical reason why an inductor “blocks” high frequency and a capacitor “lets it through,” the intuitive language used throughout filter electronics. Component At 50 Hz (mains) At 1 MHz (AM radio) 10 mH inductor \\( |Z_L| = 3.14 \\) Ω \\( |Z_L| = 62{,}832 \\) Ω 100 nF capacitor \\( |Z_C| = 31{,}831 \\) Ω \\( |Z_C| = 1.59 \\) Ω The same pair of components behaves in almost opposite ways depending on the working frequency: at 50 Hz the inductor is almost a short circuit and the capacitor almost an open circuit; at 1 MHz, the other way around. Designing a filter is, in essence, exploiting that crossover."},{"t":"The time domain: the transient response of an RC circuit","d":"Passive components: frequency and time","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/componentes-pasivos#transitorio","x":"04 The time domain: the transient response of an RC circuit Phasors only describe the steady state: what happens long after any switching event —closing a switch, connecting a source— has stopped having an effect. The moment of switching itself demands going back to the time domain and solving the differential equation. The simplest and most important case in the whole course is the first-order RC circuit. \\[ v(t) = V_f + (V_0 - V_f)\\,e^{-t/\\tau} \\qquad \\tau = RC \\] \\( V_0 \\) is the initial voltage of the capacitor, \\( V_f \\) the final voltage it tends to, and \\( \\tau \\) the time constant: the time it takes to cover 63.2 % of the distance it still has to go. After \\( 5\\tau \\) the capacitor is at 99.3 % of \\( V_f \\), the practical convention for “it has arrived.” Lab · charging a capacitor You choose \\( R \\) and \\( C \\); the plot traces the charge from 0 V to 5 V and marks \\( \\tau \\), \\( 3\\tau \\) and \\( 5\\tau \\). 100 Ω 1 kΩ 10 kΩ 100 kΩ 1 MΩ 1 nF 10 nF 100 nF 1 µF 10 µF"},{"t":"The two domains are the same physics, seen in two ways","d":"Passive components: frequency and time","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/componentes-pasivos#dosdominio","x":"05 The two domains are the same physics, seen in two ways The complete solution of any linear circuit with a sinusoidal source is always the sum of two parts: the steady-state response —the sinusoid given by phasor analysis, which lasts forever— and the transient response —a decaying exponential that describes how the circuit gets from its initial state to that steady state—. Phasors compute the first part effortlessly, at the cost of not being able to see the second; the differential equation in time computes both, at the cost of much more work. Choosing the right tool depends on which question needs to be answered."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Passive components: frequency and time","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/componentes-pasivos#electronica","x":"06 Where it shows up in electronics Application What it uses Switch-bounce suppression ( debounce ) for a pushbutton An RC with a \\( \\tau \\) of a few milliseconds filters out the mechanical bounce of the contact Power-up circuit ( power-on reset ) An RC holds a pin low for a few milliseconds after power-up, so that the microcontroller starts in a known state RC low-pass / high-pass filter The same impedance \\( Z_C \\) that falls with frequency, used to separate bands RC snubber on a relay or a thyristor It absorbs the switching transient so as not to damage the semiconductor"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Passive components: frequency and time","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/componentes-pasivos#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Designing a debounce circuit Using this page’s lab, choose \\( R \\) and \\( C \\) so that \\( 5\\tau \\) is between 5 and 10 ms, a typical debounce time. Verify with at least two different combinations of \\( R \\) and \\( C \\) that they give the same \\( \\tau \\). Activity 2 · Crossed impedances Calculate \\( |Z_L| \\) and \\( |Z_C| \\) for a 1 mH inductor and a 1 µF capacitor at 100 Hz, 10 kHz and 1 MHz. Identify in which range each one behaves most like a short circuit and in which like an open circuit. Activity 3 · Separating steady state from transient For an RC circuit driven by a sinusoidal source that is connected at \\( t=0 \\), identify which part of the response a phasor computes and which it does not, and explain why the complete differential equation is needed only at the instant of connection."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Passive components: frequency and time","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/componentes-pasivos#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Applying phasors during a transient. Phasors only describe the steady state; the moment of switching needs the differential equation in the time domain. Forgetting that \\( Z_L \\) and \\( Z_C \\) depend on frequency. They are not fixed numbers like \\( R \\): they change with \\( \\omega \\), and have to be recalculated for each frequency of interest. Confusing the time constant \\( \\tau \\) with the time it takes for the transient to “finish.” \\( \\tau \\) is 63.2 %; the practical convention for finished is \\( 5\\tau \\). Thinking an inductor or a capacitor “has” a fixed resistance. Its impedance is reactive —purely imaginary, in the ideal case— and depends on \\( \\omega \\); it is not a constant real resistance. Ignoring the initial condition of the capacitor or the inductor. \\( V_0 \\) in the charging formula is not always zero; with a nonzero initial charge, the curve is different."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Passive components: frequency and time","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/componentes-pasivos#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment Why do an inductor or a capacitor “have memory” and a resistor does not? Because their v-i relationship involves a derivative or an integral: their value at an instant depends on how they got there, not only on the present value of the other variable. What does a phasor represent? The amplitude and the phase of a steady-state sinusoid, packed into a complex number, without the explicit dependence on time. How is the impedance of a capacitor calculated? \\( Z_C = 1/(j\\omega C) \\): it falls with frequency and is inversely proportional to the capacitance. What does the time constant \\( \\tau = RC \\) mean? The time it takes the capacitor voltage to cover 63.2 % of the distance it still has to go to its final value. Why are phasors not enough to describe the instant a switch closes? Because they describe only the steady state; the transient that follows the closing needs the complete differential equation solved in the time domain. Which combination of \\( R \\) and \\( C \\) would you use for a debounce circuit with a \\( \\tau \\) of 1 ms? Any whose product \\( RC \\) gives 1 ms, for example 10 kΩ with 100 nF."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Passive components: frequency and time","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/componentes-pasivos#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 7th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2021. The chapters on phasors, impedance and first-order response, which develop in detail everything this page summarizes. James W. Nilsson and Susan A. Riedel. Electric Circuits. 11th ed., Pearson, 2019. A very gradual treatment of the RC and RL transient response, with dozens of examples worked step by step. Horowitz and Hill. The Art of Electronics. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2015. Chapter 1 connects this theory with real everyday RC circuits: debouncing, filters and snubbers."},{"t":"Software-defined radio","d":"Software-defined radio and digital communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/comunicaciones-digitales-rf#sdr","x":"01 Software-defined radio In a classic receiver, each function is a circuit: the channel filter, the demodulator, the carrier recovery. In a software-defined radio (SDR), the analog front end is reduced to amplifying, sometimes translating, and digitizing; everything else is done by a processor, an FPGA or a computer. Changing the standard, the bandwidth or the modulation is changing the program. The same blocks of receivers and transmitters are still there, but on the digital side of the converter: Digital block Function Numerically controlled oscillator and digital mixer Bring the channel down to baseband, with perfectly balanced I and Q Decimating filters (CIC and FIR) Select the channel and reduce the sampling rate Synchronization Recover the carrier, the symbol clock and the frame Equalizer and demodulator Correct the channel and decide the symbols Channel decoder Correct errors using the code The processing relies on the hardware of digital signal processing, and the modulations and their error rates are those of noise in digital modulations."},{"t":"Bandpass sampling","d":"Software-defined radio and digital communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/comunicaciones-digitales-rf#pasabanda","x":"02 Bandpass sampling The sampling theorem calls for sampling at twice the maximum frequency, but for a bandpass signal, which occupies only from \\( f_L \\) to \\( f_H \\), twice the bandwidth is enough, if the sampling frequency is chosen well: the alias of the band falls cleanly in the first Nyquist zone, without overlapping itself. This is undersampling, which replaces a mixer with the converter itself: \\[ \\frac{2 f_H}{n} \\le f_s \\le \\frac{2 f_L}{n - 1}, \\quad n = 1, 2, \\dots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{f_H}{B} \\right\\rfloor \\] Each n gives a valid interval; with even n the band appears inverted, which is corrected in the processing. The theoretical minimum frequency is 2B, but in practice margin is left for the filters and the clock tolerance. The converter has to have analog bandwidth up to \\( f_H \\) and a clock with low phase noise, because the jitter is multiplied with the signal frequency. Lab · bandpass sampling A band of width B centered at f c. Above, where it appears after sampling, in the first Nyquist zone, from 0 to f s /2; below, the valid sampling frequencies in green and the chosen one in yellow. Center frequency f c = Bandwidth B = Sampling frequency f s ="},{"t":"Current digital systems","d":"Software-defined radio and digital communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/comunicaciones-digitales-rf#sistemas","x":"03 Current digital systems Mobile communications, Wi-Fi and digital television combine everything seen: adaptive QAM modulations, OFDM with hundreds of subcarriers that turn a channel with echoes into many flat channels, LDPC or turbo codes close to the Shannon limit, multiple antennas (MIMO) that multiply the capacity by exploiting the different propagation paths, and an RF front end that is, in large part, a software-defined radio."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Software-defined radio and digital communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/comunicaciones-digitales-rf#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · SDR receiver With a low-cost SDR receiver and GNU Radio, receive a commercial FM station, filter the channel, demodulate and listen to the audio. Identify each block of the diagram. Activity 2 · Undersampling Sample a 10.7 MHz signal with a 4 MS/s converter and find where it appears in the digital spectrum. Activity 3 · Constellation Transmit and receive a QPSK between two SDRs and observe the constellation before and after carrier recovery."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Software-defined radio and digital communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/comunicaciones-digitales-rf#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Sampling a bandpass signal at any frequency greater than 2B. Only the valid intervals work. Forgetting the bandpass filter before the converter. Noise and signals from all the Nyquist zones fold onto the band. Using a noisy clock to undersample. Jitter degrades the signal-to-noise ratio in proportion to the signal frequency. Forgetting the spectrum inversion in even zones. The frequency has to be inverted in the processing."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Software-defined radio and digital communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/comunicaciones-digitales-rf#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What sampling frequencies work for a 60 to 80 MHz band with n = 2? From 80 to 120 MHz. With f s = 100 MHz, where does that band appear and how? Between 20 and 40 MHz, inverted. What is the theoretical minimum sampling frequency for a 20 MHz band? 40 MHz, if the band is located at an integer position relative to its width. What advantage does the digital mixer have over an analog one? Its I and Q branches are exactly equal: there is no imbalance or carrier leakage."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Software-defined radio and digital communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/comunicaciones-digitales-rf#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Travis F. Collins, Robin Getz, Di Pu and Alexander M. Wyglinski. Software-Defined Radio for Engineers. Artech House, 2018. Software-defined radio with exercises on real hardware, freely available. Richard G. Lyons. Understanding Digital Signal Processing. 3rd ed., Prentice Hall, 2010. Bandpass sampling and digital frequency conversion. Jeffrey H. Reed. Software Radio: A Modern Approach to Radio Engineering. Prentice Hall, 2002. The architecture of software-defined radios."},{"t":"Standards and specifications","d":"Standards, failures and reliability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/confiabilidad#normas","x":"01 Standards and specifications An electronic product is designed against a specification: what it has to do, under what conditions and for how long. A good part of it is set by standards, which make it possible to compare products and meet legal requirements: Body Examples of standards IEC Electrical safety (IEC 62368-1), electromagnetic compatibility (61000 series), components IPC Printed circuit board design (IPC-2221), acceptability of assemblies (IPC-A-610), soldering (J-STD-001) ISO Quality management (ISO 9001), environmental management (ISO 14001) JEDEC Packages, moisture sensitivity and reliability tests of semiconductors IRAM Argentine standards, many adopted from international ones The specifications of a component distinguish the absolute maximum ratings, which must never be exceeded, from the recommended operating conditions and the guaranteed characteristics. Testing and certification were covered in testing and standards, in Electronic Measurements I."},{"t":"Failures and the bathtub curve","d":"Standards, failures and reliability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/confiabilidad#fallas","x":"02 Failures and the bathtub curve The failure rate of a population of components follows the bathtub curve: high at the start, because of manufacturing defects ( infant mortality ), low and almost constant during the useful life (random failures), and rising at the end, because of wear-out. Manufacturers eliminate the first part with burn-in tests ( burn-in ). The most common failure mechanisms are electromigration in metallizations, oxide breakdown, thermal fatigue of solder joints, corrosion and the drying out of electrolytic capacitors."},{"t":"Failure rate, MTBF and reliability","d":"Standards, failures and reliability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/confiabilidad#modelo","x":"03 Failure rate, MTBF and reliability In the flat region, the failure rate \\( \\lambda \\) is constant and the probability of working without failing up to \\( t \\) is exponential. In a series system, where any failure stops it, the rates add up: \\[ R(t) = e^{-\\lambda t}, \\quad MTBF = \\frac{1}{\\lambda} \\] \\[ \\lambda_{sys} = \\sum_i \\lambda_i \\] Rates are expressed in FIT, failures per 10⁹ hours. 200 components of 10 FIT give 2000 FIT: an MTBF of 500,000 hours. That does not mean the equipment lasts 57 years: the probability of working five years without failing is 92 %. With redundancy, two units in parallel where one is enough, the probability of failure is the product: \\( R_{par} = 1 - (1 - R)^2 \\). The failure rate depends on temperature according to the Arrhenius model: \\[ AF = \\exp\\!\\left[\\frac{E_a}{k}\\left(\\frac{1}{T_{ref}} - \\frac{1}{T}\\right)\\right] \\] \\( E_a \\) is the activation energy of the failure mechanism, typically 0.5 to 1 eV, and \\( k \\) = \\( 8.617\\cdot 10^{-5} \\) eV/K. With 0.7 eV, every 20 °C more multiplies the rate by four or five. It is also the basis of accelerated tests. Lab · reliability and temperature A piece of equipment with N components of 10 FIT each at 40 °C and an activation energy of 0.7 eV. The curve is the probability of working without failing as a function of the years, without redundancy (blue) and with two redundant units (green). Components N = Operating temperature = The failure rates of each type of component are estimated with predictio"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Standards, failures and reliability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/confiabilidad#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Prediction Estimate the MTBF of a lab board by counting its components by type and using failure rates from a prediction handbook. Activity 2 · Temperature Measure with a thermal camera or thermocouples the temperature of the hottest components of a power supply and recompute the reliability. Activity 3 · Datasheet In the datasheet of a regulator, separate the absolute maximum ratings, the recommended conditions and the guaranteed characteristics."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Standards, failures and reliability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/confiabilidad#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Reading the MTBF as the useful life. It is the inverse of the random failure rate, not the time until wear-out. Designing at the absolute maximum rating. It is a damage limit; work with margins, derating voltage, current and temperature. Forgetting temperature in reliability. A few degrees more multiply the failure rate. Believing that redundancy solves everything. Common-cause failures affect both units at once."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Standards, failures and reliability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/confiabilidad#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What MTBF does a 5000 FIT piece of equipment have? 200,000 hours. What probability does it have of working one year without failing? \\( e^{-8760/200\\,000} \\approx 0.957 \\). With \\( E_a = 0.7\\ \\text{eV} \\), how much does the failure rate increase going from 40 to 60 °C? About 4.7 times. What is infant mortality? The high failure rate at the beginning of life, because of manufacturing defects; it is eliminated with burn-in tests."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Standards, failures and reliability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/confiabilidad#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Patrick D. T. O'Connor and Andre Kleyner. Practical Reliability Engineering. 5th ed., Wiley, 2012. The practical reference on reliability, prediction and accelerated testing. United States Department of Defense. MIL-HDBK-217F: Reliability Prediction of Electronic Equipment. 1991. The classic handbook of failure rates by component type. Milton Ohring and Lucian Kasprzak. Reliability and Failure of Electronic Materials and Devices. 2nd ed., Academic Press, 2014. The physical failure mechanisms of electronic materials and devices."},{"t":"Science, craft, technology and engineering","d":"Scientific and technological knowledge","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conocimiento-cientifico-tecnologico#palabras","x":"01 Science, craft, technology and engineering The words are used as synonyms and they are not. Telling them apart is not an academic whim: it changes what you can demand of each one and which questions it answers. Activity What it seeks How it is validated Example in electronics Science To explain: true knowledge about how the world is By testing predictions against experience Band theory explains why doped silicon conducts Craft To make: practical know-how, often passed on from person to person Because it works, even if nobody quite knows why Knowing how to solder well, “nudging” an adjustment until the stage stops oscillating Technology To make with a foundation: craft supported by scientific knowledge By its effectiveness, measured and reproducible The manufacturing process of an integrated circuit Engineering To solve a specific problem, with finite resources and deadlines Because the system meets the specification, within the accepted cost and risk Designing a power supply that delivers 5 V ±2% at 3 A for ten years The difference that matters most Science can afford to say “we don’t know yet.” Engineering cannot: a decision has to be made anyway, with the information available, and the engineer has to answer for the result. That asymmetry explains almost everything else: safety margins, standards, type tests and professional responsibility."},{"t":"How scientific knowledge is validated","d":"Scientific and technological knowledge","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conocimiento-cientifico-tecnologico#metodo","x":"02 How scientific knowledge is validated The classical scheme —observe, formulate a hypothesis, deduce consequences, test them— has an asymmetry that Karl Popper placed at the center: no number of confirmations proves a theory, but a single contrary experiment refutes it. That is why the criterion for calling a claim scientific is that it be falsifiable: that you can say in advance which result would leave it in trouble. A claim that cannot be refuted is not stronger: it is weaker “This cable improves the sound, but only trained ears can perceive it” is built so that it can never fail, and so it says nothing verifiable. In electronics the honest equivalent is: “this cable lowers the contact resistance by 3 mΩ, measured this way.” That can be checked, and it can be disproved. Thomas Kuhn added the historical dimension: science does not advance only by accumulating data. It works within a paradigm —a set of accepted assumptions, methods and problems— until anomalies pile up and a scientific revolution comes along and replaces it. The move from classical physics to quantum physics, without which the transistor would not exist, is the most frequently cited case."},{"t":"Technological knowledge has rules of its own","d":"Scientific and technological knowledge","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conocimiento-cientifico-tecnologico#tecnologico","x":"03 Technological knowledge has rules of its own It is not simply “applied science.” It has its own form, criteria and problems: It seeks effectiveness, not truth. A technological rule says “to obtain A under conditions B, do C.” It is judged by whether it works and at what cost, not by whether it is true. It deliberately uses false models. The ideal diode, the source with no internal resistance, the amplifier with infinite gain: everyone knows they do not exist, and they are used anyway because they simplify things and give good enough results. Knowing when a model stops being useful is part of the trade. It incorporates tacit knowledge. Some know-how is not written down anywhere and is learned by doing: why one circuit board oscillates and the one next to it doesn’t, how you “smell” an overheated component. It is codified in standards and datasheets. An IRAM or IEC standard (IRAM is the Argentine Standards and Certification Institute; IEC, the International Electrotechnical Commission) is crystallized technological knowledge: decades of tests and accidents condensed into a procedure. “It works” and “I know why it works” are not the same A circuit can work on the bench by sheer chance —a parasitic capacitance that happens to filter out the noise— and fail in production. What separates the skilled technician from the engineer is being able to explain why it works, which is the only thing that makes it possible to predict when it will stop working."},{"t":"Science and technology push each other in both directions","d":"Scientific and technological knowledge","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conocimiento-cientifico-tecnologico#relacion","x":"04 Science and technology push each other in both directions The “linear model” —science discovers, technology applies, industry produces— is convenient and often false. The history of electronics shows both directions: Case What came first Steam engine and thermodynamics The engine came first (Watt, 1769) and the theory that explains it came later (Carnot, 1824). Technology led the way Transistor First quantum mechanics and solid-state physics, then the device (Bardeen, Brattain and Shockley, 1947). Science led the way Optical fiber Optics was already known; it took a breakthrough in materials, ultrapure glass, to make the idea viable Integrated circuit No new science and no new theory: an idea about process (Kilby and Noyce, 1958-59) that changed the whole industry Today people speak of R&D&I: research, development and innovation, as activities that feed back into each other. And to describe what stage a technology has reached, technology readiness levels (TRL, from 1 to 9) are used, running from the basic principle observed to the system proven in its real environment. They help avoid mistaking a laboratory result for a product. Lab · what are we talking about? Read the case and decide. There is no trick: the idea is to discuss the boundaries, which are often blurry. Science Craft Technology Engineering Another case"},{"t":"What this means at the workbench","d":"Scientific and technological knowledge","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conocimiento-cientifico-tecnologico#practica","x":"05 What this means at the workbench Reading a datasheet is reading codified science. Every curve came from a physical model and from a measurement made with a declared method. The test conditions —temperature, voltage, time— are part of the data, not fine print. Measuring is testing a hypothesis. Before measuring, it pays to write down what you expect to find. If the result matches, the model is useful; if not, there is something to learn, and that is more valuable. Documenting is making your own work falsifiable. A report that does not say which instrument, under what conditions and with what uncertainty something was measured lets no one —not even you, six months later— check anything. Standards are not bureaucracy. They are the accumulated knowledge of the profession, and they are often written after an accident."},{"t":"Activities","d":"Scientific and technological knowledge","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conocimiento-cientifico-tecnologico#laboratorio","x":"06 Activities Activity 1 · Tracing a technology Choose an everyday component —the white LED, the lithium battery, the power MOSFET— and reconstruct its history: what scientific knowledge made it possible, what technical problem it solved, what industry pushed it and in which decade it became mass-market. Identify whether science led or followed. Activity 2 · Falsifiable or not Collect five advertising claims for electronic products and rewrite each one in falsifiable form, indicating which measurement would put it to the test and with which instrument. Activity 3 · Models and their limits Take three models used in class (ideal diode, ideal operational amplifier, resistor with no inductance) and determine experimentally under what conditions they stop describing real behavior. You can start with the instrument simulator and continue at the bench."},{"t":"Common misconceptions","d":"Scientific and technological knowledge","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conocimiento-cientifico-tecnologico#errores","x":"07 Common misconceptions Believing that technology is only “gadgets.” A quality-management method or a communication protocol is technology too. Assuming that science always comes first. Often practice leads and theory arrives later to explain it. Mistaking a model for reality and being surprised when the real circuit behaves differently. Confusing correlation with cause when interpreting a measurement: two things that change together are not always related. Discarding the tacit knowledge of the shop floor because it isn’t published."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Scientific and technological knowledge","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conocimiento-cientifico-tecnologico#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What does it mean for a claim to be falsifiable? That you can specify in advance which observation would contradict it. It is Popper’s criterion for telling a scientific claim from one that is not. Difference between craft and technology. Craft is know-how, often without an explanation of why; technology is that same doing grounded in scientific knowledge, which makes it possible to predict, optimize and transfer. Why use models that are known to be incorrect? Because they simplify the calculation and give results that are close enough within a known range. What matters is knowing what that range is. What is a paradigm according to Kuhn? The set of assumptions, methods and problems accepted by a scientific community during a period. When anomalies overwhelm it, a scientific revolution takes place. What are TRL levels for? To locate the stage of maturity a technology has reached, from the idea to the system proven under real conditions, and to avoid mistaking a laboratory prototype for a product."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Scientific and technological knowledge","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conocimiento-cientifico-tecnologico#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Mario Bunge. La ciencia, su método y su filosofía. Siglo Veinte / Sudamericana (in Spanish). Short and sharp: the distinction between science, craft and pseudoscience, written by an Argentine physicist and philosopher. Thomas S. Kuhn. The Structure of Scientific Revolutions. University of Chicago Press, 1962 (Spanish edition: “La estructura de las revoluciones científicas,” Fondo de Cultura Económica). The book that introduced the idea of the paradigm and changed the way the history of science is understood. Karl R. Popper. The Logic of Scientific Discovery. Hutchinson, 1959 (Spanish edition: “La lógica de la investigación científica,” Tecnos). The original formulation of the falsifiability criterion. Carl Mitcham. ¿Qué es la filosofía de la tecnología? Anthropos (in Spanish). An orderly introduction to the questions specific to technological knowledge, as distinct from scientific knowledge."},{"t":"Why it pays to conserve something","d":"Conservation laws in mechanics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conservacion-mecanica#porque","x":"01 Why it pays to conserve something Solving a problem with Newton’s laws requires knowing the forces at every instant. Often they are not known —what exact force do two cars exert on each other during a crash?— and yet the problem can be answered, because certain quantities have the same value before and after no matter what happened in between. The three in the syllabus Mechanical energy, if the forces acting are conservative. Momentum, if the net external force is zero. Angular momentum, if the net external torque is zero. Each has its condition: it is not always conserved, it is conserved when the condition holds."},{"t":"Work and kinetic energy","d":"Conservation laws in mechanics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conservacion-mecanica#trabajo","x":"02 Work and kinetic energy The work done by a force along a path is the line integral of that force; for a constant force and a straight-line displacement it reduces to \\(W = F \\cdot d \\cdot \\cos\\alpha\\). The work–energy theorem \\[ W_{\\mathrm{net}} = \\Delta E_c = \\frac{1}{2} m v_f^2 -\\frac{1}{2} m v_i^2 \\] The net work of all the forces equals the change in kinetic energy. It always holds, with or without friction. A force perpendicular to the displacement does no work: that is why the normal force and the centripetal force never change the speed. And power is work per unit time, \\(P = F \\cdot v\\), the same quantity that in a circuit is \\(P = V \\cdot I\\)."},{"t":"Potential energy and conservative forces","d":"Conservation laws in mechanics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conservacion-mecanica#potencial","x":"03 Potential energy and conservative forces A force is conservative when its work does not depend on the path, only on the endpoints. For such forces —and only for them— a potential energy can be defined: Force Potential energy Conservative Weight \\(E_p = m \\cdot g \\cdot h\\) Yes Elastic \\(E_p = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot k \\cdot x^2\\) Yes General gravitational \\(E_p = -G \\cdot M \\cdot m/r\\) Yes Electric \\(E_p = q \\cdot V\\) Yes Friction Does not exist No: it dissipates Conservation of mechanical energy If only conservative forces do work, \\(E_c + E_p = \\text{constant}\\) (kinetic plus potential energy). With friction, the difference is exactly the heat dissipated: \\[ \\Delta E_c + \\Delta E_p = W_{\\mathrm{fric}} \\] The relation between a conservative force and its potential is \\(\\vec{F} = -\\nabla E_p\\): the force points in the direction in which the potential energy decreases fastest. It is the same relation as between the electric field and the potential, \\(\\vec{E} = -\\nabla V\\)."},{"t":"Impulse and momentum","d":"Conservation laws in mechanics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conservacion-mecanica#cantidad","x":"04 Impulse and momentum \\[ \\vec{p} = m \\vec{v}, \\quad \\vec{J} = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\vec{F} d t = \\Delta \\vec{p} \\] The impulse is the area under the force–time curve, and it equals the change in momentum. This is why an airbag works: the change in momentum in a crash is fixed, but if the same Δp is spread over ten times as much time, the peak force is divided by ten. When it is conserved If the sum of external forces is zero, the total \\(\\vec{p}\\) does not change. During a collision, the internal forces are enormous compared with the weight or friction, so momentum is conserved to an excellent approximation even if the system is not truly isolated."},{"t":"Collisions","d":"Conservation laws in mechanics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conservacion-mecanica#choques","x":"05 Collisions Type Coefficient e Momentum Kinetic energy Elastic \\( e = 1 \\) Conserved Conserved Partially inelastic 0 < e < 1 Conserved Partly lost Perfectly inelastic \\( e = 0 \\) Conserved Maximum loss: the bodies stick together The coefficient of restitution compares the speed of separation with the speed of approach, \\(e = |v_2' - v_1'| / |v_2 - v_1|\\). With it, the final velocities of a head-on collision are determined: \\[ v_1' = \\frac{m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2 + m_2 e (v_2 -v_1)}{m_1 + m_2} \\] Lab · head-on collision between two bodies Move with the time slider: before the collision on the left, after it on the right. Watch what happens to the momentum and to the energy. Mass 1 2 kg Velocity 1 4 m/s Mass 2 3 kg Velocity 2 −2 m/s Coefficient of restitution 1.00 Time −1.0 s"},{"t":"The same thing, in electronics","d":"Conservation laws in mechanics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conservacion-mecanica#electronica","x":"06 The same thing, in electronics Energy in a capacitor and in an inductor. \\( frac{1}{2} \\cdot C \\cdot V^2\\) and \\( frac{1}{2} \\cdot L \\cdot I^2\\) are the exact twins of \\( frac{1}{2} \\cdot k \\cdot x^2\\) and \\( frac{1}{2} \\cdot m \\cdot v^2\\). An LC circuit oscillates by exchanging energy just like a pendulum. Conservation of charge. Kirchhoff’s current law is a conservation law, with the same logic as that of momentum. Impedance matching. The maximum power transfer from a source to a load has its exact mechanical analog in the elastic collision between equal masses, where one transfers all its energy to the other. Energy harvesting. A piezoelectric generator converts the work of a deformation into electrical energy; the balance is set up with the work–energy theorem."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Conservation laws in mechanics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conservacion-mecanica#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Coefficient of restitution from a bounce Drop a ball from \\(h_0\\) and measure the height of the first bounce \\(h_1\\). Then \\(e = \\sqrt{h_1/h_0}\\). Repeat with balls of different materials and tabulate the results. Activity 2 · Collisions on an air track With two carts and optical gates, measure the four velocities of a collision. Verify that \\(m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2\\) is conserved within the error, and calculate what fraction of the energy was lost. Activity 3 · Measuring impulse with a load cell Use a microcontroller to record force against time during a brief impact. The area under the curve is the impulse; compare it with \\(m \\cdot \\Delta v\\) measured from video."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Conservation laws in mechanics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conservacion-mecanica#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Assuming kinetic energy is conserved in every collision. Only in the elastic one. Adding momenta as scalars. It is a vector: in a head-on collision, one of the two velocities enters with a negative sign. Using \\(E_p = m \\cdot g \\cdot h\\) with friction present without subtracting the dissipated heat. Confusing work with force. Holding a suitcase still does no work, however tiring it is. Defining a potential energy for a non-conservative force. It does not exist. Forgetting to choose a reference level for the height. What makes physical sense is the difference in potential energy, not its absolute value."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Conservation laws in mechanics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conservacion-mecanica#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment A 2 kg body falls 5 m. How fast is it moving when it reaches the ground? \\(m \\cdot g \\cdot h = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot m \\cdot v^2\\) gives \\(v = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 9.8 \\cdot 5} \\approx\\) \\(9.9\\text{ m/s}\\), regardless of the mass. Two identical bodies, one at 5 m/s and the other at rest, collide elastically. What happens? They exchange velocities: the first comes to rest and the second leaves at 5 m/s. It is the case of maximum energy transfer. How much energy is lost in a perfectly inelastic collision between 2 kg at 4 m/s and 3 kg at rest? \\(v' = 8/5 = 1.6\\text{ m/s}\\). \\(E_{\\text{before}} = 16\\text{ J}\\), \\(E_{\\text{after}} = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 5 \\cdot 1.6^2 = 6.4\\text{ J}\\): 9.6 J is lost, 60%. Why does an airbag reduce injuries if Δp is the same? Because \\(J = F \\cdot \\Delta t\\): by lengthening the time of impact, the peak force drops in the same proportion. Does the normal force do work on a body sliding down an inclined plane? No: it is perpendicular to the displacement, so its work is zero. What is the electrical analog of \\(\\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot m \\cdot v^2\\)? The energy of an inductor, \\(\\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot L \\cdot I^2\\), with inductance playing the role of mass and current the role of velocity."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Conservation laws in mechanics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conservacion-mecanica#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics, Volume 1. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage Learning, 2014. Chapters 7 to 9: work, energy, momentum and collisions. Paul A. Tipler and Gene Mosca. Physics for Scientists and Engineers, Volume 1. 6th ed., W. H. Freeman, 2008 (Spanish edition, “Física para la ciencia y la tecnología”, Reverté, 2010). Good treatment of the coefficient of restitution and of collisions in two dimensions. Richard P. Feynman. The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I. Addison-Wesley, 1963 (Spanish edition, “Física, volumen I”, Addison-Wesley / Pearson). Chapter 4, “Conservation of Energy”, explains what it really means for something to be conserved. Marcelo Alonso and Edward J. Finn. Physics. Addison-Wesley, 1992 (Spanish edition, “Física”, Addison-Wesley Iberoamericana, 1995). Develops mechanics starting from the conservation laws instead of Newton’s laws."},{"t":"The supreme law","d":"The National Constitution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/constitucion-nacional#historia","x":"01 The supreme law The Constitución Nacional (National Constitution) is the rule that organizes the State and sets out the rights of the inhabitants. It was enacted in 1853 and amended in 1860, 1866, 1898, 1957 and 1994. The 1994 reform was the most extensive: it added the rights to a healthy environment and of consumers, the amparo action (a fast-track judicial remedy), the Council of the Judiciary (Consejo de la Magistratura) and constitutional rank for a series of human rights treaties. Its central feature is supremacy (Article 31): the Constitution, the laws of Congress made in pursuance of it, and the treaties are the supreme law of the Nation, and the provinces are bound to respect it even if their own rules say otherwise. Any rule that contradicts it is invalid."},{"t":"How it organizes the State","d":"The National Constitution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/constitucion-nacional#organizacion","x":"02 How it organizes the State Article 1 adopts the representative, republican and federal form. Each word has consequences: Feature What it means Representative The people govern through their elected representatives Republican Separation of powers, fixed terms of office, publicity of government acts, accountability of officials Federal The provinces retain all the power they did not delegate to the Nation (Article 121), enact their own constitution and elect their own authorities The three branches are the Legislative, a two-chamber Congress that makes the laws; the Executive, headed by the president, who carries them out and issues regulations implementing them; and the Judicial, with the Supreme Court at its head, which resolves disputes. Why the professional license is provincial Congress enacts the substantive codes —civil and commercial, criminal, labor— for the whole country (Article 75, clause 12). But the oversight of the practice of professions, the so-called police power, stayed in the hands of the provinces. That is why each province has its own law on the practice of engineering and its own council or association, and an engineer has to register where they work, as explained in the unit on professional practice."},{"t":"The rights that affect the profession","d":"The National Constitution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/constitucion-nacional#derechos","x":"03 The rights that affect the profession Article What it guarantees For an engineer 14 To work and practice any lawful industry, to trade, to associate for useful purposes, to teach and to learn The freedom to practice the profession and to found a company 14 bis Dignified working conditions, limited working hours, paid rest and vacations, fair remuneration, protection against arbitrary dismissal, free union organization, collective bargaining agreements The basis of all labor law 16 Equality before the law; access to public employment with no requirement other than suitability Public competitive selection, non-discrimination 17 Inviolable property; expropriation only for public utility declared by law and previously compensated; every author or inventor is the exclusive owner of their work or invention for the term set by law Patents, rights over software and designs 18 Due process, prior trial based on a law that predates the act, the right to a defense in court Guarantees if there are criminal proceedings over an accident 19 No one is obliged to do what the law does not command or deprived of what it does not prohibit The principle of freedom that governs private activity 41 A healthy environment and the duty to preserve it; whoever damages the environment must restore it Electronic waste management, environmental impact of a construction project 42 Protection of consumers and users: health, safety, adequate information Obligations of those who manufacture or sell equipmen"},{"t":"Treaties and judicial review","d":"The National Constitution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/constitucion-nacional#tratados","x":"04 Treaties and judicial review The 1994 reform gave constitutional rank to a list of human rights treaties (Article 75, clause 22), among them the American Convention on Human Rights and the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights. Other treaties rank above statutes but below the Constitution. In Argentina judicial review (control de constitucionalidad) is diffuse: any judge, when deciding a specific case, may declare a rule unconstitutional and decline to apply it. The declaration is valid only for that case and does not repeal the rule; but when the Supreme Court does it, the other courts usually follow."},{"t":"The guarantees","d":"The National Constitution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/constitucion-nacional#garantias","x":"05 The guarantees Rights need tools to defend them. Article 43 establishes three: Amparo: a fast action against an act of an authority or of a private party that infringes, in a manifestly arbitrary or unlawful way, a recognized right, when there is no more suitable judicial remedy. Habeas data: allows you to find out what personal data appear in records or databases and to demand their correction or deletion. It is the basis of Law 25,326 on personal data protection, which applies to any system that stores data about individuals. Habeas corpus: protects physical liberty against unlawful detention."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"The National Constitution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/constitucion-nacional#electronica","x":"06 Where it shows up in electronics Situation The constitutional rule behind it Patenting a circuit Article 17: the inventor’s property, regulated by the patent law, Law 24,481 Designing a system that stores customer data Article 43, habeas data, and Law 25,326 Equipment recycling plan Article 41, healthy environment Labeling and manual of a product Article 42, adequate information for the consumer Registering to work in another province Articles 121 and 14: local power over professions and the right to work"},{"t":"Activities","d":"The National Constitution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/constitucion-nacional#laboratorio","x":"07 Activities Activity 1 · Read the dogmatic part Read Articles 14 to 20 of the Constitution and mark which rights relate to the professional activity of an engineer. Activity 2 · A regulated right Choose a right from Article 14 and find a law that regulates it. Discuss whether the regulation respects the limit in Article 28. Activity 3 · Habeas data in a project For a card-based access control system, list what personal data it stores and what rights the registered people have under Law 25,326."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The National Constitution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/constitucion-nacional#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Believing that constitutional rights are absolute. They are exercised in accordance with the laws that regulate them. Assuming that a national license is valid across the whole country. Professional practice is regulated by each province. Thinking that a judge who declares a law unconstitutional repeals it. The judge merely stops applying it in that case. Forgetting habeas data when designing systems. Personal data have constitutional protection."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The National Constitution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/constitucion-nacional#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What does Article 31 establish? The supremacy of the Constitution, the national laws made in pursuance of it, and the treaties over provincial rules. Which article protects an inventor’s property? Article 17. Why does each province have its own law on the practice of engineering? Because the police power over professions was not delegated to the Nation and is retained by the provinces (Article 121). Which guarantee allows you to request the correction of personal data in a database? Habeas data, under Article 43."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The National Constitution","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/constitucion-nacional#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Germán J. Bidart Campos. Manual de la Constitución reformada. Ediar (in Spanish). The classic textbook, written after the 1994 reform, clear for readers who are not lawyers. María Angélica Gelli. Constitución de la Nación Argentina comentada y concordada. La Ley (in Spanish). Each article with its interpretation and the Supreme Court rulings that applied it. Constitución de la Nación Argentina. Texto de 1994. Official text in InfoLEG (in Spanish). Short and readable: reading all of it takes less than two hours."},{"t":"The four stages","d":"Software build tools","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/construccion-software#etapas","x":"01 The four stages Typing gcc main.c -o program looks like a single step, but it is four programs chained together. Knowing which one failed is half of debugging: Stage Input Output Typical error Preprocessor.c.i Cannot find a header Compiler.i.s (assembly) Syntax or type error Assembler.s.o (object) Rarely fails Linker.o and libraries Executable Undefined reference How to tell a compile error from a link error A compile error mentions a file and line: the compiler is reading your code. A link error says undefined reference to 'something' and points to no line: the code compiled fine, but the body of a function is missing. It is almost always a library that was not added —the classic -lm for the math functions— or a file that was left out of the build. Lab · what each stage does Walk through the four stages and see what the file turns into, along with the command that runs each one. Stage 1 of 4"},{"t":"Separate compilation and libraries","d":"Software build tools","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/construccion-software#modulos","x":"02 Separate compilation and libraries Each.c is compiled separately into its own.o, and the linker joins them. The advantage is concrete: if you touch a single file, only that one is recompiled. gcc -c main.c → main.o gcc -c sensor.c → sensor.o gcc main.o sensor.o -o program -lm Library type File How it is used Static.a on Linux,.lib on Windows Copied into the executable at link time Dynamic.so on Linux,.dll on Windows Loaded at run time; shared between programs A static library gives a self-contained, larger executable; a dynamic one, a small executable that depends on the library being installed. For an embedded device the static one is almost always the better choice: fewer surprises in the field."},{"t":"make","d":"Software build tools","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/construccion-software#make","x":"03 make Once there are more than three files, recompiling by hand is impractical. make reads a file of rules and rebuilds only what is out of date, by comparing modification times: CC = gcc CFLAGS = -Wall -Wextra -O2 program: main.o sensor.o $(CC) main.o sensor.o -o program -lm main.o: main.c sensor.h $(CC) $(CFLAGS) -c main.c clean: rm -f *.o program Each rule has a target, its dependencies and the commands to build it, which are indented with a real tab character. If sensor.h changes, make knows that main.o is stale and rebuilds it; if nothing changed, it does nothing. Flags worth always using -Wall -Wextra turn on warnings, and you have to read them: most run-time errors were first an ignored warning. -g adds information for the debugger, -O2 optimizes, and -std=c11 fixes the language version so that the program behaves the same on another machine."},{"t":"Version control","d":"Software build tools","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/construccion-software#git","x":"04 Version control A version control system keeps the history of the project: what changed, when and why. With git, the basic cycle is short: Command What it does git init Creates the repository git status What changed since the last commit git add file Marks what will go into the next commit git commit -m \"…\" Saves a version with its explanation git log The history git diff The exact differences, line by line git checkout -b branch Opens a parallel line of work Three rules of thumb that matter more than the list of commands: small commits that do one thing only, messages that explain the why and not the what —the diff already says what changed—, and a.gitignore that leaves out whatever is generated: the.o files, the executable and temporary files are not versioned."},{"t":"Debugging","d":"Software build tools","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/construccion-software#depurar","x":"05 Debugging printf. Rudimentary but effective, especially in embedded systems where there is no debugger. gdb. Compiling with -g: break to set a breakpoint, run, next and step to advance, print to see a variable and backtrace to find out how execution got there. With a program that crashed, backtrace on the core dump often solves the case in a minute. valgrind. Detects out-of-bounds accesses, use of uninitialized memory and memory leaks. A program that seems to run fine can be full of errors that valgrind reveals. Sanitizers. -fsanitize=address instruments the program so that it aborts at the exact moment of the invalid access, with the line pointed out. The error that cannot be reproduced A program that fails “sometimes” almost always has misused memory: an overflowed array or a dangling pointer. The symptom appears far from the cause, which is why analysis tools are worth more than hours of reading the code."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Software build tools","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/construccion-software#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Exercise 1 · Seeing each stage With a ten-line program, run gcc -E, -S and -c and look at the intermediate files. Count how many lines the.i has after including stdio.h. Exercise 2 · A Makefile of your own Set up a three-file project with its Makefile, with targets all, clean and debug. Check that when you touch a single.c only that one is recompiled. Exercise 3 · Causing and reading errors Deliberately produce one error from each stage: a nonexistent #include, a missing semicolon and a function that is declared but not defined. Write down exactly what each message says and learn to recognize them."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Software build tools","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/construccion-software#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Confusing a compile error with a link error. Forgetting -lm when using math functions. Indenting the commands of a Makefile with spaces instead of a tab. Ignoring the compiler’s warnings. Versioning generated files —objects, executables, build folders— and cluttering the history. Debugging a binary compiled without -g and not even seeing variable names."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Software build tools","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/construccion-software#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What exactly does the preprocessor do? It manipulates text: it pastes in the included files, replaces macros and resolves conditional compilation. It does not understand C. undefined reference to 'sqrt' appears. Which stage failed? The linking. You need to add -lm: the prototype was in math.h, but the body of the function is in the math library. What are the #ifndef guards in a header for? To keep the contents from being included twice in the same translation unit, which would cause duplicate definitions. How does make know what to recompile? By comparing modification times: if a dependency is newer than the target, it rebuilds that target. What is the difference between a static and a dynamic library? A static library is copied into the executable at link time; a dynamic one is loaded at run time and shared between programs. What should go in a commit message? The why of the change. The diff already shows the what."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Software build tools","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/construccion-software#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Brian W. Kernighan and Rob Pike. The Practice of Programming. Addison-Wesley, 1999. Debugging, testing and tools, with the judgment of two authors who worked on Unix. Scott Chacon and Ben Straub. Pro Git. 2nd ed., Apress, 2014. Available online and in Spanish. The git book, from the basics to the team workflow. John Graham-Cumming. The GNU Make Book. No Starch Press, 2015. For when the Makefile grows and starts to have logic of its own. Randal E. Bryant and David R. O'Hallaron. Computer Systems: A Programmer's Perspective. 3rd ed., Pearson, 2015. Chapter 7 explains linking in a level of detail found nowhere else."},{"t":"What a contract is","d":"Contracts","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/contratos#que-es","x":"01 What a contract is Article 957 of the Civil and Commercial Code defines a contract as the legal act by which two or more parties express their consent to create, regulate, modify, transfer or extinguish property-related legal relationships. Three principles govern it: Freedom of contract (Article 958): the parties decide whether to contract, with whom and with what content, within the limits of the law, public order and morality. Binding effect (Article 959): a valid contract binds the parties as the law itself does, and can only be modified or terminated by agreement or in the cases the law provides for. Good faith (Article 961): it binds not only to what is written, but to everything that a careful and prudent contracting party would reasonably have assumed."},{"t":"Elements and formation","d":"Contracts","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/contratos#elementos","x":"02 Elements and formation Element What it requires Consent An offer addressed to a specific person, with the intention of being bound and the necessary particulars, and full acceptance of that offer Object Lawful, possible, determined or determinable, and capable of economic valuation Cause The purpose that led the parties to contract, which must exist and be lawful Form Free, unless the law requires a particular one (Article 1015) The practical consequence of freedom of form is the one that surprises many people: a quotation sent by email and accepted by reply is already a contract, with a price, a deadline and a scope. An acceptance with modifications, on the other hand, does not close the contract: it is a counteroffer. The difficulty with a verbal contract is not that it is valid, but proving what was agreed."},{"t":"When the contract does not say everything","d":"Contracts","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/contratos#interpretacion","x":"03 When the contract does not say everything No contract provides for everything. When a dispute arises, the judge looks for the parties’ common intention (Article 1061), reading the words with their technical meaning if they belong to a profession, the parties’ conduct after signing and the context. What the contract does not regulate is filled in by the rules for that type of contract and by local usage. Adhesion contracts When one party drafts everything and the other can only accept or refuse —the terms of a service, a set of general conditions—, the contract is one of adhesion. Ambiguous clauses are interpreted against whoever drafted them (Article 987), and abusive ones are deemed unwritten. If something extraordinary and unforeseeable happens after signing that makes performance excessively burdensome —a sudden devaluation that multiplies the cost of imported components—, the affected party may ask for the contract to be adjusted or terminated on grounds of unforeseeable circumstances (imprevisión; Article 1091)."},{"t":"Work contracts and service contracts","d":"Contracts","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/contratos#obra-servicios","x":"04 Work contracts and service contracts These are the contracts an engineer signs most often, and the Code regulates them together (Articles 1251 to 1279), with a distinction that has consequences: Work contract (contrato de obra) Service contract (contrato de servicios) What is promised A reproducible result, or one capable of being delivered A diligent activity, without guaranteeing the result Example Installing and commissioning an alarm system Advising on the best communications technology When it is fulfilled When the result is delivered in proper condition When the service was provided with due diligence The contractor is obliged to carry out the work as agreed and according to the rules of the craft (accepted professional standards), and to inform the client (comitente) about the essential aspects (Article 1256). The price may be set as a lump sum (ajuste alzado; a total amount), by unit price (so much per meter of cable, so much per network point) or on a cost-plus basis (coste y costas; the actual cost plus a percentage). On receiving the work, the client who accepts it without reservations loses the right to claim for defects that were apparent; hidden ones can be claimed later. For long-lasting works, the builder is liable for structural ruin (ruina) —defects that compromise the work’s soundness or make it unfit for its purpose— if the damage occurs within ten years of acceptance (Articles 1273 to 1275). Liability also extends to the designer and to the constructio"}]);
